records[587]:
  - id: "project:project-postcraft:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's PostCraft project?
    text: "Montasim built PostCraft, a website project. A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning AI workflow, publishing integrations, and background jobs. One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "project:project-postcraft:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for PostCraft?
    text: "Montasim built PostCraft with Next.js, React, TypeScript, MongoDB, Mongoose, Redux Toolkit, Better Auth, OpenRouter, Gemini API, Groq API, ZhipuAI, Inngest, REST APIs, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Zod, Resend, System Design. The product goal is clear: A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: The Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,"
  - id: "project:project-bugreceipt:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's BugReceipt project?
    text: "Montasim built BugReceipt, an extension project. Local-first Chrome extension for capturing privacy-filtered console, network, reproduction-step, and visual evidence in a reviewable bug report. His role covered product designer and browser-extension engineer, with scope spanning capture lifecycle, privacy boundaries, review workflows, and release delivery. Reporters can preserve reproduction steps, bounded console and network evidence, environment details, and a recording or screenshot in one workflow. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-bugreceipt","case-study:bugreceipt:problem","case-study:bugreceipt:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/bugreceipt/dcjbnkadoenmkcimidcbhhckdpaondae\nLocal-first Chrome extension for capturing privacy-filtered console, network, reproduction-step, and visual evidence in a reviewable bug","Screenshots and vague descriptions rarely preserve the sequence, browser evidence, environment, and visual result that developers need to reproduce a web application failure. Collecting that evidence by hand is slow, and collecting it automatically risks capturing secrets.\nBrowser diagnostics and screen recordings can expose sensitive information, while local filtering cannot guarantee that every secret will be recognized.\nChrome permission, selected-tab, service-worker, navigation, and storage boundaries must","Reporters can preserve reproduction steps, bounded console and network evidence, environment details, and a recording or screenshot in one workflow.\nEvery captured item remains reviewable and removable before it leaves extension-owned storage.\nUsers can export a local Markdown-and-visual bundle or deliberately send a size-bounded report through the configured endpoint.\nMaintainers can exercise known failures and ship verifiable unpacked Chrome archives with published checksums."
  - id: "project:project-bugreceipt:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for BugReceipt?
    text: "Montasim built BugReceipt with WXT, React 19, TypeScript, Chrome Manifest V3, Chrome Extensions API, Tailwind CSS, TanStack Start, Zod, JSZip, Resend, Vitest, React Testing Library, Netlify, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: Local-first Chrome extension for capturing privacy-filtered console, network, reproduction-step, and visual evidence in a reviewable bug report. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A WXT Manifest V3 extension combines a side panel, a selected-tab recorder, a review page, and a background-owned capture lifecycle, with isolated page instrumentation feeding typed capture contracts. Evidence is filtered and rendered locally, stored in extension storage and IndexedDB, and leaves the browser only through a local export or an explicit Resend delivery through a TanStack Start endpoint. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-bugreceipt","case-study:bugreceipt:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/bugreceipt/dcjbnkadoenmkcimidcbhhckdpaondae\nLocal-first Chrome extension for capturing privacy-filtered console, network, reproduction-step, and visual evidence in a reviewable bug","A persistent React side panel, a selected-tab recorder, manual steps, and an editable review page make up the user-facing workflow.\nCapture interface\nThe background script owns the capture lifecycle and exchanges Zod-validated messages with the rest of the extension.\nSession domain\nBounded local filtering runs before storage, with extension storage holding session data and IndexedDB holding visual evidence.\nPrivacy and storage\nMarkdown and visual report bundles stay local unless the user explicitly invokes the"
  - id: "project:project-mulalens:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's MulaLens - Workplace Context Extension project?
    text: "Montasim built MulaLens - Workplace Context Extension, an extension project. Published Chrome Web Store extension that adds identity, workplace, salary, job, story, and cited-research context to Deshi Mula company pages. His role covered product designer and browser-extension engineer, with scope spanning browser integration, hosted API client, privacy, and store release. Users can open workplace context without leaving the company page. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-mulalens","case-study:mulalens:problem","case-study:mulalens:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/MulaLens/fchnnoakpkkefkpbcliooalddncffedo\nPublished Chrome Web Store extension that adds identity, workplace, salary, job, story, and cited-research context to Deshi Mula company","Deshi Mula company pages surface community conversation, but job seekers deciding whether to apply or accept an offer need more structure. Identity, salary, workplace, story, and cited-research context are scattered elsewhere, so they are missing at the moment of decision.\nSalary and workplace reports may be community-submitted and require independent verification.\nThe extension depends on the hosted b4join API and cannot operate as a complete offline research system.\nPage matching and injection must remain narrow","Users can open workplace context without leaving the company page.\nUsers can inspect salary and narrative signals alongside their source links.\nAnyone can install MulaLens directly from its published Chrome Web Store listing.\nThe panel makes clear that its evidence is advisory and depends on the hosted API."
  - id: "project:project-mulalens:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for MulaLens - Workplace Context Extension?
    text: "Montasim built MulaLens - Workplace Context Extension with TypeScript, Chrome Manifest V3, Chrome Extensions API, esbuild, TanStack Start, React, Tailwind CSS, Vitest, Netlify, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git. The product goal is clear: Published Chrome Web Store extension that adds identity, workplace, salary, job, story, and cited-research context to Deshi Mula company pages. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A content script detects Deshi Mula company pages and injects a research panel that calls hosted b4join endpoints through a typed thin client. A release workflow builds the Manifest V3 package for the Chrome Web Store, and a public documentation site explains the product and its privacy terms. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-mulalens","case-study:mulalens:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/MulaLens/fchnnoakpkkefkpbcliooalddncffedo\nPublished Chrome Web Store extension that adds identity, workplace, salary, job, story, and cited-research context to Deshi Mula company","A content script detects Deshi Mula company pages and activates only on them.\nPage integration\nAn injected research panel shows identity, workplace, salary, job, story, and cited-research sections.\nExtension interface\nA typed thin client calls the hosted b4join endpoints and surfaces loading and error states.\nProvider boundary\nA release workflow builds the Manifest V3 package published on the Chrome Web Store, alongside a public documentation site.\nStorage and delivery\nA content script detects Deshi Mula company"
  - id: "project:project-formflow:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's FormFlow project?
    text: "Montasim built FormFlow, a website project. Bilingual, role-aware platform for institution-owned forms, versioned submission workflows, employee assignments, requester-visible progress, notifications, and audit history. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning multi-tenant authorization, configurable workflows, and bilingual service delivery. Institutions can define bilingual forms and operational workflows without changing application source code. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-formflow","case-study:formflow:problem","case-study:formflow:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Bilingual, role-aware platform for institution-owned forms, versioned submission workflows, employee assignments, requester-visible progress, notifications, and audit history.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-29T06:42:01Z\n2ef417ae6d940253a1ff9130792af4ec43a04c15\n2026-08-29T08:16:40Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/FormFlow\nproject-formflow\nassets/projects/formflow.png\nhttps://formflown.netlify.app/en/\n\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nBetter Auth\nGoogle OAuth\nPostgreSQL\nNeon\nDrizzle","Institutions often manage operational requests across chat, spreadsheets, fixed forms, and verbal follow-up, separating submitted information from ownership, current status, and accountability.\nEnglish and Bengali must be complete interfaces, with locale-aware content and Bengali or Latin numeral handling where appropriate.\nGoogle identity, platform authority, and institution membership are separate security boundaries, so every protected read and mutation must be authorized on the server.\nHistorical submissions","Institutions can define bilingual forms and operational workflows without changing application source code.\nRequesters can submit a published form, receive a tracking number, and follow requester-visible progress from one record.\nAdmins and delegated Managers can review queues, assign Employees, manage people, and inspect accountable history within their institution.\nEmployees can accept assigned work, record progress and evidence, and complete or return the assignment through explicit workflow states.\nPublished"
  - id: "project:project-formflow:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for FormFlow?
    text: "Montasim built FormFlow with TanStack Start, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Better Auth, Google OAuth, PostgreSQL, Neon, Drizzle ORM, Resend, Zod, Hugeicons, Netlify, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: Bilingual, role-aware platform for institution-owned forms, versioned submission workflows, employee assignments, requester-visible progress, notifications, and audit history. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: TanStack Start routes and server functions deliver role-aware React workspaces, while framework-independent domain modules own forms, workflows, status transitions, and authorization. Drizzle repositories scope Neon PostgreSQL records by institution and actor, and Better Auth, Google OAuth, Resend, Netlify Functions, and GitHub Actions form the identity, communication, deployment, and quality boundaries. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-formflow","case-study:formflow:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Bilingual, role-aware platform for institution-owned forms, versioned submission workflows, employee assignments, requester-visible progress, notifications, and audit history.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-29T06:42:01Z\n2ef417ae6d940253a1ff9130792af4ec43a04c15\n2026-08-29T08:16:40Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/FormFlow\nproject-formflow\nassets/projects/formflow.png\nhttps://formflown.netlify.app/en/\n\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nBetter Auth\nGoogle OAuth\nPostgreSQL\nNeon\nDrizzle","Locale-aware TanStack Start routes and React workspaces serve platform administrators, institution managers, requesters, and employees.\nRole-aware interface\nFramework-independent modules own form validation, workflow versioning, ordering, permissions, assignments, and status transitions.\nDomain rules\nDrizzle repositories over Neon PostgreSQL store immutable form and workflow versions, notifications, work logs, and audit events.\nInstitution-scoped data\nBetter Auth with Google OAuth handles sign-in, Resend sends"
  - id: "project:project-b4joinacompany:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's b4joinacompany project?
    text: "Montasim built b4joinacompany, a website project. An evidence-labeled company research tool that helps job seekers prepare before they apply, interview or accept an offer. His role covered product design, evidence modeling, and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning decision support, cited AI, private research, and versioned data. Visitors can research a company without creating an account and open original evidence for important claims. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-b4joinacompany","case-study:b4joinacompany:problem","case-study:b4joinacompany:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "An evidence-labeled company research tool that helps job seekers prepare before they apply, interview or accept an offer.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-26T04:31:13Z\n9849284bf1ac0b399c7b9d9f59bbbb9b36ba9629\n2026-07-26T04:30:07Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/b4joinacompany\nproject-b4joinacompany\nassets/projects/b4joinacompany.png\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app/icon.svg?icon.10kda_g1712l_.svg\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nGemini API\nGroq API\nBetter Auth\nREST","Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment.\nPersonal workplace reports provide context, not verified statements of current company policy.\nCompany names and salary records can be ambiguous, so fuzzy matching cannot silently publish a result.\nAI answers must remain bounded by","Visitors can research a company without creating an account and open original evidence for important claims.\nPrepared questions are generated by fixed rules, while Ask provides cited synthesis or an explicit evidence gap.\nSaved checkpoints retain the exact evidence version and append revisions on successful updates.\nThe product supports comparison without producing a synthetic company score or declaring a winner."
  - id: "project:project-b4joinacompany:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for b4joinacompany?
    text: "Montasim built b4joinacompany with Next.js, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, MongoDB, Gemini API, Groq API, Better Auth, REST APIs, Zod, Vitest, System Design. The product goal is clear: An evidence-labeled company research tool that helps job seekers prepare before they apply, interview or accept an offer. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Versioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while private checkpoints live separately in MongoDB. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-b4joinacompany","case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "An evidence-labeled company research tool that helps job seekers prepare before they apply, interview or accept an offer.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-26T04:31:13Z\n9849284bf1ac0b399c7b9d9f59bbbb9b36ba9629\n2026-07-26T04:30:07Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/b4joinacompany\nproject-b4joinacompany\nassets/projects/b4joinacompany.png\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app/icon.svg?icon.10kda_g1712l_.svg\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nGemini API\nGroq API\nBetter Auth\nREST","Versioned records hold company, story, comment, and salary evidence.\nSource releases\nDeterministic rules handle identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking.\nResearch layer\nNext.js serves research briefs, compare, and Ask, alongside a versioned REST API.\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB store private checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions.\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while"
  - id: "project:project-devtools:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's DevTools project?
    text: "Montasim built DevTools, a tool project. A suite of 50+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating and transforming data, where local tools keep your input in the browser by default. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning developer utilities, shared interaction patterns, and account-backed tools. More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-devtools","case-study:devtools:problem","case-study:devtools:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A suite of 50+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating and transforming data, where local tools keep your input in the browser by default.\n\ntrue\n2026-04-08T05:59:03Z\n88e28b53c9c618f016edbaada4918442f070d25e\n2026-04-12T09:19:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/devtools\nproject-devtools\nassets/projects/devtools.png\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app/icon.svg?icon.0qz0rw08sizuj.svg\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nCodeMirror 6\nTanStack Query\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nBetter Auth\nTailwind","Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse.\nMany transformations should stay in the browser, while network diagnostics necessarily contact a target or server endpoint.\nA catalog with more than 50 workflows must remain quick to search, navigate, and extend without duplicating page infrastructure.\nSaved items, shares, webhooks, and short links introduce","More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model.\nCommon transformations can run without an account and without sending their current input to the server.\nServer-backed features add reuse and sharing without obscuring when data leaves the browser.\nThe architecture supports both small local utilities and stateful workflows without forcing them through one execution path."
  - id: "project:project-devtools:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for DevTools?
    text: "Montasim built DevTools with Next.js, React, TypeScript, CodeMirror 6, TanStack Query, PostgreSQL, Prisma, Better Auth, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Zod, Vitest, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: A suite of 50+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating and transforming data, where local tools keep your input in the browser by default. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool features own their tabs and logic, shared app infrastructure handles auth and storage, and route handlers isolate network and persistence work. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-devtools","case-study:devtools:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A suite of 50+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating and transforming data, where local tools keep your input in the browser by default.\n\ntrue\n2026-04-08T05:59:03Z\n88e28b53c9c618f016edbaada4918442f070d25e\n2026-04-12T09:19:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/devtools\nproject-devtools\nassets/projects/devtools.png\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app/icon.svg?icon.0qz0rw08sizuj.svg\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nCodeMirror 6\nTanStack Query\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nBetter Auth\nTailwind","A navigation registry defines the searchable routes, labels, and keyboard discovery for every tool.\nTool registry\nFocused modules implement the formatters, generators, testers, inspectors, and references.\nFeature modules\nLocal transforms, preferences, input history, and reusable state stay in the browser.\nBrowser boundary\nNext.js APIs, Better Auth, PostgreSQL, Prisma, and target services handle the work that has to leave the browser.\nServer boundary\nA navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool"
  - id: "project:project-skillfoliox:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog project?
    text: "Montasim built Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog, a skill project. A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents. His role covered product designer and frontend engineer, with scope spanning catalog design, content model, documentation rendering, and deployment. Visitors can browse and compare skills in one place. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-skillfoliox","case-study:skillfoliox:problem","case-study:skillfoliox:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-30T12:47:03Z\nb31bdbbbdf5f762ddfced47299833244b4399739\n2026-08-30T12:47:21Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/skills\nproject-skillfoliox\nassets/projects/skillfoliox.png\nhttps://skillfoliox.netlify.app\nhttps://skillfoliox.netlify.app/favicon.svg\n\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTypeScript\nVite\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nReact Markdown\nVitest\nNetlify\nReact Testing Library\nSystem","Reusable SKILL.md capabilities are scattered across repositories. Developers have no consistent way to compare them by purpose, host compatibility, maturity, or install path, so choosing a skill means reading many repositories by hand.\nCatalog facts can drift from the source repositories and package releases they describe.\nFetching README files from GitHub can fail or be rate-limited.\nMarkdown from many authors must render safely and consistently without implying that every skill has identical support.","Visitors can browse and compare skills in one place.\nVisitors can open rendered documentation and each skill's canonical repository.\nEach entry shows its compatibility, version, status, and categories.\nMaintainers can add catalog entries through a documented, testable workflow."
  - id: "project:project-skillfoliox:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog?
    text: "Montasim built Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog with TanStack Start, React, TypeScript, Vite, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, React Markdown, Vitest, Netlify, React Testing Library, System Design. The product goal is clear: A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: TanStack Start routes and a React interface present a typed local catalog with search and filters. Detail pages fetch and render each skill's README from GitHub with retry and source fallbacks, and build-time metadata, a sitemap, and Netlify deployment complete the delivery path. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-skillfoliox","case-study:skillfoliox:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A curated portfolio of reusable SKILL.md capabilities for Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Cursor, and other AI coding agents.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-30T12:47:03Z\nb31bdbbbdf5f762ddfced47299833244b4399739\n2026-08-30T12:47:21Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/skills\nproject-skillfoliox\nassets/projects/skillfoliox.png\nhttps://skillfoliox.netlify.app\nhttps://skillfoliox.netlify.app/favicon.svg\n\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTypeScript\nVite\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nReact Markdown\nVitest\nNetlify\nReact Testing Library\nSystem","TanStack Start routes and React components render the catalog, cards, summaries, and detail pages.\nCatalog interface\nA typed local catalog drives search and filtering by status, compatibility, version, and category.\nCatalog model\nEach detail page fetches and renders the skill's README from GitHub, retrying and falling back to the source link when a fetch fails.\nDocumentation pipeline\nBuild-time metadata, a generated sitemap, and Netlify configuration publish the site.\nDistribution\nTanStack Start routes and a React"
  - id: "project:project-bangladesh-location-registry:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Bangladesh Location Registry project?
    text: "Montasim built Bangladesh Location Registry, a dataset project. Versioned, provenance-rich Bangladesh location registry with census, postal, facility, ward, and infrastructure data from government-published sources. His role covered data modeling, validation, and release engineering, with scope spanning provenance, transformation, validation, and distribution. Consumers can download structured location data as JSON, streaming JSONL, or SQLite. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-bangladesh-location-registry","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:problem","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Versioned, provenance-rich Bangladesh location registry with census, postal, facility, ward, and infrastructure data from government-published sources.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-04T09:46:31Z\n06438720f285a7904f4778af3d30ae827cfdcc8d\n2026-08-04T09:46:58Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/bangladesh-location-registry\nproject-bangladesh-location-registry\nassets/projects/bangladesh-location-registry.png\nhttps://address-bd.netlify.app\n\nJSON\nJSONL\nSQLite\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nGitHub Actions\nCI/CD\nBangladesh Location","Bangladesh location records are spread across government sources that each use a different structure. That makes normalized lookup, provenance review, and repeatable machine consumption difficult for anyone building on the data.\nAdministrative boundaries, names, and facilities change over time.\nSource coverage and confidence vary, and neither establishes authoritative identity or address truth.\nHealth, emergency, eligibility, routing, or legal use requires confirmation from a current authoritative source.","Consumers can download structured location data as JSON, streaming JSONL, or SQLite.\nConsumers can validate records against the published schemas.\nCoverage and provenance limitations can be traced before the data is used."
