  - id: "project:project-thoughtline:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant project?
    text: "Montasim built Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant, an extension project. User-controlled Chrome side-panel extension for drafting replies from pasted or selected LinkedIn posts, refining writing, and keeping every publish action manual. His role covered product designer and browser-extension engineer, with scope spanning browser integration, provider workflows, trust boundaries, and privacy. Users can paste a post or select a visible conversation and draft four reply directions. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-thoughtline","case-study:thoughtline:problem","case-study:thoughtline:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "User-controlled Chrome side-panel extension for drafting replies from pasted or selected LinkedIn posts, refining writing, and keeping every publish action manual.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-17T21:02:58Z\nb2085eead2f039535d2caf81c901d57c0028298a\n2026-07-17T20:56:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Thoughtline\nproject-thoughtline\nassets/projects/thoughtline.png\nhttps://thoughtline.netlify.app\nhttps://thoughtline.netlify.app/brand/thoughtline-mark.svg\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Thoughtline/releases/latest\nWXT\nReact","Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while keeping the author's own voice, intent, and control. The page is mutable third-party content, so passing it straight into a prompt would mix data, instructions, and potentially hostile text. An assistant that drafts well but cannot be trusted with page content and credentials is not useful.\nLinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before it enters any prompt.\nOpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all","Users can paste a post or select a visible conversation and draft four reply directions.\nUsers can generate and refine post ideas in their configured voice.\nRequests follow a fixed free-only route across OpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq under defined fallback rules.\nUsers can export and restore validated configurations and history."
  - id: "project:project-thoughtline:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant?
    text: "Montasim built Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant with WXT, React 19, TypeScript, Chrome Manifest V3, Chrome Extensions API, Tailwind CSS, Hugeicons, OpenRouter API, Gemini API, Groq API, Zod, Vitest, Playwright, React Testing Library, TanStack Start, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git, System Design. The product goal is clear: User-controlled Chrome side-panel extension for drafting replies from pasted or selected LinkedIn posts, refining writing, and keeping every publish action manual. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: LinkedIn content scripts and a manual paste input feed React side-panel workflows. Typed drafting and source-research ports route requests through curated free OpenRouter models first, with Gemini and Groq as fallbacks, and encrypted browser storage handles credentials, history, migrations, and backups under Manifest V3 permissions. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-thoughtline","case-study:thoughtline:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "User-controlled Chrome side-panel extension for drafting replies from pasted or selected LinkedIn posts, refining writing, and keeping every publish action manual.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-17T21:02:58Z\nb2085eead2f039535d2caf81c901d57c0028298a\n2026-07-17T20:56:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Thoughtline\nproject-thoughtline\nassets/projects/thoughtline.png\nhttps://thoughtline.netlify.app\nhttps://thoughtline.netlify.app/brand/thoughtline-mark.svg\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Thoughtline/releases/latest\nWXT\nReact","LinkedIn content scripts extract the visible conversation, and a bounded manual input accepts pasted posts.\nPage integration\nReact side-panel workflows cover replies, ideas, refinement, history, settings, and onboarding.\nExtension interface\nTyped drafting and source-research ports use curated free OpenRouter models first, apply bounded provider timeouts, and fall back to Gemini and Groq.\nProvider boundary\nEncrypted browser storage, migrations, and backup and export features sit behind Manifest V3"
  - id: "project:project-sigcraft:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Sigcraft project?
    text: "Montasim built Sigcraft, a tool project. A Gmail-ready email signature generator with 26 layouts in any colour, plus eight ready-to-send email templates for job search and client work, with every detail saved only in the visitor's browser. His role covered product designer and full-stack engineer, with scope spanning product design, email-safe HTML rendering, server-side icon rendering, editor experience, testing and deployment. Signatures paste into Gmail and render as previewed, at the same size in any window. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-sigcraft","case-study:sigcraft:problem","case-study:sigcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A Gmail-ready email signature generator with 26 layouts in any colour, plus eight ready-to-send email templates for job search and client work, with every detail saved only in the visitor's browser.\nfalse\n2026-11-02T14:30:35Z\n74987e62945bad6432dd3dc58bebdf685dc6c22b\n2026-11-02T14:41:04Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/sigcraft\nproject-sigcraft\nassets/projects/sigcraft.webp\nhttps://sigcraft.netlify.app\nhttps://sigcraft.netlify.app/favicon.svg\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix","Most email signatures look right in a design tool and break in Gmail. Classes, style blocks, SVG icons and percentage widths get stripped, images stop loading, and the layout changes from one inbox to the next.\nGmail strips classes, style blocks and SVG, so every signature has to be built from tables with inline styles and fixed pixel widths.\nThe photo and icons load from a public URL each time an email opens, so the image host must stay stable and cache-friendly.\nMail apps' dark modes recolour text but never","Signatures paste into Gmail and render as previewed, at the same size in any window.\nTwenty-six signature layouts, nine colour presets or any custom hex, and eight email-safe fonts ship from one editor.\nIcons in any colour are served as year-cached PNGs, and older icon versions remain available for emails already sent.\nThirty-one automated tests cover the signature builder, email fields and icon renderer, and CI runs format, lint, type check, test and build on every push.\nKnown limits are documented: images depend"
  - id: "project:project-sigcraft:technology"
