    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Sigcraft?
    text: "Montasim built Sigcraft with TanStack Start, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Hugeicons, Netlify, Vitest, GitHub Actions. The product goal is clear: A Gmail-ready email signature generator with 26 layouts in any colour, plus eight ready-to-send email templates for job search and client work, with every detail saved only in the visitor's browser. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A TanStack Start app renders the editor and email templates, a pure builder turns editor state into email-safe HTML, and a server route draws icons as long-cached PNGs on Netlify. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-sigcraft","case-study:sigcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A Gmail-ready email signature generator with 26 layouts in any colour, plus eight ready-to-send email templates for job search and client work, with every detail saved only in the visitor's browser.\nfalse\n2026-11-02T14:30:35Z\n74987e62945bad6432dd3dc58bebdf685dc6c22b\n2026-11-02T14:41:04Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/sigcraft\nproject-sigcraft\nassets/projects/sigcraft.webp\nhttps://sigcraft.netlify.app\nhttps://sigcraft.netlify.app/favicon.svg\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix","Four steps (Details, Images, Templates, Styles) drive a live preview inside a mail window, with the editor state kept in the browser's localStorage.\nEditor\nA pure function turns editor state into tables with inline styles only. The preview and the copy share that markup and differ only in where images load from.\nSignature builder\nA versioned /api/icon route draws each Hugeicons glyph as a PNG at four times display size without image libraries. Each URL fully describes its image, so Netlify's CDN caches it for a"
  - id: "project:project-rootstory:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Rootstory project?
    text: "Montasim built Rootstory, a website project. A private, local-first family archive for mapping relationships, preserving life details and photographs, and carrying a complete family history between browsers. His role covered product designer and frontend engineer, with scope spanning product strategy, relationship modeling, responsive interface design, browser persistence, data portability, testing, and deployment. Families can build and inspect a multi-generation tree with direct, consistently styled relationship connectors. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-rootstory","case-study:rootstory:problem","case-study:rootstory:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A private, local-first family archive for mapping relationships, preserving life details and photographs, and carrying a complete family history between browsers.\nfalse\n2026-10-08T06:19:42Z\nfafb2b14994bec04d9240d4bddb99411b387f050\n2026-10-08T13:52:41Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/rootstory\nproject-rootstory\nassets/projects/rootstory.webp\nhttps://therootstory.netlify.app\nhttps://therootstory.netlify.app/favicon.ico\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nRadix UI\nZod\nIndexedDB\nNetlify\nGitHub Actions\nNode.js","Family history becomes difficult to trust when relationships are visually ambiguous, important life details are scattered across tools, and an export cannot recreate the archive that a family carefully assembled.\nParent, child, partner, former-partner, and sibling relationships must remain visually distinct without making the tree difficult to scan.\nPortraits, contact details, notes, and living-person records are sensitive, so the application stores its working archive locally and does not silently upload it.\nA","Families can build and inspect a multi-generation tree with direct, consistently styled relationship connectors.\nThe complete archive format restores profiles, relationships, portraits, private fields, sharing records, and display settings together.\nPeople, Timeline, Calendar, version history, and print views reuse the same validated archive rather than maintaining separate copies.\nTwenty-two automated domain and portability tests, formatting, linting, type checking, and production builds run in the public CI"
