  - id: "project:project-giftbook:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Giftbook project?
    text: "Montasim built Giftbook, a website project. A Bangla-first offline-capable PWA for recording family event gifts in the owner's own Google Sheet, with QR-based family sharing, Excel export, and no application server. His role covered product designer and frontend engineer, with scope spanning product specification, Bangla-first interface design, local-first data model, Google Sheets synchronization, sharing workflow, PWA packaging, testing, and Netlify deployment. Families can record who gave what at weddings, aqiqahs, and birthdays from any phone, offline, and see per-event received, given, and unopened-envelope summaries. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-giftbook","case-study:giftbook:problem","case-study:giftbook:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A Bangla-first offline-capable PWA for recording family event gifts in the owner's own Google Sheet, with QR-based family sharing, Excel export, and no application server.\nfalse\n2026-10-09T06:25:34Z\n88022a4f297b1b53a040f7dfcab1bea55e93e55a\n2026-10-09T06:27:06Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/giftbook\nproject-giftbook\nassets/projects/giftbook.webp\nhttps://giftbook.netlify.app\nhttps://giftbook.netlify.app/favicon.ico\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nTanStack","Bangladeshi families track wedding, aqiqah, and birthday gifts in paper notebooks that are hard to search, easy to lose, and impossible for several relatives to update at once, while generic apps store the family's financial records on a third-party server and ignore Bangla numerals and lakh formatting.\nThe ledger must live in the owner's Google Drive with no application server or database, so the app may request only the drive.file scope and never see the rest of the Drive.\nGift entry happens at crowded events","Families can record who gave what at weddings, aqiqahs, and birthdays from any phone, offline, and see per-event received, given, and unopened-envelope summaries.\nThe ledger stays in the owner's Google Drive as an editable spreadsheet, with the app holding only drive.file access and no server-side copy.\nMultiple relatives write to the same ledger through a QR link, and conflicting edits resolve predictably without duplicate rows.\nThe complete workflow, including sharing and sync failure simulations, runs in mock"
  - id: "project:project-giftbook:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Giftbook?
    text: "Montasim built Giftbook with TanStack Start, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, TanStack Form, Zod, Dexie, IndexedDB, Google Sheets API, Google Drive API, Google Identity Services, SheetJS, Vite PWA, Netlify, Infisical, Node.js Test Runner, System Design. The product goal is clear: A Bangla-first offline-capable PWA for recording family event gifts in the owner's own Google Sheet, with QR-based family sharing, Excel export, and no application server. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A TanStack Start single-page PWA keeps a per-ledger Dexie database as the working copy, and a sync engine pulls, merges, and pushes rows against a three-tab Google Sheet whose columns are derived from Zod schemas. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-giftbook","case-study:giftbook:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A Bangla-first offline-capable PWA for recording family event gifts in the owner's own Google Sheet, with QR-based family sharing, Excel export, and no application server.\nfalse\n2026-10-09T06:25:34Z\n88022a4f297b1b53a040f7dfcab1bea55e93e55a\n2026-10-09T06:27:06Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/giftbook\nproject-giftbook\nassets/projects/giftbook.webp\nhttps://giftbook.netlify.app\nhttps://giftbook.netlify.app/favicon.ico\nTanStack Start\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nTanStack","Zod schemas for events, people, and gifts define both form validation and the exact column order of the sheet, with every record carrying updatedAt, updatedBy, and a soft-delete marker.\nLedger domain\nEach ledger gets its own Dexie IndexedDB database. A single repository module is the only write path, and live queries feed the pages so edits appear instantly across tabs.\nLocal persistence\nA pull, merge, save, push cycle runs after writes, on an interval, and when the device comes back online, resolving conflicts by"
  - id: "project:project-release-agent-skill:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's release-agent-skill project?
    text: "Montasim built release-agent-skill, a skill project. An end-to-end release skill for Codex and Claude Code that validates portable Agent Skills, inspects npm archives, runs only authorized publication stages and verifies the public artifacts. His role covered agent workflow designer and automation engineer, with scope spanning agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. Agents can prepare a release without publishing anything. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-release-agent-skill","case-study:release-agent-skill:problem","case-study:release-agent-skill:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "An end-to-end release skill for Codex and Claude Code that validates portable Agent Skills, inspects npm archives, runs only authorized publication stages and verifies the public","Releasing a skill spans mutable local state and several independent remote systems. That makes partial success, version drift, and accidental publication easy, and an agent told to \"ship\" can easily do more than the maintainer intended.\nPublishing, pushing, creating releases, deploying, and changing the catalog each need separate authorization.\nCodex and Claude Code require different install destinations but must share one portable payload.\nAn immutable artifact may already exist when a later stage fails, so the","Agents can prepare a release without publishing anything.\nAgents execute only the stages the maintainer has authorized.\nPartial remote success is reported as partial rather than hidden.\nThe tag, GitHub release, npm package, install command, and catalog state are verified from the consumer side."
  - id: "project:project-release-agent-skill:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for release-agent-skill?
    text: "Montasim built release-agent-skill with Agent Skills, JavaScript, Node.js, npm, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git. The product goal is clear: An end-to-end release skill for Codex and Claude Code that validates portable Agent Skills, inspects npm archives, runs only authorized publication stages and verifies the public artifacts. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A SKILL.md file guides the agent through each release stage, backed by deterministic preflight and release-gate references. An npm installer and package contract, verified by consumer-side tests, deliver the skill to both hosts. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-release-agent-skill","case-study:release-agent-skill:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "An end-to-end release skill for Codex and Claude Code that validates portable Agent Skills, inspects npm archives, runs only authorized publication stages and verifies the public","SKILL.md orchestrates the release stages the agent follows and the authorization each one needs.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic references define the preflight checks and release gates that must pass before publication.\nAutomation\nAn npm installer, a package contract, and consumer-side tests confirm what users actually receive.\nVerification\nA SKILL.md file guides the agent through each release stage, backed by deterministic preflight and release-gate references. An npm installer and package contract, verified by"
  - id: "project:project-1snap:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's 1Snap project?
    text: "Montasim built 1Snap, an extension project. Local-first Chrome extension that captures complete web pages, restores page state, stitches frames in the browser, and adds non-destructive annotations before PNG export. His role covered product designer and browser-extension engineer, with scope spanning capture lifecycle, page stabilization, local image processing, annotation tooling, and release delivery. Users can turn a regular web page into one full-height PNG, then mark details with Marker, Highlight, or Border tools. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-1snap","case-study:1snap:problem","case-study:1snap:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Local-first Chrome extension that captures complete web pages, restores page state, stitches frames in the browser, and adds non-destructive annotations before PNG export.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-28T05:24:40Z\na95586379ad877413a97faa96afadfbbdd771a86\n2026-08-28T05:51:14Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/1Snap\nproject-1snap\nassets/projects/1snap.png\nhttps://1snap.netlify.app\nhttps://1snap.netlify.app/favicon-32.png\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/1Snap/releases/latest\nWXT\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions","Chrome's standard screenshot flow captures only the visible viewport, while many full-page tools add heavy editors, accounts, or cloud upload. 1Snap had to capture and mark one complete image without introducing a remote processing boundary.\nChrome can capture only the active visible tab, so the source tab must stay active while 1Snap scrolls and records each frame.\nDynamic content, visible images and fonts, fixed or sticky elements, and the user's original scroll position must be stabilized or restored without","Users can turn a regular web page into one full-height PNG, then mark details with Marker, Highlight, or Border tools.\nCapture frames, vector annotations, image assembly, and export composition remain inside extension-owned browser storage and processing.\nThe result page combines measured capture evidence with selection, movement, resizing, color and width controls, undo, redo, clear, copy, and download.\nMaintainers shipped v0.1.1 as a verified Chrome ZIP with a matching published SHA-256 checksum."
  - id: "project:project-1snap:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for 1Snap?
    text: "Montasim built 1Snap with WXT, React 19, TypeScript, Chrome Manifest V3, Chrome Extensions API, Browser Canvas API, SVG, IndexedDB, Vite, Sharp, Vitest, React Testing Library, Happy DOM, Netlify, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: Local-first Chrome extension that captures complete web pages, restores page state, stitches frames in the browser, and adds non-destructive annotations before PNG export. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A toolbar-triggered WXT background controller coordinates the active tab, while an injected page script measures, stabilizes, scrolls, and restores the document. Frames and annotations live in extension-owned IndexedDB, and local Canvas stitching feeds a React result page with an SVG editing overlay that composes marks into the PNG at original resolution. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-1snap","case-study:1snap:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Local-first Chrome extension that captures complete web pages, restores page state, stitches frames in the browser, and adds non-destructive annotations before PNG export.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-28T05:24:40Z\na95586379ad877413a97faa96afadfbbdd771a86\n2026-08-28T05:51:14Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/1Snap\nproject-1snap\nassets/projects/1snap.png\nhttps://1snap.netlify.app\nhttps://1snap.netlify.app/favicon-32.png\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/1Snap/releases/latest\nWXT\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions","The background controller owns the active-tab lifecycle, progress badge, capture rate, cancellation, and recovery.\nCapture orchestration\nAn injected page script measures the document, waits for visible assets, pauses motion, manages sticky elements, and restores the original presentation.\nPage preparation\nCaptured frames and vector annotation documents are retained separately in IndexedDB, and the browser Canvas API stitches frames into a bounded PNG.\nLocal processing\nA React result page reports capture"
