    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A Chromium extension that answers questions about a YouTube video with Gemini, using its visible metadata, transcript and the viewer's own API key.\n\nfalse\n2025-08-26T19:20:17Z\n56548b55d75dfd868310994f001c2650f541ee3f\n2025-08-26T19:20:14Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery\nproject-vidquery\nassets/projects/vidquery.png\nhttps://vidquery.netlify.app\nhttps://vidquery.netlify.app/brand/logo.svg\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery/releases/latest\nWXT\nTypeScript\nReact 19\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions","Viewers often scrub through long YouTube videos to find one specific answer. The page already exposes metadata and a transcript that could answer the question directly. Without a way to query that context, the viewer spends time searching instead of learning.\nAnswer quality is limited by whether a transcript exists and how accurate it is.\nGemini calls require the user's own API key and send bounded video context to an external provider.\nChanges to YouTube routes or page structure, along with extension permission","Users can ask questions about an open YouTube video.\nAnswers draw on the available transcript and visible metadata.\nUsers keep control of their own provider credential.\nMaintainers can produce installable GitHub release artifacts."
  - id: "project:project-vidquery:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension?
    text: "Montasim built VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension with WXT, TypeScript, React 19, Chrome Manifest V3, Chrome Extensions API, Tailwind CSS, Gemini API, Zod, TanStack Start, Vitest, React Testing Library, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git, System Design. The product goal is clear: A Chromium extension that answers questions about a YouTube video with Gemini, using its visible metadata, transcript and the viewer's own API key. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A content layer extracts metadata and transcripts from the open YouTube page and passes them to a React extension interface. A validated context envelope and a Gemini adapter form the provider boundary, while browser storage holds settings and a companion web app documents and distributes the Manifest V3 package. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-vidquery","case-study:vidquery:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A Chromium extension that answers questions about a YouTube video with Gemini, using its visible metadata, transcript and the viewer's own API key.\n\nfalse\n2025-08-26T19:20:17Z\n56548b55d75dfd868310994f001c2650f541ee3f\n2025-08-26T19:20:14Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery\nproject-vidquery\nassets/projects/vidquery.png\nhttps://vidquery.netlify.app\nhttps://vidquery.netlify.app/brand/logo.svg\n\nhttps://github.com/montasim/VidQuery/releases/latest\nWXT\nTypeScript\nReact 19\nChrome Manifest V3\nChrome Extensions","Content scripts detect YouTube routes and extract the visible metadata and transcript.\nPage integration\nA React interface collects the question and tracks request state.\nExtension interface\nA validated envelope bounds the transcript and context before a Gemini adapter sends it.\nProvider boundary\nBrowser storage and permissions hold the user's settings, and a release workflow builds the Manifest V3 package that the companion web app documents and distributes.\nStorage and delivery\nA content layer extracts metadata"
  - id: "project:project-shrnkly:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Shrnkly project?
    text: "Montasim built Shrnkly, a website project. Multilingual Next.js application for shortening URLs, sharing text, generating QR codes, and viewing basic usage analytics. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning product workflows, data boundaries, and delivery. Users can shorten links and track how they are used. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-shrnkly","case-study:shrnkly:problem","case-study:shrnkly:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Multilingual Next.js application for shortening URLs, sharing text, generating QR codes, and viewing basic usage analytics.\n\nfalse\n2025-06-16T05:25:05Z\n2653a830a22850be2144a63fe429f887acfc6f31\n2025-06-16T05:24:51Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/shrnkly-url-shortener\nproject-shrnkly\nassets/projects/shrnkly.png\nhttps://shrnkly.netlify.app\nhttps://shrnkly.netlify.app/favicon.ico\n\nNext.js\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nPrisma\nnext-intl\nQRCode\nReact Hook Form\nZod\nJest\nREST APIs\nReact\nTailwind CSS\nGitHub Actions\nGit\nSystem","People who share links, short-lived text or code, and QR codes usually need a separate tool for each. Those tools give the sharer little control over custom slugs, access, expiry, or basic view and click records, so the same lifecycle questions are answered differently in every place.\nPassword protection is application access control, not a safe place to store secrets.\nLogging clicks and views has privacy and data retention implications.\nRedis is installed and Docker artifacts are checked in, but neither is","Users can shorten links and track how they are used.\nText and code can be shared with bounded visibility and lifetime.\nQR payloads can be created once and reused.\nUsers can apply the supported access and lifecycle controls with their privacy limits stated plainly."
  - id: "project:project-shrnkly:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Shrnkly?
    text: "Montasim built Shrnkly with Next.js, TypeScript, MongoDB, Prisma, next-intl, QRCode, React Hook Form, Zod, Jest, REST APIs, React, Tailwind CSS, GitHub Actions, Git, System Design. The product goal is clear: Multilingual Next.js application for shortening URLs, sharing text, generating QR codes, and viewing basic usage analytics. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A localized Next.js interface handles URL, text, and QR creation, while server actions and routes perform creation, redirects, and access checks. Prisma persists the records to MongoDB, and authentication, validation, and QR and browser-share adapters support those core flows. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-shrnkly","case-study:shrnkly:architecture"
