  - id: "project:project-ramadan-clock:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Ramadan Clock project?
    text: "Montasim built Ramadan Clock, a website project. District-aware Sehri and Iftar schedules for Bangladesh, with PDF exports and an authenticated area for managing timetables. His role covered product design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning product workflows, data boundaries, and delivery. Visitors can check today's times and the full Ramadan schedule for their district. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-ramadan-clock","case-study:ramadan-clock:problem","case-study:ramadan-clock:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "District-aware Sehri and Iftar schedules for Bangladesh, with PDF exports and an authenticated area for managing timetables.\n\nfalse\n2026-02-19T04:40:57Z\nda1ee826b06fb925d92fef33f40a8f28da29b1e4\n2026-02-19T09:47:00Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/ramadan-clock\nproject-ramadan-clock\nassets/projects/ramadan-clock.png\nhttps://fasttimes.netlify.app\nhttps://fasttimes.netlify.app/favicon.ico\n\nNext.js\nTypeScript\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nTailwind CSS\nNextAuth\nAladhan API\njsPDF\nREST APIs\nReact\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nZod\nVitest\nGitHub","People observing Ramadan in Bangladesh need clear Sehri and Iftar times for their own district, shown in local time. Those times depend on the external prayer-time source, the selected district, and later timetable corrections, so the maintenance workflow behind the public view has to be auditable.\nCalculated times depend on the source, calculation method, location, and the local authority people follow.\nExternal fetching needs retries and rate limiting, and it must not overwrite validated data","Visitors can check today's times and the full Ramadan schedule for their district.\nAdministrators can import, fetch, and manage location data.\nDuplicate and malformed records are rejected before they are written.\nUsers can export schedules, and the source and accuracy guidance travels with the data."
  - id: "project:project-ramadan-clock:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Ramadan Clock?
    text: "Montasim built Ramadan Clock with Next.js, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, Prisma, Tailwind CSS, NextAuth, Aladhan API, jsPDF, REST APIs, React, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, Zod, Vitest, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: District-aware Sehri and Iftar schedules for Bangladesh, with PDF exports and an authenticated area for managing timetables. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A Next.js application serves the public schedule and a protected admin area, and a schedule service handles queries, imports, status, and formatting. Times are stored in PostgreSQL through Prisma as unique date and location records behind a tagged cache, while an Aladhan adapter with retry and token-bucket controls fetches source data and export generators produce downloadable schedules. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-ramadan-clock","case-study:ramadan-clock:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "District-aware Sehri and Iftar schedules for Bangladesh, with PDF exports and an authenticated area for managing timetables.\n\nfalse\n2026-02-19T04:40:57Z\nda1ee826b06fb925d92fef33f40a8f28da29b1e4\n2026-02-19T09:47:00Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/ramadan-clock\nproject-ramadan-clock\nassets/projects/ramadan-clock.png\nhttps://fasttimes.netlify.app\nhttps://fasttimes.netlify.app/favicon.ico\n\nNext.js\nTypeScript\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nTailwind CSS\nNextAuth\nAladhan API\njsPDF\nREST APIs\nReact\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nZod\nVitest\nGitHub","Next.js pages serve the public schedule views and the authenticated admin area.\nInterface\nA schedule service handles queries, imports, live status, and time formatting.\nDomain\nPrisma stores times in PostgreSQL as unique date and location records, and a tagged cache serves public reads.\nData and integrations\nAn Aladhan adapter fetches source times with retry and token-bucket controls, and export generators produce PDF and image schedules.\nSource fetching and export\nA Next.js application serves the public schedule"
  - id: "project:project-air-traffic-control:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Air Traffic Control project?
    text: "Montasim built Air Traffic Control, a desktop project. A flight-path drawing arcade game for Android, browsers, Windows, and Linux. Guide three aircraft types across varied airfields, avoid collisions, and build a local career with offline play. His role covered game design and TypeScript engineering, with scope spanning traffic simulation, pointer routing, local progression, browser delivery, and Windows and Linux desktop distribution. Players can draw routes for three aircraft types on Android, in the browser, or in Windows and Linux desktop apps. The Android release features twelve airfields; the browser and desktop versions documented here feature nine. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-air-traffic-control","case-study:air-traffic-control:problem","case-study:air-traffic-control:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A flight-path drawing arcade game for Android, browsers, Windows, and Linux. Guide three aircraft types across varied airfields, avoid collisions, and build a local career with offline play.\nfalse\nAir Traffic Control home screen with Saltmarsh Gateway, difficulty selection, flight controls, and Google Play, Microsoft Store, and Snap Store links\n/images/projects/air-traffic-control-home.webp\nAir Traffic Control airfield selection showing unlocked locations and career","A flight-path drawing game needs collision and landing rules that agree with what players see, while keeping progress meaningful across short sessions and different screen layouts.\nFlight paths require mouse, touch, or pen input; keyboard navigation alone does not cover gameplay.\nBrowser and desktop saves are separate and local, with no cloud sync or save export.\nBrowser offline play requires an initial online visit and successful asset caching.\nResizing or changing orientation can invalidate an active airfield","Players can draw routes for three aircraft types on Android, in the browser, or in Windows and Linux desktop apps. The Android release features twelve airfields; the browser and desktop versions documented here feature nine.\nLocal career progression and achievements persist between completed sessions without requiring an account.\nThe simulation can be tested independently of Phaser rendering.\nThe product provides a Google Play installation path for Android, a browser entry point, a Microsoft Store installation"
  - id: "project:project-air-traffic-control:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Air Traffic Control?
    text: "Montasim built Air Traffic Control with TypeScript, Phaser, Vite, Electron, IndexedDB, Progressive Web App, Vitest, Playwright. The product goal is clear: A flight-path drawing arcade game for Android, browsers, Windows, and Linux. Guide three aircraft types across varied airfields, avoid collisions, and build a local career with offline play. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A framework-independent TypeScript simulation feeds Phaser rendering and pointer interactions. DOM menus coordinate sessions, IndexedDB persists local progression, and Vite builds browser and Electron delivery targets. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-air-traffic-control","case-study:air-traffic-control:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A flight-path drawing arcade game for Android, browsers, Windows, and Linux. Guide three aircraft types across varied airfields, avoid collisions, and build a local career with offline play.\nfalse\nAir Traffic Control home screen with Saltmarsh Gateway, difficulty selection, flight controls, and Google Play, Microsoft Store, and Snap Store links\n/images/projects/air-traffic-control-home.webp\nAir Traffic Control airfield selection showing unlocked locations and career","Fixed-step updates at 1/60 second, seeded random traffic, flight-path geometry, and aircraft-shaped collision polygons live outside Phaser.\nSimulation\nPhaser scenes render airfields and aircraft, while pointer routes guide aircraft to matching landing zones. DOM menus expose difficulty, career, achievements, and settings.\nInteraction and rendering\nIndexedDB stores versioned saves, career progression, achievements, and records. Completed shifts contribute progress; practice and abandoned shifts do not.\nLocal"
  - id: "project:project-air-traffic-control:download"
