  - id: "project:project-foliofarer:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Foliofarer project?
    text: "Montasim built Foliofarer, a website project. An interactive 3D developer portfolio built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, Three.js and TypeScript, with an accessible career Atlas, local Passport progress and adaptive WebGL rendering. His role covered product designer and frontend engineer, with scope spanning product design, 3D scene runtime, accessible content paths, deterministic world generation, and testing. Visitors can explore a 3D career world built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, and Three.js. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-foliofarer","case-study:foliofarer:problem","case-study:foliofarer:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "An interactive 3D developer portfolio built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, Three.js and TypeScript, with an accessible career Atlas, local Passport progress and adaptive WebGL rendering.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-11T04:55:39Z\n358c8036ebd35b75398baa5463b6acd445356533\n2026-08-11T04:54:55Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/foliofarer\nproject-foliofarer\nassets/projects/foliofarer.png\nhttps://foliofarer.netlify.app\nhttps://foliofarer.netlify.app/favicon.ico?favicon.bd4e147e.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nReact Three","A memorable 3D portfolio can hide the information recruiters need. Visitors whose devices, motion preferences, or assistive technology do not suit WebGL exploration can be shut out entirely. If the 3D scene owned the content, every fallback would be a reduced version of the real site.\nGenerated worlds must be deterministic enough for navigation, the minimap, landmarks, and tests to agree on the same route.\nEvery piece of content must remain reachable without operating the 3D scene.\nRendering must adapt to reduced","Visitors can explore a 3D career world built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, and Three.js.\nVisitors can read the same professional history through the accessible Atlas without using the scene.\nVisit progress is tracked locally through the Passport.\nThe experience adapts to a broader range of device capabilities and user preferences."
  - id: "project:project-foliofarer:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Foliofarer?
    text: "Montasim built Foliofarer with Next.js, React, TypeScript, React Three Fiber, Three.js, Drei, React Three Rapier, Tailwind CSS, Playwright, System Design. The product goal is clear: An interactive 3D developer portfolio built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, Three.js and TypeScript, with an accessible career Atlas, local Passport progress and adaptive WebGL rendering. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A Next.js route shell serves both the accessible Atlas and a React Three Fiber and Three.js scene with Rapier interaction. Both paths read the same validated portfolio data, which a deterministic journey compiler turns into route and world data. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-foliofarer","case-study:foliofarer:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "An interactive 3D developer portfolio built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, Three.js and TypeScript, with an accessible career Atlas, local Passport progress and adaptive WebGL rendering.\n\nfalse\n2026-08-11T04:55:39Z\n358c8036ebd35b75398baa5463b6acd445356533\n2026-08-11T04:54:55Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/foliofarer\nproject-foliofarer\nassets/projects/foliofarer.png\nhttps://foliofarer.netlify.app\nhttps://foliofarer.netlify.app/favicon.ico?favicon.bd4e147e.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nReact Three","Next.js routes host the accessible Atlas, which presents the professional history as a keyboard-friendly document.\nRoute shell and Atlas\nReact Three Fiber and Three.js render the explorable world, and Rapier handles physical interaction.\nScene runtime\nA deterministic journey compiler and generators produce route and world data from validated portfolio content.\nWorld data\nPlaywright, unit, and visual-baseline tests guard the compiler, interaction, and accessibility contracts, and the site ships as static"
  - id: "project:project-routempo:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Routempo project?
    text: "Montasim built Routempo, a website project. A timezone-aware routine planner for planning repeatable rhythms, recording outcomes, and learning from real behavior. His role covered product designer and full-stack engineer, with scope spanning product workflows, domain modeling, data boundaries, integrations, and delivery. Users can plan routines and run them each day in their own timezone. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-routempo","case-study:routempo:problem","case-study:routempo:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A timezone-aware routine planner for planning repeatable rhythms, recording outcomes, and learning from real behavior.\n\nfalse\n2026-06-20T17:10:25Z\n5d8a4ec2d1d8024bddbf34d7c3f4b21185f7c656\n2026-06-20T17:11:23Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Routempo\nproject-routempo\nassets/projects/routempo.png\nhttps://routempo.netlify.app\nhttps://routempo.netlify.app/logo.svg\n\nTanStack Start\nTypeScript\nReact\nTailwind CSS\nPostgreSQL\nDrizzle ORM\nBetter Auth\nWeb Push\nVitest\nPlaywright\nReact Testing Library\nSystem","Repeatable routines are easy to schedule but hard to evaluate. When plans, actual outcomes, timezone changes, and external calendars live in separate systems, a person cannot tell what they intended from what they did. Storing everything on one recurring record also means every edit risks rewriting history.\nSchedule and completion semantics must stay correct across user timezones, so the meaning of \"today\" cannot be left to display formatting.\nReminders depend on browser permission, push subscriptions, and a","Users can plan routines and run them each day in their own timezone.\nRecorded outcomes stay separate from plans, so users can review real patterns over time.\nUsers can move their account data in and out of the product.\nUsers who opt in can connect Google services and receive reminders and weekly summaries."
  - id: "project:project-routempo:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Routempo?
    text: "Montasim built Routempo with TanStack Start, TypeScript, React, Tailwind CSS, PostgreSQL, Drizzle ORM, Better Auth, Web Push, Vitest, Playwright, React Testing Library, System Design. The product goal is clear: A timezone-aware routine planner for planning repeatable rhythms, recording outcomes, and learning from real behavior. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: TanStack Start routes and a React interface sit on top of domain services for routines, sessions, and reviews. Drizzle repositories persist that history in Neon PostgreSQL behind Better Auth, while Web Push and Google Calendar and Tasks adapters, along with scheduled summary endpoints, stay at the edge. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-routempo","case-study:routempo:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A timezone-aware routine planner for planning repeatable rhythms, recording outcomes, and learning from real behavior.\n\nfalse\n2026-06-20T17:10:25Z\n5d8a4ec2d1d8024bddbf34d7c3f4b21185f7c656\n2026-06-20T17:11:23Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/Routempo\nproject-routempo\nassets/projects/routempo.png\nhttps://routempo.netlify.app\nhttps://routempo.netlify.app/logo.svg\n\nTanStack Start\nTypeScript\nReact\nTailwind CSS\nPostgreSQL\nDrizzle ORM\nBetter Auth\nWeb Push\nVitest\nPlaywright\nReact Testing Library\nSystem","TanStack Start routes and React screens handle routine planning, daily execution, and review.\nInterface\nRoutine, session, and review services own timezone conversion and keep planned intent separate from recorded outcomes.\nDomain\nDrizzle repositories store durable behavioral records in Neon PostgreSQL, with Better Auth managing accounts.\nData and auth\nWeb Push and Google Calendar and Tasks adapters, plus scheduled summary endpoints, connect the planner to reminders and external calendars.\nIntegrations\nTanStack"
  - id: "project:project-markdown-typing-svg:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Markdown Typing SVG project?
    text: "Montasim built Markdown Typing SVG, a tool project. Generator and cacheable API for customizable animated typing SVGs used in GitHub profiles, README files, and websites. His role covered developer-tool design and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning developer workflow, API surface, and deployment. Users can compose animated text visually instead of editing SVG by hand. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-markdown-typing-svg","case-study:markdown-typing-svg:problem","case-study:markdown-typing-svg:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Generator and cacheable API for customizable animated typing SVGs used in GitHub profiles, README files, and websites.\n\nfalse\n2026-02-24T08:15:43Z\n2d7790ae81f2d2cdc2a856ea59e9d16bb6825b88\n2026-02-24T08:16:21Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/markdown-typing-svg\nproject-markdown-typing-svg\nassets/projects/markdown-typing-svg.png\nhttps://markdown-typing-svg.netlify.app\nhttps://markdown-typing-svg.netlify.app/favicon.ico?favicon.b64cfb76.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nSVG\nZod\nREST APIs\nRadix UI\nMarkdown Typing","Developers who want a typing animation in a GitHub profile or README usually have to edit SVG and CSS by hand and then host the result somewhere. That makes it slow to experiment with text, timing, or styling, and hard to share or reproduce a design someone else made.\nPublic embeds depend on the deployment staying available, because every image is generated on request.\nSuccessful responses are cached for 24 hours, so an updated design may not appear in an existing embed until the cache expires.\nThere is no","Users can compose animated text visually instead of editing SVG by hand.\nA finished design can be embedded through Markdown, HTML, or a plain image URL.\nAny design can be reproduced from its query parameters alone.\nInvalid options produce an explicit validation response, and no generated files are stored."
  - id: "project:project-markdown-typing-svg:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Markdown Typing SVG?
    text: "Montasim built Markdown Typing SVG with Next.js, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, SVG, Zod, REST APIs, Radix UI. The product goal is clear: Generator and cacheable API for customizable animated typing SVGs used in GitHub profiles, README files, and websites. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A React visual editor builds the design and serializes it into URL query parameters, which the server validates with Zod. The server then generates the SVG on demand and returns it with explicit image and cache headers, so no generated file is ever stored. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-markdown-typing-svg","case-study:markdown-typing-svg:architecture"
