  - id: "project:project-http-status-lite:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's http-status-lite project?
    text: "Montasim built http-status-lite, a package project. Tiny, standards-backed, type-safe HTTP status codes for Node.js and browsers. His role covered library design and package engineering, with scope spanning typed APIs, compatibility, and package distribution. Consumers can retrieve name, category, and lifecycle metadata for status codes. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-http-status-lite","case-study:http-status-lite:problem","case-study:http-status-lite:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Tiny, standards-backed, type-safe HTTP status codes for Node.js and browsers.\n\nfalse\n2026-03-27T12:05:31Z\n8d062f2fb26e3a8e947cc164a575ab7db0be1bd7\n2025-01-28T20:25:33Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/http-status-lite\nproject-http-status-lite\nassets/projects/http-status-lite.png\nhttps://http-status-lite.netlify.app\nhttps://http-status-lite.netlify.app/favicon.svg\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/http-status-lite\nTypeScript\nNode.js\nESM\nCommonJS\ntsup\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTailwind CSS\nVite\nNetlify\nGitHub Actions\nCI/CD\nSystem","Developers need status-code names, categories, and lifecycle metadata. Without a library they fall back on memorized numeric checks or hand-written lists that go stale, and many alternatives bring a heavier dependency than the task needs.\nThe IANA registry changes over time.\nObsolete, temporary, and unused codes must remain distinguishable.\nTyping and ESM and CommonJS compatibility must not depend on live network access.","Consumers can retrieve name, category, and lifecycle metadata for status codes.\nConsumers can classify codes into success, client error, and server error categories.\nConsumers can parse and validate status codes.\nBuilds rely on a reviewable standards snapshot without runtime network access."
  - id: "project:project-http-status-lite:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for http-status-lite?
    text: "Montasim built http-status-lite with TypeScript, Node.js, ESM, CommonJS, tsup, TanStack Start, React, Tailwind CSS, Vite, Netlify, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: Tiny, standards-backed, type-safe HTTP status codes for Node.js and browsers. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A committed snapshot of the IANA registry generates the typed library. Constants, predicates, and metadata are exposed as separately importable entry points, and a workspace-linked interactive reference plus automated registry and release workflows keep the snapshot and package in step. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
