  - id: "project:project-mime-types-lite:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's mime-types-lite project?
    text: "Montasim built mime-types-lite, a package project. Tiny, zero-dependency, type-safe MIME constants and helpers for HTTP APIs, uploads, and file extensions. His role covered library design and package engineering, with scope spanning typed APIs, compatibility, and package distribution. Consumers can infer common media types from filenames. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-mime-types-lite","case-study:mime-types-lite:problem","case-study:mime-types-lite:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Tiny, zero-dependency, type-safe MIME constants and helpers for HTTP APIs, uploads, and file extensions.\n\nfalse\n2025-01-28T08:37:08Z\n4676726679de21a7d24346ac3feadf5a0af5787c\n2025-01-28T08:37:08Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/mime-types-lite\nproject-mime-types-lite\nassets/projects/mime-types-lite.png\nhttps://mime-types-lite.netlify.app\nhttps://mime-types-lite.netlify.app/favicon.svg\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/mime-types-lite\nTypeScript\nESM\nCommonJS\ntsup\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTailwind CSS\nVite\nNetlify\nGitHub","Upload, download, and HTTP code needs dependable mappings between filenames or extensions and media types. Carrying a broad MIME database into every bundle for that job adds weight most applications do not need.\nOne extension can have aliases or ambiguous mappings.\nLookup must normalize case, dots, and paths without implying that file content was inspected.\nESM and CommonJS consumers need stable typed exports with no runtime dependencies.","Consumers can infer common media types from filenames.\nConsumers can recover the preferred extension for a media type.\nMedia-type inputs can be validated and normalized before use.\nThe same API works in browser and Node.js module systems."
  - id: "project:project-mime-types-lite:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for mime-types-lite?
    text: "Montasim built mime-types-lite with TypeScript, ESM, CommonJS, tsup, TanStack Start, React, Tailwind CSS, Vite, Netlify, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, System Design. The product goal is clear: Tiny, zero-dependency, type-safe MIME constants and helpers for HTTP APIs, uploads, and file extensions. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A curated media-type registry backs pure lookup and validation functions, which are built with tsup into a dual-format package with typed exports. A TanStack Start docs and playground app in the same workspace runs against the local package. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
