  - id: "project:project-verify-github-npm-release:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's verify-github-npm-release project?
    text: "Montasim built verify-github-npm-release, a skill project. A read-only release check for Codex and Claude Code that confirms Git tags, GitHub releases, npm versions, CI evidence and install commands all agree for one release. His role covered agent workflow designer and automation engineer, with scope spanning agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. Agents can detect drift between versions and tags. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-verify-github-npm-release","case-study:verify-github-npm-release:problem","case-study:verify-github-npm-release:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A read-only release check for Codex and Claude Code that confirms Git tags, GitHub releases, npm versions, CI evidence and install commands all agree for one","A release can look complete in one system while its tag, package version, CI run, install instructions, or catalog entry points somewhere else. Each surface looks fine on its own, so the disagreement is easy to miss until a user installs the wrong version.\nThe audit must never change or repair release state.\nAuthentication and network errors cannot be interpreted as a missing artifact.\nMutable aliases such as latest are weaker proof than an exact package@version.","Agents can detect drift between versions and tags.\nReports distinguish missing artifacts from verification that was blocked.\nEach report names the exact surface that failed.\nReports provide evidence links without claiming that an install was executed."
  - id: "project:project-verify-github-npm-release:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for verify-github-npm-release?
    text: "Montasim built verify-github-npm-release with Agent Skills, JavaScript, Node.js, npm, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git. The product goal is clear: A read-only release check for Codex and Claude Code that confirms Git tags, GitHub releases, npm versions, CI evidence and install commands all agree for one release. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: The skill collects evidence from Git, GitHub, npm, and an optional catalog, then compares it against one release identity with deterministic evaluators. Each finding is classified and written into a human-readable report. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-verify-github-npm-release","case-study:verify-github-npm-release:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A read-only release check for Codex and Claude Code that confirms Git tags, GitHub releases, npm versions, CI evidence and install commands all agree for one","The agent collects release evidence from local Git, GitHub, npm, and the catalog.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic evaluators compare that evidence against one release identity and classify each finding.\nAutomation\nA human-facing report marks each surface as pass, warning, fail, or blocked, with links to the evidence.\nVerification\nThe skill collects evidence from Git, GitHub, npm, and an optional catalog, then compares it against one release identity with deterministic evaluators. Each finding is classified and"
  - id: "project:project-make-project-github-ready:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's make-project-github-ready project?
    text: "Montasim built make-project-github-ready, a skill project. A repository-readiness skill for Codex and Claude Code that evaluates identity, metadata, topics, community health, security guidance, CI, and Git state before applying authorized local or GitHub changes. His role covered agent workflow design and automation engineering, with scope spanning agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. Agents can identify the gaps between a working codebase and a publicly ready repository. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-make-project-github-ready","case-study:make-project-github-ready:problem","case-study:make-project-github-ready:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A repository-readiness skill for Codex and Claude Code that evaluates identity, metadata, topics, community health, security guidance, CI, and Git state before applying authorized local or GitHub","A codebase can build and pass tests while its Git identity, metadata, security guidance, community files, secret hygiene, and CI are not ready for public collaboration. Making it public exposes all of that at once, including whatever is buried in the history. The move needs to be a deliberate security and operations change rather than a single click.\nCreating a repository, changing visibility, changing branch settings, and pushing are external mutations.\nA license and a homepage URL cannot be assumed or","Agents can identify the gaps between a working codebase and a publicly ready repository.\nCommunity and CI materials are prepared from facts about the project.\nProposed repository metadata reflects what the project actually is.\nA repository is created or configured only when authorized, and the resulting state is verified."
  - id: "project:project-make-project-github-ready:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for make-project-github-ready?
    text: "Montasim built make-project-github-ready with Agent Skills, JavaScript, Node.js, npm, GitHub Actions, CI/CD, Git. The product goal is clear: A repository-readiness skill for Codex and Claude Code that evaluates identity, metadata, topics, community health, security guidance, CI, and Git state before applying authorized local or GitHub changes. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A local auditor inventories manifests, Git state, community files, and CI workflows, and metadata rules turn that inventory into proposed repository settings. Remote changes go through the GitHub CLI or API only when authorized, and each one is read back to confirm the result. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-make-project-github-ready","case-study:make-project-github-ready:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A repository-readiness skill for Codex and Claude Code that evaluates identity, metadata, topics, community health, security guidance, CI, and Git state before applying authorized local or GitHub","A local auditor inventories recognized manifests, Git state, community files, CI workflows, and secret-named configuration.\nSkill workflow\nMetadata and preparation rules propose an accurate description, homepage, topics, and default branch based on what the project actually is.\nAutomation\nAuthorization-gated GitHub CLI and API actions are followed by a read-back of the remote settings and commit SHAs.\nVerification\nA local auditor inventories manifests, Git state, community files, and CI workflows, and metadata"
