  - id: "project:project-book-heaven:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Book Heaven project?
    text: "Montasim built Book Heaven, a website project. Admin Dashboard UI built with Shadcn and Next.js. His role covered incident diagnosis, build engineering, and release verification, with scope spanning next.js build reliability, dependency compatibility, and Vercel delivery. The repaired main-branch build completed successfully on Vercel at the verified commit. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-book-heaven","case-study:book-heaven:problem","case-study:book-heaven:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Admin Dashboard UI built with Shadcn and Next.js.\n\nfalse\n2025-01-20T04:52:31Z\n1287edc200127f4a20ae7a343531d6aaaa6ad5e7\n2025-12-21T01:36:12Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/book-heaven\nproject-book-heaven\nassets/projects/book-heaven.png\nhttps://bookheavenbeta.vercel.app\nhttps://bookheavenbeta.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.0b3bf435.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nRedis\nBullMQ\nSocket.io\nStripe\nREST APIs\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nTanStack Query\nReact Hook Form\nResend\nZod\nSystem Design\nBook","The SupportKori integration was correct in source and merged to main, but Book Heaven's Vercel production deployment failed before the new build reached visitors. The live site therefore looked as if the widget change were missing, even though the actual fault was in the delivery path. The recovery needed to identify each build blocker without weakening existing API contracts or treating a merged pull request as proof of a successful release.\nThe large Next.js application compiles many routes and integrations","The repaired main-branch build completed successfully on Vercel at the verified commit.\nBook Heaven's public site now loads the exact SupportKori script with the required ID, message, color, and position.\nBuild behavior is less sensitive to heap exhaustion and excessive worker parallelism.\nStripe type compatibility can no longer drift beyond the selected API contract through an implicit minor-version update.\nThe release workflow now distinguishes code merged from deployment completed and production artifact"
  - id: "project:project-book-heaven:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Book Heaven?
    text: "Montasim built Book Heaven with Next.js, React, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, Prisma, Redis, BullMQ, Socket.io, Stripe, REST APIs, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui, Radix UI, TanStack Query, React Hook Form, Resend, Zod, System Design. The product goal is clear: Admin Dashboard UI built with Shadcn and Next.js. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A four-stage recovery loop separated source correctness, deterministic production compilation, platform deployment, and public runtime verification so each failure could be diagnosed at the boundary where it occurred. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-book-heaven","case-study:book-heaven:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Admin Dashboard UI built with Shadcn and Next.js.\n\nfalse\n2025-01-20T04:52:31Z\n1287edc200127f4a20ae7a343531d6aaaa6ad5e7\n2025-12-21T01:36:12Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/book-heaven\nproject-book-heaven\nassets/projects/book-heaven.png\nhttps://bookheavenbeta.vercel.app\nhttps://bookheavenbeta.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.0b3bf435.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nRedis\nBullMQ\nSocket.io\nStripe\nREST APIs\nTailwind CSS\nshadcn/ui\nRadix UI\nTanStack Query\nReact Hook Form\nResend\nZod\nSystem Design\nBook","A root-layout Next.js Script component owns the exact SupportKori URL, account ID, message, color, and position.\nSource contract\nPrisma generation and Next.js compilation run with a 4 GB Node.js heap and two build workers to keep resource use predictable.\nBuild envelope\nStripe is pinned to version 20.1.0 so its types remain aligned with the API version selected by the application.\nDependency contract\nGitHub status, the Vercel preview, the production deployment, and the returned public HTML form a chain of release"
  - id: "project:project-github-readme-counter:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's GitHub README Counter project?
    text: "Montasim built GitHub README Counter, an API project. Express service that increments a file-backed counter and returns the current value as a customizable SVG image. His role covered API design and backend engineering, with scope spanning HTTP delivery, state management, and SVG generation. Self-hosters can embed a counter in any README without provisioning a database. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-github-readme-counter","case-study:github-readme-counter:problem","case-study:github-readme-counter:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Express service that increments a file-backed counter and returns the current value as a customizable SVG image.\n\nfalse\n2024-06-25T12:31:13Z\n9e56d4f2204c5ec6366c5306f20404219739290c\n2024-06-25T10:48:55Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/github-readme-counter\nproject-github-readme-counter\nhttps://github-readme-counter.onrender.com/count.svg\n\nNode.js\nExpress.js\nJavaScript\nSVG\nSystem Design\nGitHub README","README authors who want a number that changes cannot get one from Markdown alone, because Markdown cannot run JavaScript or call an API and render the result. The only dynamic thing a README can embed is an image URL, so a counter has to be a service that returns an image.\nEvery image fetch is counted, including bots, caches, image proxies, renderers, and refreshes, so the value is a request count rather than unique human visitors.\nCounts persist to a file in a single process, so they can reset on an ephemeral","Self-hosters can embed a counter in any README without provisioning a database.\nUsers can customize the badge's colors, label, and style through the request.\nSeparate counters can be kept for different identifiers.\nSelf-hosters can check basic process health through the status endpoint."
  - id: "project:project-github-readme-counter:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for GitHub README Counter?
    text: "Montasim built GitHub README Counter with Node.js, Express.js, JavaScript, SVG, System Design. The product goal is clear: Express service that increments a file-backed counter and returns the current value as a customizable SVG image. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Express routes validate the query options and increment or read a named counter that is held in memory and persisted to a file. The current value is rendered into an escaped SVG response, and a separate JSON endpoint reports service health. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-github-readme-counter","case-study:github-readme-counter:architecture"
