  - id: "project:project-mmh-patient-portal:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's MyMedicalHub Patient Portal project?
    text: "Montasim contributed to MyMedicalHub Patient Portal, a website project delivered within the MyMedicalHub team. A multi-tenant digital-health portal for patient onboarding, AI-assisted intake, movement assessments, exercise programs, reports, billing, appointments, and remote-assistance workflows. His role covered frontend architecture and product engineering within the MyMedicalHub team, with scope spanning patient journeys, movement and computer-vision workflows, tenant configuration, payments, reporting, and reliability. Patients can move through onboarding, assessment, exercise, reporting, payment, and remote-care journeys in one tenant-aware portal. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-mmh-patient-portal","case-study:mmh-patient-portal:problem","case-study:mmh-patient-portal:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A multi-tenant digital-health portal for patient onboarding, AI-assisted intake, movement assessments, exercise programs, reports, billing, appointments, and remote-assistance workflows.\n\nfalse\n2020-05-01T17:45:10Z\ncfe5a65a6a9f674bca39e533374874738345f389\n2020-05-01T17:45:10Z\ntrue\nhttps://dev.azure.com/mymedicalhub/MMH%20Product%20Release%20Management/_git/patient-portal\nproject-mmh-patient-portal\nnull\n\n\ntrue\nReact\nRedux Toolkit\nJavaScript\nTensorFlow.js\nMediaPipe\nWebRTC\nVonage OpenTok\nMicrosoft Bot","Patients need to complete intake, assessments, guided movements, exercises, appointments, payments, and result review without navigating disconnected healthcare tools. The portal must keep those journeys understandable while adapting to different tenants, devices, clinical programs, and service configurations.\nCamera, audio, browser capability, network quality, and local ML performance vary across patient devices and directly affect clinical workflow reliability.\nTenant-specific features and branded journeys must","Patients can move through onboarding, assessment, exercise, reporting, payment, and remote-care journeys in one tenant-aware portal.\nMovement capture handles more device, tenant, and lifecycle edge cases without silently recording invalid or stale state.\nFeature configuration supports multiple healthcare programs while preserving a shared application foundation.\nOperational warnings and recovery paths surface earlier, reducing avoidable failures during patient-facing clinical workflows."
  - id: "project:project-mmh-patient-portal:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for MyMedicalHub Patient Portal?
    text: "Montasim's documented work on MyMedicalHub Patient Portal used React, Redux Toolkit, JavaScript, TensorFlow.js, MediaPipe, WebRTC, Vonage OpenTok, Microsoft Bot Framework, i18next, Formik, Yup, Jest, React Testing Library, System Design. The product goal is clear: A multi-tenant digital-health portal for patient onboarding, AI-assisted intake, movement assessments, exercise programs, reports, billing, appointments, and remote-assistance workflows. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A React application composes tenant-aware patient journeys, Redux and focused context boundaries coordinate long-running workflows, browser media and ML adapters support movement analysis, and guarded service integrations connect assessments, reporting, payments, appointments, and remote assistance. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-mmh-patient-portal","case-study:mmh-patient-portal:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A multi-tenant digital-health portal for patient onboarding, AI-assisted intake, movement assessments, exercise programs, reports, billing, appointments, and remote-assistance workflows.\n\nfalse\n2020-05-01T17:45:10Z\ncfe5a65a6a9f674bca39e533374874738345f389\n2020-05-01T17:45:10Z\ntrue\nhttps://dev.azure.com/mymedicalhub/MMH%20Product%20Release%20Management/_git/patient-portal\nproject-mmh-patient-portal\nnull\n\n\ntrue\nReact\nRedux Toolkit\nJavaScript\nTensorFlow.js\nMediaPipe\nWebRTC\nVonage OpenTok\nMicrosoft Bot","Responsive screens cover onboarding, dashboards, intake, assessments, exercises, reports, subscriptions, billing, calendars, and assistance flows.\nPatient experience\nRedux slices, React contexts, form state, and lifecycle hooks coordinate multi-step assessment, payment, callback, and chatbot journeys.\nWorkflow state\nWebcam, MediaPipe, TensorFlow.js, speech, chatbot, and WebRTC boundaries support guided movement and remote-care interactions.\nMedia and intelligence\nFeature flags, tenant themes, authenticated APIs,"
  - id: "project:project-mmh-re-annotation:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Clinical Movement Re-annotation project?
    text: "Montasim contributed to Clinical Movement Re-annotation, a website project delivered within the MyMedicalHub team. A clinical review workspace for selecting recorded movement frames, correcting detected key points, lines, and angles, comparing reference guidance, and saving validated measurements. His role covered frontend architecture and modernization within the MyMedicalHub team, with scope spanning clinical review workflow, canvas geometry, legacy API integration, validation, error recovery, and automated testing. Reviewers can correct automated clinical measurements through a guided workflow instead of manipulating an opaque legacy canvas. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-mmh-re-annotation","case-study:mmh-re-annotation:problem","case-study:mmh-re-annotation:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A clinical review workspace for selecting recorded movement frames, correcting detected key points, lines, and angles, comparing reference guidance, and saving validated measurements.\n\nfalse\n2024-04-30T14:08:06Z\n09ab2ae7824e294c39416ec16376bf94d375bae2\n2024-04-30T14:08:06Z\ntrue\nhttps://dev.azure.com/mymedicalhub/MMH%20Product%20Release%20Management/_git/re-annotation\nproject-mmh-re-annotation\nnull\n\n\ntrue\nNext.js\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nKonva\nReact Konva\nZod\nTailwind CSS\nVitest\nReact Testing","Automated movement analysis can place measurement geometry on an imperfect frame or anatomical position. Clinical reviewers need a precise, explainable way to choose the right frame, correct lines and angles, compare reference guidance, and save revised measurements without losing the original exercise context.\nMeasurement geometry must remain correct while media is scaled, points are dragged, and line types restrict movement to one axis.\nThe workflow depends on multiple tenant-specific legacy APIs and session","Reviewers can correct automated clinical measurements through a guided workflow instead of manipulating an opaque legacy canvas.\nTyped domain models preserve line, angle, direction, and measurement semantics across load, edit, render, and save operations.\nMalformed or unavailable upstream data produces explicit recovery guidance rather than partially initialized review state.\nThe modernized boundaries make geometry and API behavior independently testable while legacy services continue to operate."
  - id: "project:project-mmh-re-annotation:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Clinical Movement Re-annotation?
    text: "Montasim's documented work on Clinical Movement Re-annotation used Next.js, React 19, TypeScript, Konva, React Konva, Zod, Tailwind CSS, Vitest, React Testing Library, Playwright, Pino, System Design. The product goal is clear: A clinical review workspace for selecting recorded movement frames, correcting detected key points, lines, and angles, comparing reference guidance, and saving validated measurements. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A Next.js and React review shell separates workflow state from Konva geometry, validates upstream payloads through Zod, maps legacy responses into typed domain models, and coordinates frame selection, alignment, verification, and persistence through a reducer-driven controller. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-mmh-re-annotation","case-study:mmh-re-annotation:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A clinical review workspace for selecting recorded movement frames, correcting detected key points, lines, and angles, comparing reference guidance, and saving validated measurements.\n\nfalse\n2024-04-30T14:08:06Z\n09ab2ae7824e294c39416ec16376bf94d375bae2\n2024-04-30T14:08:06Z\ntrue\nhttps://dev.azure.com/mymedicalhub/MMH%20Product%20Release%20Management/_git/re-annotation\nproject-mmh-re-annotation\nnull\n\n\ntrue\nNext.js\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nKonva\nReact Konva\nZod\nTailwind CSS\nVitest\nReact Testing","A reducer and controller own frame selection, alignment, verification, loading, failure, save, and completion transitions.\nReview workflow\nTyped points, lines, angles, plumb references, scaling rules, and measurement calculations stay independent of rendering components.\nGeometry domain\nReact Konva renders the patient frame, draggable key points, measurement geometry, reference guidance, and responsive controls.\nCanvas interface\nSchema validation, response mappers, diagnostics, and an API client normalize tenant"
  - id: "project:project-mmh-telemedicine:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's MyMedicalHub Telemedicine project?
    text: "Montasim contributed to MyMedicalHub Telemedicine, a website project delivered within the MyMedicalHub team. A real-time telehealth application for guided movement assessments, exercise recording, pain and strength workflows, clinician collaboration, and tenant-configured session behavior. His role covered frontend and real-time workflow engineering within the MyMedicalHub team, with scope spanning webRTC sessions, media recording, exercise state, Socket.IO coordination, tenant features, and failure recovery. Patients and providers can coordinate guided movement and exercise sessions through one real-time room experience. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-mmh-telemedicine","case-study:mmh-telemedicine:problem","case-study:mmh-telemedicine:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A real-time telehealth application for guided movement assessments, exercise recording, pain and strength workflows, clinician collaboration, and tenant-configured session behavior.\n\nfalse\n2024-04-29T11:48:18Z\n41c7ac62f077a1333bbf618afe0f61f940fbe266\n2024-04-29T11:48:18Z\ntrue\nhttps://dev.azure.com/mymedicalhub/MMH%20Product%20Release%20Management/_git/telemedicine\nproject-mmh-telemedicine\nnull\n\n\ntrue\nNext.js\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nRedux Toolkit\nWebRTC\nSocket.IO\nReact Player\nFormik\nYup\nTailwind CSS\nSystem","Remote movement assessment requires more than a video call. Patients and providers must stay synchronized while exercises change, instructions play, recordings start and stop, pain or strength questions appear, network conditions vary, and either participant can leave unexpectedly.\nCamera, microphone, permissions, network quality, and WebRTC lifecycle events differ across browsers and patient devices.\nExercise, recording, pain, strength, queue, and provider-presence state must not leak from one movement or session","Patients and providers can coordinate guided movement and exercise sessions through one real-time room experience.\nExercise changes no longer reuse stale recordings or question state from a previous movement.\nStrength and other tenant-specific workflows can be introduced without forking the telehealth application.\nDeparture, connection, and service failures resolve into clearer recovery or redirect paths instead of trapping the session."
  - id: "project:project-mmh-telemedicine:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for MyMedicalHub Telemedicine?
    text: "Montasim's documented work on MyMedicalHub Telemedicine used Next.js, React 19, TypeScript, Redux Toolkit, WebRTC, Socket.IO, React Player, Formik, Yup, Tailwind CSS, System Design. The product goal is clear: A real-time telehealth application for guided movement assessments, exercise recording, pain and strength workflows, clinician collaboration, and tenant-configured session behavior. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Next.js routes host tenant-aware telehealth rooms, focused React contexts own socket, recording, media, and error state, Redux coordinates assessment APIs, and Socket.IO synchronizes room and exercise events across participants. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-mmh-telemedicine","case-study:mmh-telemedicine:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "A real-time telehealth application for guided movement assessments, exercise recording, pain and strength workflows, clinician collaboration, and tenant-configured session behavior.\n\nfalse\n2024-04-29T11:48:18Z\n41c7ac62f077a1333bbf618afe0f61f940fbe266\n2024-04-29T11:48:18Z\ntrue\nhttps://dev.azure.com/mymedicalhub/MMH%20Product%20Release%20Management/_git/telemedicine\nproject-mmh-telemedicine\nnull\n\n\ntrue\nNext.js\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nRedux Toolkit\nWebRTC\nSocket.IO\nReact Player\nFormik\nYup\nTailwind CSS\nSystem","Responsive patient and provider views present participants, exercise guidance, recording state, pain questions, and recovery messages.\nSession interface\nWebRTC streams, camera switching, recording previews, device permissions, and network-quality hooks manage browser media explicitly.\nMedia lifecycle\nSocket.IO room events synchronize connection, exercise, recording, audio, video, and participant departure behavior.\nReal-time coordination\nRedux slices and authenticated services load exercises, verify tokens,"
  - id: "project:project-liftuno:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Liftuno project?
    text: "Montasim built Liftuno, a website project. A bilingual gym-management platform for Bangladesh that replaces paper ledgers with dependable membership coverage, billing, attendance, receipts, offers, and role-aware operations. His role covered client product architecture and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning multi-tenant operations, billing rules, authentication, localization, and cross-platform delivery. Gym staff can replace fragmented paper tracking with one member, payment, and attendance ledger. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-liftuno","case-study:liftuno:problem","case-study:liftuno:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "true\nA bilingual gym-management platform for Bangladesh that replaces paper ledgers with dependable membership coverage, billing, attendance, receipts, offers, and role-aware operations.\n\nfalse\n2026-09-08T06:44:54Z\n41d4def8cc788d1fa36dc2bfbb6254fb0c2dc3d6\n2026-09-08T06:38:28Z\ntrue\nproject-liftuno\nassets/projects/liftuno.png\nhttps://liftuno-website.vercel.app\nhttps://liftuno-website.vercel.app/favicon.ico\n\nhttps://snapcraft.io/liftuno\nExpo\nReact Native\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nConvex\nBetter Auth\nExpo","Local gyms often track members, payments, attendance, and outstanding balances through paper records or staff memory. That makes daily work slow and allows financial history, access boundaries, and membership state to become ambiguous.\nMembership accounting must distinguish expired coverage from actual debt instead of inventing fees when a member stops attending.\nPlatform operators, gym administrators, staff, and members require different permissions with strict organization isolation.\nThe product must support","Gym staff can replace fragmented paper tracking with one member, payment, and attendance ledger.\nPrepaid coverage, outstanding balances, partial payments, offers, and reversals retain explicit financial meaning.\nOrganization and role boundaries are enforced by the backend rather than inferred from navigation state.\nThe same product foundation supports responsive web use now and Android validation as a separate release step."
  - id: "project:project-liftuno:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Liftuno?
    text: "Montasim built Liftuno with Expo, React Native, React 19, TypeScript, Convex, Better Auth, Expo Router, Zod, Vitest, System Design. The product goal is clear: A bilingual gym-management platform for Bangladesh that replaces paper ledgers with dependable membership coverage, billing, attendance, receipts, offers, and role-aware operations. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Expo Router and React Native provide a shared responsive interface, Convex owns realtime gym-scoped data and transactional commands, Better Auth manages sessions, and explicit domain services preserve billing and membership invariants. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-liftuno","case-study:liftuno:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "true\nA bilingual gym-management platform for Bangladesh that replaces paper ledgers with dependable membership coverage, billing, attendance, receipts, offers, and role-aware operations.\n\nfalse\n2026-09-08T06:44:54Z\n41d4def8cc788d1fa36dc2bfbb6254fb0c2dc3d6\n2026-09-08T06:38:28Z\ntrue\nproject-liftuno\nassets/projects/liftuno.png\nhttps://liftuno-website.vercel.app\nhttps://liftuno-website.vercel.app/favicon.ico\n\nhttps://snapcraft.io/liftuno\nExpo\nReact Native\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nConvex\nBetter Auth\nExpo","Expo and React Native Web provide role-aware navigation, bilingual content, and touch-friendly operational screens.\nCross-platform interface\nPure billing and workspace rules model prepaid coverage, partial payments, offers, returns, reversals, and attendance.\nMembership domain\nConvex functions resolve the authenticated user, gym grant, role, and requested organization for every protected operation.\nSecure data boundary\nBetter Auth sessions, hashed invitations, and persistent sign-in budgets protect access, while"
