    factIds[2]: "project:project-liftuno","case-study:liftuno:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "true\nA bilingual gym-management platform for Bangladesh that replaces paper ledgers with dependable membership coverage, billing, attendance, receipts, offers, and role-aware operations.\n\nfalse\n2026-09-08T06:44:54Z\n41d4def8cc788d1fa36dc2bfbb6254fb0c2dc3d6\n2026-09-08T06:38:28Z\ntrue\nproject-liftuno\nassets/projects/liftuno.png\nhttps://liftuno-website.vercel.app\nhttps://liftuno-website.vercel.app/favicon.ico\n\nhttps://snapcraft.io/liftuno\nExpo\nReact Native\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nConvex\nBetter Auth\nExpo","Gym staff can replace fragmented paper tracking with one member, payment, and attendance ledger.\nPrepaid coverage, outstanding balances, partial payments, offers, and reversals retain explicit financial meaning.\nOrganization and role boundaries are enforced by the backend rather than inferred from navigation state.\nThe same product foundation supports responsive web use now and Android validation as a separate release step."
  - id: "project:project-coaching-management:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's Coaching Management project?
    text: "Montasim built Coaching Management, a website project. A multi-tenant SaaS platform for coaching institutes to manage academic structure, students, teachers, attendance, payments, expenses, biometric devices, and SMS outreach. His role covered client platform architecture and full-stack engineering, with scope spanning multi-tenant RBAC, academic operations, finance, biometric integration, and messaging infrastructure. Institute operators can manage academic, attendance, financial, and communication work from one tenant-aware platform. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-coaching-management","case-study:coaching-management:problem","case-study:coaching-management:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "true\nA multi-tenant SaaS platform for coaching institutes to manage academic structure, students, teachers, attendance, payments, expenses, biometric devices, and SMS outreach.\n\nfalse\n2025-09-22T17:15:02Z\ne4017ceaf2450524e42db8f32ac094e30c71ad3d\n2025-09-22T17:29:41Z\ntrue\nproject-coaching-management\nnull\n\n\nNext.js\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nPostgreSQL\nSupabase\nPrisma\nTanStack Query\nshadcn/ui\nTailwind CSS\nZod\nVitest\nSystem Design\nCoaching","Coaching institutes often split student records, class structures, attendance devices, fee collection, expenses, and communication across disconnected tools. Operators need one system without allowing one organization to see or mutate another organization’s data.\nEvery academic and financial workflow must preserve organization isolation and granular role permissions.\nStudents and teachers share physical biometric devices even though they have different domain records and responsibilities.\nPayments and other","Institute operators can manage academic, attendance, financial, and communication work from one tenant-aware platform.\nGranular roles separate platform administration, organization administration, finance, and academic coordination.\nMultiple biometric device vendors can feed one attendance model without leaking vendor-specific behavior into core workflows.\nFinancial corrections and record removal retain audit evidence instead of erasing operational history."
  - id: "project:project-coaching-management:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Coaching Management?
    text: "Montasim built Coaching Management with Next.js, React 19, TypeScript, PostgreSQL, Supabase, Prisma, TanStack Query, shadcn/ui, Tailwind CSS, Zod, Vitest, System Design. The product goal is clear: A multi-tenant SaaS platform for coaching institutes to manage academic structure, students, teachers, attendance, payments, expenses, biometric devices, and SMS outreach. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Next.js App Router combines server-rendered reads with interactive client workflows, Prisma models PostgreSQL tenancy and audit state, Supabase provides database and identity infrastructure, and adapter boundaries isolate biometric devices and SMS providers. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-coaching-management","case-study:coaching-management:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "true\nA multi-tenant SaaS platform for coaching institutes to manage academic structure, students, teachers, attendance, payments, expenses, biometric devices, and SMS outreach.\n\nfalse\n2025-09-22T17:15:02Z\ne4017ceaf2450524e42db8f32ac094e30c71ad3d\n2025-09-22T17:29:41Z\ntrue\nproject-coaching-management\nnull\n\n\nNext.js\nReact 19\nTypeScript\nPostgreSQL\nSupabase\nPrisma\nTanStack Query\nshadcn/ui\nTailwind CSS\nZod\nVitest\nSystem Design\nCoaching","Next.js server and client components, shadcn/ui, TanStack Table, and forms support optimistic operational workflows.\nApplication interface\nThe domain models organizations, roles, levels, subjects, class sections, enrollments, attendance, fees, payments, expenses, and audit records.\nAcademic and finance domain\nPrisma over PostgreSQL provides indexed tenant queries and soft-delete history, with Supabase-backed authentication.\nPersistence and identity\nVendor adapters normalize biometric devices while encrypted"
  - id: "project:project-clm-api:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's CLM API project?
    text: "Montasim built CLM API, an API project. This client work was part of his Freelance Developer engagement with WaTheta. A multi-tenant customer lifecycle management API. Freelance work for WaTheta focused on WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram messaging, conversation automation and routing, real-time inbox events, and integration tests. His role covered freelance developer, with scope spanning messaging services and integrations, persistence, real-time conversation events, and automated tests. Repository history contains 47 commits authored by Montasim between 28 May and 10 June 2026. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-clm-api","experience:experience-watheta-freelance-developer","case-study:clm-api:problem","case-study:clm-api:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "true\nA multi-tenant customer lifecycle management API. Freelance work for WaTheta focused on WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram messaging, conversation automation and routing, real-time inbox events, and integration tests.\nexperience-watheta-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2025-11-16T05:39:18Z\n36f2ec46eca854df2c594e4cd5c9380be2e72cf9\n2026-06-21T04:24:27Z\ntrue\nproject-clm-api\nnull\nMeta Graph API\nWhatsApp Cloud API\nMessenger API\nInstagram API\nMeta Conversions","independent\nWaTheta\nContributed to the **CLM API messaging backend** with **WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram integrations**. Implemented inbound event handling and deduplication, **keyword-based automation**, and agent assignment by skill, department, and priority. Added **durable WhatsApp coexistence storage**, history synchronization, quoted replies, and **conversation SLA timers**. Implemented presence and typing events with **conversation access checks**, and added **unit and end-to-end test coverage**. The","Support teams need to receive and respond to conversations across WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram, route them to suitable agents, and preserve conversation state across asynchronous events and service restarts.\nWork took place in an existing multi-tenant backend; the full platform is shared team work, not an individual implementation.\nExternal messaging providers have distinct event formats, registration flows, media lifetimes, and channel restrictions.\nThe 28 May–10 June 2026 contribution window comes from 47","Repository history contains 47 commits authored by Montasim between 28 May and 10 June 2026.\nMessaging workflows gained persistent coexistence state, event deduplication, and explicit conversation-routing fallbacks.\nAutomated test sources cover provider event handling, real-time access checks, routing, and messaging integration paths; no production performance claim is inferred from those tests."
  - id: "project:project-clm-api:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for CLM API?
    text: "Montasim built CLM API with Meta Graph API, WhatsApp Cloud API, Messenger API, Instagram API, Meta Conversions API, TypeScript, NestJS, MongoDB, Mongoose, Redis, BullMQ, Socket.IO, Zod, Jest. The product goal is clear: A multi-tenant customer lifecycle management API. Freelance work for WaTheta focused on WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram messaging, conversation automation and routing, real-time inbox events, and integration tests. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: NestJS messaging modules separate application services, domain contracts, and MongoDB persistence. Queued webhook processing connects provider events to conversations, automation, and real-time updates. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-clm-api","case-study:clm-api:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "true\nA multi-tenant customer lifecycle management API. Freelance work for WaTheta focused on WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram messaging, conversation automation and routing, real-time inbox events, and integration tests.\nexperience-watheta-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2025-11-16T05:39:18Z\n36f2ec46eca854df2c594e4cd5c9380be2e72cf9\n2026-06-21T04:24:27Z\ntrue\nproject-clm-api\nnull\nMeta Graph API\nWhatsApp Cloud API\nMessenger API\nInstagram API\nMeta Conversions","Meta webhook handlers normalize inbound events for WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram, with event-specific deduplication and validation.\nProvider event processing\nApplication services coordinate keyword automation, routing, quoted replies, agent assignment, and SLA timers.\nConversation workflows\nMongoDB repositories retain coexistence bindings and conversation state. Redis and Socket.IO support presence, typing, and conversation events with access checks.\nPersistence and real-time delivery\nNestJS messaging modules"
  - id: "project:project-mcq-topper-backend:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's MCQ Topper Backend project?
    text: "Montasim built MCQ Topper Backend, an API project. This client work was part of his Freelance Developer engagement with Infomax. An Express API foundation for an MCQ learning platform, with versioned domain routes, shared response handling, request middleware, and email error notifications. Domain operations remain scaffold implementations. His role covered backend API structure and shared infrastructure development, with scope spanning versioned routing, domain controller scaffolds, request middleware, response utilities, error notifications, and deployment configuration. The repository provides an inspectable API structure for extending MCQ learning workflows. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-mcq-topper-backend","experience:experience-infomax-freelance-developer","case-study:mcq-topper-backend:problem","case-study:mcq-topper-backend:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "true\nAn Express API foundation for an MCQ learning platform, with versioned domain routes, shared response handling, request middleware, and email error notifications. Domain operations remain scaffold implementations.\nexperience-infomax-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2024-03-03T14:51:20Z\n977f5de42ec89ba65a4f292441ef283cc237259a\n2024-01-28T06:08:31Z\ntrue\nproject-mcq-topper-backend\nnull\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nExpress.js\nREST APIs\nHelmet\nexpress-rate-limit\nNodemailer\nDocker\nPM2\nMCQ Topper","independent\nInfomax\nOrganized questions, exams, courses, enrolment, and results into **versioned Express modules** for an MCQ learning platform. Centralized **controller wrappers, response formatting, and error handling** to give new features a consistent API structure. Added Helmet security headers, request limits, timeouts, and rate limiting at the application boundary. Included **Nodemailer error notifications** and **Docker/PM2 deployment configuration**. The reviewed implementation establishes API","An MCQ learning platform spans questions, exams, courses, enrolment, and results. The backend needs a consistent place for each domain and common request handling before those learning workflows can be implemented end to end.\nMost domain controllers return placeholder messages rather than completed learning workflows.\nDatabase wiring is not active in the inspected request and server lifecycle; MongoDB-related files do not establish working persistence.\nAuthentication and authorization require route-level","The repository provides an inspectable API structure for extending MCQ learning workflows.\nCommon middleware and response helpers establish shared conventions across domain modules.\nThe inspected source demonstrates backend scaffolding, without evidence of completed exam delivery, persisted results, production adoption, or measured performance improvements."
  - id: "project:project-mcq-topper-backend:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for MCQ Topper Backend?
    text: "Montasim built MCQ Topper Backend with JavaScript, Node.js, Express.js, REST APIs, Helmet, express-rate-limit, Nodemailer, Docker, PM2. The product goal is clear: An Express API foundation for an MCQ learning platform, with versioned domain routes, shared response handling, request middleware, and email error notifications. Domain operations remain scaffold implementations. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: An Express application applies shared middleware before mounting a configurable API version prefix. Domain routers call controller scaffolds and common response utilities, while error handling attempts administrator email notification. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-mcq-topper-backend","case-study:mcq-topper-backend:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "true\nAn Express API foundation for an MCQ learning platform, with versioned domain routes, shared response handling, request middleware, and email error notifications. Domain operations remain scaffold implementations.\nexperience-infomax-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2024-03-03T14:51:20Z\n977f5de42ec89ba65a4f292441ef283cc237259a\n2024-01-28T06:08:31Z\ntrue\nproject-mcq-topper-backend\nnull\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nExpress.js\nREST APIs\nHelmet\nexpress-rate-limit\nNodemailer\nDocker\nPM2\nMCQ Topper","Helmet, HPP, CORS, JSON payload limits, timeouts, and rate limiting are configured in the application before routing.\nRequest boundary\nThe /api/{API_VERSION} router composes modules for questions, exams, courses, enrolment, results, and supporting content.\nVersioned domain routing\nDomain controller scaffolds use shared controller and response helpers. The inspected question controller returns a fixed message instead of creating a persisted question.\nControllers and responses\nNodemailer utilities support error"
  - id: "project:project-school-management-backend:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's School Management Backend project?
    text: "Montasim built School Management Backend, an API project. This client work was part of his Freelance Developer engagement with TalendIT. A modular Express and MongoDB prototype for school administration and website content, with admission resources, student records, academic downloads, and Google Drive file integration. His role covered backend API and school-content service development, with scope spanning domain routes and services, MongoDB access, validation, admission-file handling, and shared middleware. The inspected source includes database-backed service implementations rather than only route placeholders. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-school-management-backend","experience:experience-talendit-freelance-developer","case-study:school-management-backend:problem","case-study:school-management-backend:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "true\nA modular Express and MongoDB prototype for school administration and website content, with admission resources, student records, academic downloads, and Google Drive file integration.\nexperience-talendit-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2023-10-23T11:10:07Z\n46554def6f1ba0bcdf8dafe1be535edeb26e45e2\n2023-10-23T11:10:46Z\ntrue\nproject-school-management-backend\nnull\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nExpress.js\nMongoDB\nJoi\nGoogle Drive API\nSchool Management Backend\neducation\nschool-management\nrest-api\nfile-storage\napi","independent\nTalendIT\nBuilt **Next.js and React school pages** for admissions, notices, routines, results, staff, and galleries, with responsive navigation, Swiper galleries, and a Google Maps integration. The frontend uses local sample content and an in-memory admin demonstration. In the school backend, implemented **Google Drive file handling** and stored admission-document identifiers and links in MongoDB. Separated controllers, persistence services, and **Joi input validation**. Built **Mongoose survey CRUD","School websites need to organize admissions, notices, results, and downloadable documents alongside administrative records. These resources need consistent service boundaries and a way to associate stored files with database metadata.\nThe repository is a prototype. Its test command is a failing placeholder, and no automated suite is included.\nDatabase, email, and Google Drive operations require configured external services and were not executed during this review.\nAccess control and file handling need production","The inspected source includes database-backed service implementations rather than only route placeholders.\nThe prototype provides a reusable structure for school-site resources, although deployment behavior and operational reliability remain unverified.\nThe admission-document service code connects file upload results to school-content metadata."
  - id: "project:project-school-management-backend:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for School Management Backend?
    text: "Montasim built School Management Backend with JavaScript, Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB, Joi, Google Drive API. The product goal is clear: A modular Express and MongoDB prototype for school administration and website content, with admission resources, student records, academic downloads, and Google Drive file integration. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Express routes hand requests to domain controllers and services, which validate input with Joi and work with MongoDB collections through shared helpers. The admission-form service also uploads documents to Google Drive and records the resulting file identifiers and links in MongoDB. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
