    supportingExcerpts[4]: "true\nA legacy React inventory client with Firebase sign-in, item browsing, stock update and delivery controls, and user-specific item pages. Data workflows depend on a separate POSDash API.\nexperience-ndevers-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2022-04-29T16:39:44Z\n5533ac7ed486888f623f87681f057f602db19928\n2022-05-08T14:48:29Z\nfalse\nproject-warehouse-management-client\nnull\nJavaScript\nReact\nReact Router\nFirebase Authentication\nAxios\nTailwind CSS\nPOSDash Client\nlegacy-project\ninventory\nwebsite","independent\nnDevers\nBuilt **inventory browsing and item-management interfaces** with React, React Router, and Tailwind CSS. Integrated **Firebase Authentication**, shared sign-in helpers, and an email/token request flow, using **Axios** for stock updates, delivery actions, and user-specific item requests. POSDash depends on its own companion server; it is not the client for the separately documented inventory API. In that **Express and MongoDB backend**, implemented product reads, creates, updates, and deletes,","An inventory interface has to connect sign-in, item browsing, stock updates, and personal item lists. Each of those screens depends on browser state staying coordinated with a separate API.\nThe client embeds a legacy Heroku API address; current end-to-end operation was not verified.\nOnly some account-oriented routes use the client authentication guard, and backend authorization remains a separate responsibility.\nThis client references warehouse-management-server, not inventory-management-system-server.","The source demonstrates a browser-to-API inventory workflow.\nAccount and inventory screens share routing and identity helpers.\nThe project records early React integration work, while hosted workflow success and business impact remain unverified."
