    supportingExcerpts[4]: "true\nA legacy Express and MongoDB API organizing pharmacy and non-pharmacy products, orders, returns, customers, employees, and supplier records into resource routers.\nexperience-ndevers-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2022-07-20T15:29:19Z\na1e65bb3271ffe306f7af644fdcd1dc968fe1a3d\n2022-07-20T15:29:12Z\nfalse\nproject-inventory-management-system-server\nnull\nJavaScript\nNode.js\nExpress.js\nMongoDB\nInventory Management System Server\nlegacy-project\ninventory\napi","independent\nnDevers\nBuilt **inventory browsing and item-management interfaces** with React, React Router, and Tailwind CSS. Integrated **Firebase Authentication**, shared sign-in helpers, and an email/token request flow, using **Axios** for stock updates, delivery actions, and user-specific item requests. POSDash depends on its own companion server; it is not the client for the separately documented inventory API. In that **Express and MongoDB backend**, implemented product reads, creates, updates, and deletes,","Inventory administration involves more than product lists. Orders, returns, supplier records, and setup data each need their own resource paths and persistent records.\nThe source lacks authentication and authorization middleware for data-changing endpoints.\nPurchase paths mount order routers in the inspected entry point, so distinct purchase behavior is not established.\nLegacy connection configuration requires remediation, and no automated tests or working deployment were verified.","The source provides inspectable resource-level MongoDB CRUD code.\nThe API structure covers several related inventory record types.\nThe project demonstrates early backend implementation; security, transaction behavior, and deployment remain unverified."
  - id: "project:project-inventory-management-system-server:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for Inventory Management System Server?
    text: "Montasim built Inventory Management System Server with JavaScript, Node.js, Express.js, MongoDB. The product goal is clear: A legacy Express and MongoDB API organizing pharmacy and non-pharmacy products, orders, returns, customers, employees, and supplier records into resource routers. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: A single Express entry point applies JSON parsing and CORS, then mounts separate routers for pharmacy and non-pharmacy products, orders, setup, people, returns, and suppliers. Request handlers in the inspected product router call MongoDB collection methods directly. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
