  - id: "project:project-technofire:overview"
    category: project
    question: What is Montasim's TechnoFire project?
    text: "Montasim built TechnoFire, a website project. This client work was part of his Freelance Developer engagement with TalendIT. A legacy PHP and MariaDB fire-safety catalog prototype with database-backed product pages, galleries, client content, and a session-based administration area, co-developed with Abid Hasan Piash. His role covered collaborative PHP website development with Abid Hasan Piash, with scope spanning public catalog pages, reusable PHP includes, and administration interfaces; individual file ownership is not established. The repository contains public catalog pages and content-management interfaces. A hiring manager, developer, or client can use the linked case study to review the constraints, architecture, Montasim's contribution, and the result—not just the project summary."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-technofire","experience:experience-talendit-freelance-developer","case-study:technofire:problem","case-study:technofire:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "true\nA legacy PHP and MariaDB fire-safety catalog prototype with database-backed product pages, galleries, client content, and a session-based administration area, co-developed with Abid Hasan Piash.\nexperience-talendit-freelance-developer\nfalse\n2021-12-16T06:33:52Z\n937049ff5f8c896a2d5858e2d9d21bce5a74127b\n2021-12-16T06:33:45Z\nfalse\nproject-technofire\nnull\nPHP\nMariaDB\nMySQLi\nBootstrap\nJavaScript\nTechnoFire\nlegacy-project\nproduct-catalog\nwebsite","independent\nTalendIT\nBuilt **Next.js and React school pages** for admissions, notices, routines, results, staff, and galleries, with responsive navigation, Swiper galleries, and a Google Maps integration. The frontend uses local sample content and an in-memory admin demonstration. In the school backend, implemented **Google Drive file handling** and stored admission-document identifiers and links in MongoDB. Separated controllers, persistence services, and **Joi input validation**. Built **Mongoose survey CRUD","A business catalog needs product categories, detail pages, gallery images, and company content presented consistently. The business also needs an administration interface for maintaining those resources.\nThe repository documents legacy authentication, SQL handling, and upload weaknesses, so it is a reference prototype rather than a production-ready system.\nNo automated tests, verified deployment, or measured business outcomes were established.\nThe project credits both Montasim and Abid Hasan Piash; the inspected","The repository contains public catalog pages and content-management interfaces.\nShared includes and database-backed content demonstrate an early server-rendered website architecture.\nThe artifact documents collaborative PHP experience while keeping production readiness and individual feature ownership unclaimed."
  - id: "project:project-technofire:technology"
    category: project
    question: Which technologies did Montasim use for TechnoFire?
    text: "Montasim built TechnoFire with PHP, MariaDB, MySQLi, Bootstrap, JavaScript. The product goal is clear: A legacy PHP and MariaDB fire-safety catalog prototype with database-backed product pages, galleries, client content, and a session-based administration area, co-developed with Abid Hasan Piash. More importantly, the architecture is explicit: Server-rendered PHP pages assemble shared includes and read catalog and settings records from MariaDB through MySQLi. A session-based admin area works with the same database to manage products, categories, galleries, clients, and site settings. That gives technical reviewers a concrete system boundary to discuss instead of a list of framework keywords."
