  - id: "case-study:sigcraft:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Sigcraft case study?
    text: "Most email signatures look right in a design tool and break in Gmail. Classes, style blocks, SVG icons and percentage widths get stripped, images stop loading, and the layout changes from one inbox to the next. Montasim owned product designer and full-stack engineer, covering product design, email-safe HTML rendering, server-side icon rendering, editor experience, testing and deployment. The solution also had to respect “Gmail strips classes, style blocks and SVG, so every signature has to be built from tables with inline styles and fixed pixel widths”; “The photo and icons load from a public URL each time an email opens, so the image host must stay stable and cache-friendly”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:sigcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Most email signatures look right in a design tool and break in Gmail. Classes, style blocks, SVG icons and percentage widths get stripped, images stop loading, and the layout changes from one inbox to the next.\nGmail strips classes, style blocks and SVG, so every signature has to be built from tables with inline styles and fixed pixel widths.\nThe photo and icons load from a public URL each time an email opens, so the image host must stay stable and cache-friendly.\nMail apps' dark modes recolour text but never"
  - id: "case-study:sigcraft:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Sigcraft solution?
    text: "A TanStack Start app renders the editor and email templates, a pure builder turns editor state into email-safe HTML, and a server route draws icons as long-cached PNGs on Netlify. A representative decision was “Generate email markup, not web markup”: Signatures use tables, inline styles and fixed pixel widths with no classes or SVG, because that is the markup Gmail keeps intact. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:sigcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Four steps (Details, Images, Templates, Styles) drive a live preview inside a mail window, with the editor state kept in the browser's localStorage.\nEditor\nA pure function turns editor state into tables with inline styles only. The preview and the copy share that markup and differ only in where images load from.\nSignature builder\nA versioned /api/icon route draws each Hugeicons glyph as a PNG at four times display size without image libraries. Each URL fully describes its image, so Netlify's CDN caches it for a"
  - id: "case-study:sigcraft:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Sigcraft?
    text: "Montasim designed the four-step editor and 26 named signature layouts, from photo-left and centred to banner, card, minimal, monogram and business-card styles. He built the email-safe signature builder with rich HTML, raw HTML and plain-text copy, Gmail setup steps, and a character count against Gmail's 10,000-character limit. He wrote the PNG icon renderer and the versioned, cache-friendly icon API route. He built the email templates page for job search, client work and meetings, with field-driven forms and shared details across templates. He set up CI for formatting, linting, type checking, tests and builds, added security headers, and deployed the server-rendered app to Netlify. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Signatures paste into Gmail and render as previewed, at the same size in any window”; “Twenty-six signature layouts, nine colour presets or any custom hex, and eight email-safe fonts ship from one editor”; “Icons in any colour are served as year-cached PNGs, and older icon versions remain available for emails already sent”; “Thirty-one automated tests cover the signature builder, email fields and icon renderer, and CI runs format, lint, type check, test and build on every push”; “Known limits are documented: images depend on the live site, Outlook on Windows shows round images as squares, and a content security policy is still to come”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:sigcraft:contribution","case-study:sigcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed the four-step editor and 26 named signature layouts, from photo-left and centred to banner, card, minimal, monogram and business-card styles.\nBuilt the email-safe signature builder with rich HTML, raw HTML and plain-text copy, Gmail setup steps, and a character count against Gmail's 10,000-character limit.\nWrote the PNG icon renderer and the versioned, cache-friendly icon API route.\nBuilt the email templates page for job search, client work and meetings, with field-driven forms and shared details across","Signatures paste into Gmail and render as previewed, at the same size in any window.\nTwenty-six signature layouts, nine colour presets or any custom hex, and eight email-safe fonts ship from one editor.\nIcons in any colour are served as year-cached PNGs, and older icon versions remain available for emails already sent.\nThirty-one automated tests cover the signature builder, email fields and icon renderer, and CI runs format, lint, type check, test and build on every push.\nKnown limits are documented: images depend"
  - id: "case-study:giftbook:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Giftbook case study?
    text: "Bangladeshi families track wedding, aqiqah, and birthday gifts in paper notebooks that are hard to search, easy to lose, and impossible for several relatives to update at once, while generic apps store the family's financial records on a third-party server and ignore Bangla numerals and lakh formatting. Montasim owned product designer and frontend engineer, covering product specification, Bangla-first interface design, local-first data model, Google Sheets synchronization, sharing workflow, PWA packaging, testing, and Netlify deployment. The solution also had to respect “The ledger must live in the owner's Google Drive with no application server or database, so the app may request only the drive.file scope and never see the rest of the Drive”; “Gift entry happens at crowded events with unreliable connectivity, so writes must land locally first and reach the sheet later without duplicating rows”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:giftbook:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Bangladeshi families track wedding, aqiqah, and birthday gifts in paper notebooks that are hard to search, easy to lose, and impossible for several relatives to update at once, while generic apps store the family's financial records on a third-party server and ignore Bangla numerals and lakh formatting.\nThe ledger must live in the owner's Google Drive with no application server or database, so the app may request only the drive.file scope and never see the rest of the Drive.\nGift entry happens at crowded events"
  - id: "case-study:giftbook:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Giftbook solution?
    text: "A TanStack Start single-page PWA keeps a per-ledger Dexie database as the working copy, and a sync engine pulls, merges, and pushes rows against a three-tab Google Sheet whose columns are derived from Zod schemas. A representative decision was “Use the owner's Google Sheet as the only backend”: Avoiding a server means the family keeps ownership of its data, can open or back up the sheet directly, and the product carries no hosting cost or data-retention liability. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:giftbook:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Zod schemas for events, people, and gifts define both form validation and the exact column order of the sheet, with every record carrying updatedAt, updatedBy, and a soft-delete marker.\nLedger domain\nEach ledger gets its own Dexie IndexedDB database. A single repository module is the only write path, and live queries feed the pages so edits appear instantly across tabs.\nLocal persistence\nA pull, merge, save, push cycle runs after writes, on an interval, and when the device comes back online, resolving conflicts by"
  - id: "case-study:giftbook:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Giftbook?
    text: "Montasim specified the product from an approved HTML prototype and rebuilt it in TanStack Start with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui, and Hugeicons across mobile, tablet, and desktop layouts. He designed the Zod-driven ledger schema that doubles as the sheet column contract, the per-ledger Dexie store, the single repository write path, and live-query hooks. He implemented the sync engine with header verification, last-write-wins merge, duplicate-append protection, and nine Node.js test runner cases covering rows, merge, and the full cycle. He built Google Drive, Sheets, Picker, and Identity Services adapters plus a complete localStorage mock, QR and link sharing with member management, Excel export, and a print view. He wrote the Bangla copy, numeral, date, and lakh-grouped currency formatting, custom form validation, PWA manifest and offline shell, social preview assets, and deployed to Netlify with secrets from Infisical. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Families can record who gave what at weddings, aqiqahs, and birthdays from any phone, offline, and see per-event received, given, and unopened-envelope summaries”; “The ledger stays in the owner's Google Drive as an editable spreadsheet, with the app holding only drive.file access and no server-side copy”; “Multiple relatives write to the same ledger through a QR link, and conflicting edits resolve predictably without duplicate rows”; “The complete workflow, including sharing and sync failure simulations, runs in mock mode without Google credentials, and nine automated sync tests guard the merge and cycle logic”; “The app is live at giftbook.netlify.app as an installable PWA with a fully Bangla interface, including numerals and currency”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:giftbook:contribution","case-study:giftbook:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Specified the product from an approved HTML prototype and rebuilt it in TanStack Start with TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui, and Hugeicons across mobile, tablet, and desktop layouts.\nDesigned the Zod-driven ledger schema that doubles as the sheet column contract, the per-ledger Dexie store, the single repository write path, and live-query hooks.\nImplemented the sync engine with header verification, last-write-wins merge, duplicate-append protection, and nine Node.js test runner cases covering rows, merge,","Families can record who gave what at weddings, aqiqahs, and birthdays from any phone, offline, and see per-event received, given, and unopened-envelope summaries.\nThe ledger stays in the owner's Google Drive as an editable spreadsheet, with the app holding only drive.file access and no server-side copy.\nMultiple relatives write to the same ledger through a QR link, and conflicting edits resolve predictably without duplicate rows.\nThe complete workflow, including sharing and sync failure simulations, runs in mock"
  - id: "case-study:rootstory:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Rootstory case study?
    text: "Family history becomes difficult to trust when relationships are visually ambiguous, important life details are scattered across tools, and an export cannot recreate the archive that a family carefully assembled. Montasim owned product designer and frontend engineer, covering product strategy, relationship modeling, responsive interface design, browser persistence, data portability, testing, and deployment. The solution also had to respect “Parent, child, partner, former-partner, and sibling relationships must remain visually distinct without making the tree difficult to scan”; “Portraits, contact details, notes, and living-person records are sensitive, so the application stores its working archive locally and does not silently upload it”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:rootstory:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Family history becomes difficult to trust when relationships are visually ambiguous, important life details are scattered across tools, and an export cannot recreate the archive that a family carefully assembled.\nParent, child, partner, former-partner, and sibling relationships must remain visually distinct without making the tree difficult to scan.\nPortraits, contact details, notes, and living-person records are sensitive, so the application stores its working archive locally and does not silently upload it.\nA"
  - id: "case-study:rootstory:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Rootstory solution?
    text: "TanStack Start and React render a responsive archive workspace around a typed family domain. IndexedDB persistence, versioned recovery, and explicit import and export boundaries sit beneath that workspace. A representative decision was “Treat relationships as graph data”: Tree placement, generation labels, parent junctions, sibling grouping, and relationship views are derived from shared relationship records instead of separate presentation-only links. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:rootstory:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Typed people and relationship records drive generation levels, relatives, life events, tree ordering, and direct parent-child connectors from one shared graph.\nFamily domain\nIndexedDB stores the active archive and recent recoverable versions, with migration support for older browser-local records.\nLocal persistence\nTree, People, Timeline, Calendar, profile, settings, and print surfaces share one responsive application shell with focused scrolling regions.\nArchive workspace\nA native Rootstory archive preserves the"
  - id: "case-study:rootstory:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Rootstory?
    text: "Montasim designed and implemented the landing page, responsive application shell, family tree, profile inspector, People, Timeline, Calendar, settings, and print workflows. He modeled people and relationships so parent pairs, children, siblings, partners, former partners, and generation ordering resolve consistently across the product. He implemented local persistence, recoverable version history, complete backup restoration, GEDCOM interchange, and privacy-safe HTML, CSV, text, PDF, and iCalendar exports. He added validation at form and import boundaries plus automated coverage for search, dates, relationship direction, tree ordering, backup restoration, export privacy, and calendar recurrence. He prepared the repository for public GitHub collaboration and deployed the TanStack Start SSR build to Netlify. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Families can build and inspect a multi-generation tree with direct, consistently styled relationship connectors”; “The complete archive format restores profiles, relationships, portraits, private fields, sharing records, and display settings together”; “People, Timeline, Calendar, version history, and print views reuse the same validated archive rather than maintaining separate copies”; “Twenty-two automated domain and portability tests, formatting, linting, type checking, and production builds run in the public CI workflow”; “The application is publicly available while keeping each visitor's working family archive local to their browser”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
