    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed and implemented the landing page, responsive application shell, family tree, profile inspector, People, Timeline, Calendar, settings, and print workflows.\nModeled people and relationships so parent pairs, children, siblings, partners, former partners, and generation ordering resolve consistently across the product.\nImplemented local persistence, recoverable version history, complete backup restoration, GEDCOM interchange, and privacy-safe HTML, CSV, text, PDF, and iCalendar exports.\nAdded validation at","Families can build and inspect a multi-generation tree with direct, consistently styled relationship connectors.\nThe complete archive format restores profiles, relationships, portraits, private fields, sharing records, and display settings together.\nPeople, Timeline, Calendar, version history, and print views reuse the same validated archive rather than maintaining separate copies.\nTwenty-two automated domain and portability tests, formatting, linting, type checking, and production builds run in the public CI"
  - id: "case-study:liftuno:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Liftuno case study?
    text: "Local gyms often track members, payments, attendance, and outstanding balances through paper records or staff memory. That makes daily work slow and allows financial history, access boundaries, and membership state to become ambiguous. Montasim owned client product architecture and full-stack engineering, covering multi-tenant operations, billing rules, authentication, localization, and cross-platform delivery. The solution also had to respect “Membership accounting must distinguish expired coverage from actual debt instead of inventing fees when a member stops attending”; “Platform operators, gym administrators, staff, and members require different permissions with strict organization isolation”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:liftuno:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Local gyms often track members, payments, attendance, and outstanding balances through paper records or staff memory. That makes daily work slow and allows financial history, access boundaries, and membership state to become ambiguous.\nMembership accounting must distinguish expired coverage from actual debt instead of inventing fees when a member stops attending.\nPlatform operators, gym administrators, staff, and members require different permissions with strict organization isolation.\nThe product must support"
  - id: "case-study:liftuno:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Liftuno solution?
    text: "Expo Router and React Native provide a shared responsive interface, Convex owns realtime gym-scoped data and transactional commands, Better Auth manages sessions, and explicit domain services preserve billing and membership invariants. A representative decision was “Model prepaid coverage instead of automatic monthly debt”: Coverage expires without creating a new charge, and a recorded return creates the next payable period, preserving the difference between inactivity and money owed. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:liftuno:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Expo and React Native Web provide role-aware navigation, bilingual content, and touch-friendly operational screens.\nCross-platform interface\nPure billing and workspace rules model prepaid coverage, partial payments, offers, returns, reversals, and attendance.\nMembership domain\nConvex functions resolve the authenticated user, gym grant, role, and requested organization for every protected operation.\nSecure data boundary\nBetter Auth sessions, hashed invitations, and persistent sign-in budgets protect access, while"
  - id: "case-study:liftuno:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Liftuno?
    text: "Montasim designed the gym, member, access, invitation, attendance, offer, payment, and audit domain boundaries. He implemented bilingual, role-aware operational flows for dashboards, member records, attendance, billing, receipts, settings, and administration. He built the Better Auth and Convex authorization path, invitation lifecycle, account-attempt budget, and gym-scoped backend commands. He established domain, authentication, and backend integration tests around the highest-risk financial and access rules. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Gym staff can replace fragmented paper tracking with one member, payment, and attendance ledger”; “Prepaid coverage, outstanding balances, partial payments, offers, and reversals retain explicit financial meaning”; “Organization and role boundaries are enforced by the backend rather than inferred from navigation state”; “The same product foundation supports responsive web use now and Android validation as a separate release step”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:liftuno:contribution","case-study:liftuno:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed the gym, member, access, invitation, attendance, offer, payment, and audit domain boundaries.\nImplemented bilingual, role-aware operational flows for dashboards, member records, attendance, billing, receipts, settings, and administration.\nBuilt the Better Auth and Convex authorization path, invitation lifecycle, account-attempt budget, and gym-scoped backend commands.\nEstablished domain, authentication, and backend integration tests around the highest-risk financial and access rules.","Gym staff can replace fragmented paper tracking with one member, payment, and attendance ledger.\nPrepaid coverage, outstanding balances, partial payments, offers, and reversals retain explicit financial meaning.\nOrganization and role boundaries are enforced by the backend rather than inferred from navigation state.\nThe same product foundation supports responsive web use now and Android validation as a separate release step."
  - id: "case-study:air-traffic-control:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Air Traffic Control case study?
    text: "A flight-path drawing game needs collision and landing rules that agree with what players see, while keeping progress meaningful across short sessions and different screen layouts. Montasim owned game design and TypeScript engineering, covering traffic simulation, pointer routing, local progression, browser delivery, and Windows and Linux desktop distribution. The solution also had to respect “Flight paths require mouse, touch, or pen input; keyboard navigation alone does not cover gameplay”; “Browser and desktop saves are separate and local, with no cloud sync or save export”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:air-traffic-control:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A flight-path drawing game needs collision and landing rules that agree with what players see, while keeping progress meaningful across short sessions and different screen layouts.\nFlight paths require mouse, touch, or pen input; keyboard navigation alone does not cover gameplay.\nBrowser and desktop saves are separate and local, with no cloud sync or save export.\nBrowser offline play requires an initial online visit and successful asset caching.\nResizing or changing orientation can invalidate an active airfield"
  - id: "case-study:air-traffic-control:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Air Traffic Control solution?
    text: "A framework-independent TypeScript simulation feeds Phaser rendering and pointer interactions. DOM menus coordinate sessions, IndexedDB persists local progression, and Vite builds browser and Electron delivery targets. A representative decision was “Keep simulation independent from rendering”: A fixed-step simulation and seeded traffic make gameplay rules testable without a browser renderer. The fixed step is an update interval, not a measured frame-rate guarantee. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:air-traffic-control:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Fixed-step updates at 1/60 second, seeded random traffic, flight-path geometry, and aircraft-shaped collision polygons live outside Phaser.\nSimulation\nPhaser scenes render airfields and aircraft, while pointer routes guide aircraft to matching landing zones. DOM menus expose difficulty, career, achievements, and settings.\nInteraction and rendering\nIndexedDB stores versioned saves, career progression, achievements, and records. Completed shifts contribute progress; practice and abandoned shifts do not.\nLocal"
  - id: "case-study:air-traffic-control:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Air Traffic Control?
    text: "Montasim implemented aircraft routing, traffic pacing, landing checks, and collision geometry. He built nine airfields, three difficulty levels, seven career ranks, and nine achievements. He integrated IndexedDB persistence, offline browser assets, and desktop distribution for Windows and Linux. He added automated coverage for simulation, progression, storage, audio, and UI behavior. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Players can draw routes for three aircraft types on Android, in the browser, or in Windows and Linux desktop apps. The Android release features twelve airfields; the browser and desktop versions documented here feature nine”; “Local career progression and achievements persist between completed sessions without requiring an account”; “The simulation can be tested independently of Phaser rendering”; “The product provides a Google Play installation path for Android, a browser entry point, a Microsoft Store installation path for Windows, and a Snap Store installation path for Linux”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:air-traffic-control:contribution","case-study:air-traffic-control:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Implemented aircraft routing, traffic pacing, landing checks, and collision geometry.\nBuilt nine airfields, three difficulty levels, seven career ranks, and nine achievements.\nIntegrated IndexedDB persistence, offline browser assets, and desktop distribution for Windows and Linux.\nAdded automated coverage for simulation, progression, storage, audio, and UI behavior.","Players can draw routes for three aircraft types on Android, in the browser, or in Windows and Linux desktop apps. The Android release features twelve airfields; the browser and desktop versions documented here feature nine.\nLocal career progression and achievements persist between completed sessions without requiring an account.\nThe simulation can be tested independently of Phaser rendering.\nThe product provides a Google Play installation path for Android, a browser entry point, a Microsoft Store installation"
  - id: "case-study:build-android-app-from-web:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the build-android-app-from-web case study?
    text: "Teams turning an existing web product into an Android app can silently drop routes, states, integrations, and recovery paths. This happens when implementation starts before the product's capabilities have been inventoried. Without that inventory, an app can look like the website and still be missing workflows. Montasim owned agent workflow design and automation engineering, covering mobile development, deterministic validation, and portable distribution. The solution also had to respect “Every web capability needs an explicit Android disposition and evidence trail”; “Platform adaptation must preserve product behavior without forcing browser interaction patterns onto Android”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:build-android-app-from-web:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Teams turning an existing web product into an Android app can silently drop routes, states, integrations, and recovery paths. This happens when implementation starts before the product's capabilities have been inventoried. Without that inventory, an app can look like the website and still be missing workflows.\nEvery web capability needs an explicit Android disposition and evidence trail.\nPlatform adaptation must preserve product behavior without forcing browser interaction patterns onto Android.\nDeferred and"
  - id: "case-study:build-android-app-from-web:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the build-android-app-from-web solution?
    text: "The skill first inventories the web product's capabilities from repository evidence, then records each one in a parity manifest with an explicit disposition and its dependencies. Android adaptation guidance shapes the implementation, and deterministic validation checks that the manifest is closed before parity is claimed. A representative decision was “Inventory before implementation”: Establishing the complete product surface first means Android architecture and UI patterns are chosen against known requirements rather than discovered midway through the build. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:build-android-app-from-web:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "This layer inventories routes, workflows, data, states, and integrations from repository evidence.\nDiscovery\nEach capability is mapped to an Android implementation, adaptation, deferral, or justified exclusion.\nTraceability\nValidation checks dependencies, provenance, and closure before any parity claim is made.\nVerification\nThe skill first inventories the web product's capabilities from repository evidence, then records each one in a parity manifest with an explicit disposition and its dependencies. Android"
  - id: "case-study:build-android-app-from-web:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with build-android-app-from-web?
    text: Montasim designed an evidence-backed workflow for discovering web capabilities. He defined the parity manifest and its dependency model. He implemented deterministic manifest validation. He documented Android-specific adaptation and verification gates. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Agents can expose parity gaps before implementation begins”; “Every capability receives a reviewable Android disposition”; “Deferred work stays explicit and carries a justification”; “Parity claims rest on evidence rather than visual similarity alone”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed.
    factIds[2]: "case-study:build-android-app-from-web:contribution","case-study:build-android-app-from-web:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed an evidence-backed workflow for discovering web capabilities.\nDefined the parity manifest and its dependency model.\nImplemented deterministic manifest validation.\nDocumented Android-specific adaptation and verification gates.","Agents can expose parity gaps before implementation begins.\nEvery capability receives a reviewable Android disposition.\nDeferred work stays explicit and carries a justification.\nParity claims rest on evidence rather than visual similarity alone."
  - id: "case-study:skillfoliox:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog case study?
    text: "Reusable SKILL.md capabilities are scattered across repositories. Developers have no consistent way to compare them by purpose, host compatibility, maturity, or install path, so choosing a skill means reading many repositories by hand. Montasim owned product designer and frontend engineer, covering catalog design, content model, documentation rendering, and deployment. The solution also had to respect “Catalog facts can drift from the source repositories and package releases they describe”; “Fetching README files from GitHub can fail or be rate-limited”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:skillfoliox:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Reusable SKILL.md capabilities are scattered across repositories. Developers have no consistent way to compare them by purpose, host compatibility, maturity, or install path, so choosing a skill means reading many repositories by hand.\nCatalog facts can drift from the source repositories and package releases they describe.\nFetching README files from GitHub can fail or be rate-limited.\nMarkdown from many authors must render safely and consistently without implying that every skill has identical support."
  - id: "case-study:skillfoliox:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog solution?
    text: "TanStack Start routes and a React interface present a typed local catalog with search and filters. Detail pages fetch and render each skill's README from GitHub with retry and source fallbacks, and build-time metadata, a sitemap, and Netlify deployment complete the delivery path. A representative decision was “Keep discovery metadata in a curated local catalog”: A local catalog gives predictable builds, and the cost is explicit synchronization work whenever a source repository changes. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:skillfoliox:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "TanStack Start routes and React components render the catalog, cards, summaries, and detail pages.\nCatalog interface\nA typed local catalog drives search and filtering by status, compatibility, version, and category.\nCatalog model\nEach detail page fetches and renders the skill's README from GitHub, retrying and falling back to the source link when a fetch fails.\nDocumentation pipeline\nBuild-time metadata, a generated sitemap, and Netlify configuration publish the site.\nDistribution\nTanStack Start routes and a React"
  - id: "case-study:skillfoliox:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Skillfoliox - Agent Skill Catalog?
    text: "Montasim designed the catalog schema, filters, summaries, cards, and detail routes. He implemented remote README rendering and failure fallbacks. He documented the workflow for adding a skill and the validation it must pass. He added tests, sitemap generation, a responsive interface, and Netlify configuration. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Visitors can browse and compare skills in one place”; “Visitors can open rendered documentation and each skill's canonical repository”; “Each entry shows its compatibility, version, status, and categories”; “Maintainers can add catalog entries through a documented, testable workflow”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:skillfoliox:contribution","case-study:skillfoliox:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed the catalog schema, filters, summaries, cards, and detail routes.\nImplemented remote README rendering and failure fallbacks.\nDocumented the workflow for adding a skill and the validation it must pass.\nAdded tests, sitemap generation, a responsive interface, and Netlify configuration.","Visitors can browse and compare skills in one place.\nVisitors can open rendered documentation and each skill's canonical repository.\nEach entry shows its compatibility, version, status, and categories.\nMaintainers can add catalog entries through a documented, testable workflow."
  - id: "case-study:write-complete-project-readme:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the write-complete-project-readme case study?
    text: "README generators often preserve stale claims or produce generic sections because they never inspect the actual project. They also fail to separate evidence from inference, so polished prose ends up describing features and commands that do not exist. Montasim owned agent workflow designer and automation engineer, covering agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. The solution also had to respect “The skill may edit only the root README”; “Features, commands, compatibility, status, URLs, metrics, and license terms cannot be invented”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:write-complete-project-readme:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "README generators often preserve stale claims or produce generic sections because they never inspect the actual project. They also fail to separate evidence from inference, so polished prose ends up describing features and commands that do not exist.\nThe skill may edit only the root README.\nFeatures, commands, compatibility, status, URLs, metrics, and license terms cannot be invented.\nApplications, packages, APIs, CLIs, monorepos, datasets, and skills each require a different reading flow."
  - id: "case-study:write-complete-project-readme:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the write-complete-project-readme solution?
    text: "The skill first builds an inventory of repository evidence, then follows project-type and quality references to write the README. A deterministic checker validates the result, and an npm installer with tests delivers the skill to each host. A representative decision was “Treat the old README as evidence to verify”: An existing README often contains stale claims, so it is checked against the code, manifests, and configuration instead of being preserved as a template. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:write-complete-project-readme:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "The agent inventories repository evidence from code, manifests, and configuration before writing.\nSkill workflow\nProject-type and quality references route the authoring workflow, so a library and an application get different sections.\nAutomation\nA deterministic README checker validates the finished document, and an npm installer with tests packages the skill.\nVerification\nThe skill first builds an inventory of repository evidence, then follows project-type and quality references to write the README. A"
  - id: "case-study:write-complete-project-readme:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with write-complete-project-readme?
    text: "Montasim defined the evidence priority and routing by project type. He bundled comprehensive quality and benchmark references. He implemented structural and content checks. He added installer, release-workflow, compatibility, and package tests. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Agents can produce audience-first setup and usage documentation”; “Verified voice and attribution are preserved while stale claims are removed”; “Material absences, such as a missing license, are surfaced instead of hidden”; “The finished README is validated for completeness before the work is done”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:write-complete-project-readme:contribution","case-study:write-complete-project-readme:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Defined the evidence priority and routing by project type.\nBundled comprehensive quality and benchmark references.\nImplemented structural and content checks.\nAdded installer, release-workflow, compatibility, and package tests.","Agents can produce audience-first setup and usage documentation.\nVerified voice and attribution are preserved while stale claims are removed.\nMaterial absences, such as a missing license, are surfaced instead of hidden.\nThe finished README is validated for completeness before the work is done."
  - id: "case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the fix-project-metadata-mismatches case study?
    text: "A project's name, version, links, and compatibility claims are repeated across manifests, lockfiles, documentation, deployment configuration, and catalogs, and those copies drift apart. Users then follow broken links, run the wrong install command, or read conflicting compatibility claims. Finding and reconciling every copy by hand is slow and easy to get wrong. Montasim owned agent workflow design and automation engineering, covering agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. The solution also had to respect “Each metadata field has its own authoritative source, so no single file can be treated as the truth for everything”; “A human-facing description must match what the project actually does, not just what the manifest says”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A project's name, version, links, and compatibility claims are repeated across manifests, lockfiles, documentation, deployment configuration, and catalogs, and those copies drift apart. Users then follow broken links, run the wrong install command, or read conflicting compatibility claims. Finding and reconciling every copy by hand is slow and easy to get wrong.\nEach metadata field has its own authoritative source, so no single file can be treated as the truth for everything.\nA human-facing description must match"
  - id: "case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the fix-project-metadata-mismatches solution?
    text: "A source precedence model names the authoritative file for each field, and a deterministic auditor compares every copy against that authority. After any reconciliation, the repository's own validation runs, and the skill itself is distributed through npm. A representative decision was “Define source authority field by field”: Names, versions, and URLs each have a different natural owner, so one universal source of truth would overwrite correct values with wrong ones. The cost is a more detailed rule set. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "An evidence and source precedence model decides which file is authoritative for each metadata field.\nSkill workflow\nA deterministic auditor detects drift between copies and drives a scoped reconciliation workflow.\nAutomation\nRepository-native validation checks downstream consumers after edits, and npm distribution packages the skill for installation.\nVerification\nA source precedence model names the authoritative file for each field, and a deterministic auditor compares every copy against that authority. After any"
  - id: "case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with fix-project-metadata-mismatches?
    text: "Montasim encoded the canonical-source rules for each metadata field. He built metadata drift detection. He designed scoped local synchronization and validation reporting. He added safe installation and package tests. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Agents can identify conflicting names, versions, and URLs across a project”; “Agents repair only the local copies they are authorized to change”; “Unknown values are preserved for manual resolution instead of being overwritten”; “Remaining remote actions are reported separately from completed local work”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:contribution","case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Encoded the canonical-source rules for each metadata field.\nBuilt metadata drift detection.\nDesigned scoped local synchronization and validation reporting.\nAdded safe installation and package tests.","Agents can identify conflicting names, versions, and URLs across a project.\nAgents repair only the local copies they are authorized to change.\nUnknown values are preserved for manual resolution instead of being overwritten.\nRemaining remote actions are reported separately from completed local work."
  - id: "case-study:release-agent-skill:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the release-agent-skill case study?
    text: "Releasing a skill spans mutable local state and several independent remote systems. That makes partial success, version drift, and accidental publication easy, and an agent told to \"ship\" can easily do more than the maintainer intended. Montasim owned agent workflow designer and automation engineer, covering agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. The solution also had to respect “Publishing, pushing, creating releases, deploying, and changing the catalog each need separate authorization”; “Codex and Claude Code require different install destinations but must share one portable payload”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:release-agent-skill:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Releasing a skill spans mutable local state and several independent remote systems. That makes partial success, version drift, and accidental publication easy, and an agent told to \"ship\" can easily do more than the maintainer intended.\nPublishing, pushing, creating releases, deploying, and changing the catalog each need separate authorization.\nCodex and Claude Code require different install destinations but must share one portable payload.\nAn immutable artifact may already exist when a later stage fails, so the"
  - id: "case-study:release-agent-skill:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the release-agent-skill solution?
    text: "A SKILL.md file guides the agent through each release stage, backed by deterministic preflight and release-gate references. An npm installer and package contract, verified by consumer-side tests, deliver the skill to both hosts. A representative decision was “Model release stages separately”: Treating \"ship\" as blanket permission risks publishing or pushing without consent, so each stage is authorized on its own. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:release-agent-skill:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "SKILL.md orchestrates the release stages the agent follows and the authorization each one needs.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic references define the preflight checks and release gates that must pass before publication.\nAutomation\nAn npm installer, a package contract, and consumer-side tests confirm what users actually receive.\nVerification\nA SKILL.md file guides the agent through each release stage, backed by deterministic preflight and release-gate references. An npm installer and package contract, verified by"
  - id: "case-study:release-agent-skill:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with release-agent-skill?
    text: "Montasim defined an authorization-aware release inventory and workflow. He built deterministic preflight checks. He implemented safe installation for both hosts with path, symlink, and rollback protections. He tested dry runs, replacement, package contents, and consumer contracts. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Agents can prepare a release without publishing anything”; “Agents execute only the stages the maintainer has authorized”; “Partial remote success is reported as partial rather than hidden”; “The tag, GitHub release, npm package, install command, and catalog state are verified from the consumer side”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:release-agent-skill:contribution","case-study:release-agent-skill:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Defined an authorization-aware release inventory and workflow.\nBuilt deterministic preflight checks.\nImplemented safe installation for both hosts with path, symlink, and rollback protections.\nTested dry runs, replacement, package contents, and consumer contracts.","Agents can prepare a release without publishing anything.\nAgents execute only the stages the maintainer has authorized.\nPartial remote success is reported as partial rather than hidden.\nThe tag, GitHub release, npm package, install command, and catalog state are verified from the consumer side."
  - id: "case-study:verify-github-npm-release:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the verify-github-npm-release case study?
    text: "A release can look complete in one system while its tag, package version, CI run, install instructions, or catalog entry points somewhere else. Each surface looks fine on its own, so the disagreement is easy to miss until a user installs the wrong version. Montasim owned agent workflow designer and automation engineer, covering agent workflow, validation automation, and packaging. The solution also had to respect “The audit must never change or repair release state”; “Authentication and network errors cannot be interpreted as a missing artifact”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:verify-github-npm-release:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A release can look complete in one system while its tag, package version, CI run, install instructions, or catalog entry points somewhere else. Each surface looks fine on its own, so the disagreement is easy to miss until a user installs the wrong version.\nThe audit must never change or repair release state.\nAuthentication and network errors cannot be interpreted as a missing artifact.\nMutable aliases such as latest are weaker proof than an exact package@version."
  - id: "case-study:verify-github-npm-release:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the verify-github-npm-release solution?
