    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Visual inconsistencies are often reported from a single screenshot, with no record of the route, build, or state it came from and nothing to compare it against. Without responsive states or a systemic root cause, a fix tends to patch one page while the underlying drift stays in place.\nA screenshot only counts as evidence when it is tied to a route, viewport, time or build, and state.\nResponsive and accessibility behavior can only be judged across multiple widths and interaction states.\nA request for an audit does"
  - id: "case-study:find-ui-inconsistencies:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the find-ui-inconsistencies solution?
    text: "An agent workflow runs the audit and, when asked, the repair. A consistency reference defines what counts as evidence, how findings are ranked by severity, the order in which dependencies are checked, and what verification is expected, while packaging installs the skill on either host. A representative decision was “Compare equivalent elements side by side”: Placing matching elements next to each other confirms that a difference is real before it becomes a general rule, so one odd screenshot cannot drive a sweeping change. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:find-ui-inconsistencies:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "The agent workflow reviews typography, spacing, color, layout, shared UI, responsive behavior, skeletons, motion, and visible accessibility states, and repairs only on request.\nSkill workflow\nA consistency reference defines evidence, severity, dependency order, and verification expectations, and packaging supplies portable installation for each host.\nAutomation\nAn agent workflow runs the audit and, when asked, the repair. A consistency reference defines what counts as evidence, how findings are ranked by severity,"
  - id: "case-study:find-ui-inconsistencies:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with find-ui-inconsistencies?
    text: "Montasim defined screenshot provenance rules and viewport and state coverage. He codified severity levels and the fields every finding must include. He designed dependency-ordered audit and repair sequencing. He packaged cross-host installation and contract tests. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Agents can produce UI findings that someone else can reproduce”; “Findings distinguish observed defects from inferred causes”; “Agents propose the smallest systemic fix rather than page-level patches”; “When implementation is requested, agents recheck affected viewports, states, motion, focus, tests, and builds”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
