    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Defined screenshot provenance rules and viewport and state coverage.\nCodified severity levels and the fields every finding must include.\nDesigned dependency-ordered audit and repair sequencing.\nPackaged cross-host installation and contract tests.","Agents can produce UI findings that someone else can reproduce.\nFindings distinguish observed defects from inferred causes.\nAgents propose the smallest systemic fix rather than page-level patches.\nWhen implementation is requested, agents recheck affected viewports, states, motion, focus, tests, and builds."
  - id: "case-study:formflow:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the FormFlow case study?
    text: "Institutions often manage operational requests across chat, spreadsheets, fixed forms, and verbal follow-up, separating submitted information from ownership, current status, and accountability. Montasim owned product design and full-stack engineering, covering multi-tenant authorization, configurable workflows, and bilingual service delivery. The solution also had to respect “English and Bengali must be complete interfaces, with locale-aware content and Bengali or Latin numeral handling where appropriate”; “Google identity, platform authority, and institution membership are separate security boundaries, so every protected read and mutation must be authorized on the server”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:formflow:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Institutions often manage operational requests across chat, spreadsheets, fixed forms, and verbal follow-up, separating submitted information from ownership, current status, and accountability.\nEnglish and Bengali must be complete interfaces, with locale-aware content and Bengali or Latin numeral handling where appropriate.\nGoogle identity, platform authority, and institution membership are separate security boundaries, so every protected read and mutation must be authorized on the server.\nHistorical submissions"
  - id: "case-study:formflow:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the FormFlow solution?
    text: "TanStack Start routes and server functions deliver role-aware React workspaces, while framework-independent domain modules own forms, workflows, status transitions, and authorization. Drizzle repositories scope Neon PostgreSQL records by institution and actor, and Better Auth, Google OAuth, Resend, Netlify Functions, and GitHub Actions form the identity, communication, deployment, and quality boundaries. A representative decision was “Separate identity from institution access”: Google sign-in establishes identity, while platform authority and institution membership independently determine what a person can see and do. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:formflow:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Locale-aware TanStack Start routes and React workspaces serve platform administrators, institution managers, requesters, and employees.\nRole-aware interface\nFramework-independent modules own form validation, workflow versioning, ordering, permissions, assignments, and status transitions.\nDomain rules\nDrizzle repositories over Neon PostgreSQL store immutable form and workflow versions, notifications, work logs, and audit events.\nInstitution-scoped data\nBetter Auth with Google OAuth handles sign-in, Resend sends"
  - id: "case-study:formflow:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with FormFlow?
    text: "Montasim designed role-specific journeys for Super Admins, Admins, Managers, Requesters, and Employees across English and Bengali interfaces. He built dynamic form creation, immutable publishing, configurable workflows, submission tracking, assignment, work updates, notifications, invitations, profiles, and audit views. He modeled tenant boundaries, delegated Manager capabilities, versioned records, and role-specific data access across domain modules, repositories, and database migrations. He established automated domain, application, authorization, route, and presentation checks with GitHub Actions and a Netlify SSR deployment. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Institutions can define bilingual forms and operational workflows without changing application source code”; “Requesters can submit a published form, receive a tracking number, and follow requester-visible progress from one record”; “Admins and delegated Managers can review queues, assign Employees, manage people, and inspect accountable history within their institution”; “Employees can accept assigned work, record progress and evidence, and complete or return the assignment through explicit workflow states”; “Published versions, work logs, notifications, decisions, and audit events preserve a traceable history as the process evolves”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:formflow:contribution","case-study:formflow:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed role-specific journeys for Super Admins, Admins, Managers, Requesters, and Employees across English and Bengali interfaces.\nBuilt dynamic form creation, immutable publishing, configurable workflows, submission tracking, assignment, work updates, notifications, invitations, profiles, and audit views.\nModeled tenant boundaries, delegated Manager capabilities, versioned records, and role-specific data access across domain modules, repositories, and database migrations.\nEstablished automated domain,","Institutions can define bilingual forms and operational workflows without changing application source code.\nRequesters can submit a published form, receive a tracking number, and follow requester-visible progress from one record.\nAdmins and delegated Managers can review queues, assign Employees, manage people, and inspect accountable history within their institution.\nEmployees can accept assigned work, record progress and evidence, and complete or return the assignment through explicit workflow states.\nPublished"
  - id: "case-study:1snap:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the 1Snap case study?
    text: "Chrome's standard screenshot flow captures only the visible viewport, while many full-page tools add heavy editors, accounts, or cloud upload. 1Snap had to capture and mark one complete image without introducing a remote processing boundary. Montasim owned product designer and browser-extension engineer, covering capture lifecycle, page stabilization, local image processing, annotation tooling, and release delivery. The solution also had to respect “Chrome can capture only the active visible tab, so the source tab must stay active while 1Snap scrolls and records each frame”; “Dynamic content, visible images and fonts, fixed or sticky elements, and the user's original scroll position must be stabilized or restored without leaving the page altered”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:1snap:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Chrome's standard screenshot flow captures only the visible viewport, while many full-page tools add heavy editors, accounts, or cloud upload. 1Snap had to capture and mark one complete image without introducing a remote processing boundary.\nChrome can capture only the active visible tab, so the source tab must stay active while 1Snap scrolls and records each frame.\nDynamic content, visible images and fonts, fixed or sticky elements, and the user's original scroll position must be stabilized or restored without"
  - id: "case-study:1snap:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the 1Snap solution?
    text: "A toolbar-triggered WXT background controller coordinates the active tab, while an injected page script measures, stabilizes, scrolls, and restores the document. Frames and annotations live in extension-owned IndexedDB, and local Canvas stitching feeds a React result page with an SVG editing overlay that composes marks into the PNG at original resolution. A representative decision was “Keep capture and stitching inside Chrome”: Storing recent frames and assembling the PNG locally avoids uploading page captures, which removes any need for an account or a remote image-processing boundary. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:1snap:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "The background controller owns the active-tab lifecycle, progress badge, capture rate, cancellation, and recovery.\nCapture orchestration\nAn injected page script measures the document, waits for visible assets, pauses motion, manages sticky elements, and restores the original presentation.\nPage preparation\nCaptured frames and vector annotation documents are retained separately in IndexedDB, and the browser Canvas API stitches frames into a bounded PNG.\nLocal processing\nA React result page reports capture"
  - id: "case-study:1snap:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with 1Snap?
    text: "Montasim designed the one-click capture, progress, result, annotation, copy, download, and recovery journeys. He implemented document measurement, scroll planning, visible-asset waiting, animation pausing, sticky-element handling, and state restoration. He built IndexedDB frame and annotation retention, local Canvas stitching, SVG editing and history controls, output sizing safeguards, and original-resolution annotated export. He established unit and UI coverage, the public product website, Netlify deployment, and the checksummed Manifest V3 v0.1.1 release. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Users can turn a regular web page into one full-height PNG, then mark details with Marker, Highlight, or Border tools”; “Capture frames, vector annotations, image assembly, and export composition remain inside extension-owned browser storage and processing”; “The result page combines measured capture evidence with selection, movement, resizing, color and width controls, undo, redo, clear, copy, and download”; “Maintainers shipped v0.1.1 as a verified Chrome ZIP with a matching published SHA-256 checksum”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:1snap:contribution","case-study:1snap:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed the one-click capture, progress, result, annotation, copy, download, and recovery journeys.\nImplemented document measurement, scroll planning, visible-asset waiting, animation pausing, sticky-element handling, and state restoration.\nBuilt IndexedDB frame and annotation retention, local Canvas stitching, SVG editing and history controls, output sizing safeguards, and original-resolution annotated export.\nEstablished unit and UI coverage, the public product website, Netlify deployment, and the checksummed","Users can turn a regular web page into one full-height PNG, then mark details with Marker, Highlight, or Border tools.\nCapture frames, vector annotations, image assembly, and export composition remain inside extension-owned browser storage and processing.\nThe result page combines measured capture evidence with selection, movement, resizing, color and width controls, undo, redo, clear, copy, and download.\nMaintainers shipped v0.1.1 as a verified Chrome ZIP with a matching published SHA-256 checksum."
  - id: "case-study:bugreceipt:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the BugReceipt case study?
    text: "Screenshots and vague descriptions rarely preserve the sequence, browser evidence, environment, and visual result that developers need to reproduce a web application failure. Collecting that evidence by hand is slow, and collecting it automatically risks capturing secrets. Montasim owned product designer and browser-extension engineer, covering capture lifecycle, privacy boundaries, review workflows, and release delivery. The solution also had to respect “Browser diagnostics and screen recordings can expose sensitive information, while local filtering cannot guarantee that every secret will be recognized”; “Chrome permission, selected-tab, service-worker, navigation, and storage boundaries must remain understandable during a capture”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:bugreceipt:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Screenshots and vague descriptions rarely preserve the sequence, browser evidence, environment, and visual result that developers need to reproduce a web application failure. Collecting that evidence by hand is slow, and collecting it automatically risks capturing secrets.\nBrowser diagnostics and screen recordings can expose sensitive information, while local filtering cannot guarantee that every secret will be recognized.\nChrome permission, selected-tab, service-worker, navigation, and storage boundaries must"
  - id: "case-study:bugreceipt:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the BugReceipt solution?
    text: "A WXT Manifest V3 extension combines a side panel, a selected-tab recorder, a review page, and a background-owned capture lifecycle, with isolated page instrumentation feeding typed capture contracts. Evidence is filtered and rendered locally, stored in extension storage and IndexedDB, and leaves the browser only through a local export or an explicit Resend delivery through a TanStack Start endpoint. A representative decision was “Capture evidence locally before defining any sharing path”: Filtering inside the extension first keeps downloading as the default, so email delivery becomes a separate and explicit boundary rather than part of capture. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:bugreceipt:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A persistent React side panel, a selected-tab recorder, manual steps, and an editable review page make up the user-facing workflow.\nCapture interface\nThe background script owns the capture lifecycle and exchanges Zod-validated messages with the rest of the extension.\nSession domain\nBounded local filtering runs before storage, with extension storage holding session data and IndexedDB holding visual evidence.\nPrivacy and storage\nMarkdown and visual report bundles stay local unless the user explicitly invokes the"
  - id: "case-study:bugreceipt:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with BugReceipt?
    text: "Montasim designed the selected-tab capture, manual-step, stop, review, edit, download, copy, and explicit email journeys. He implemented background session ownership, isolated page instrumentation, cross-origin interruption handling, and persistent evidence storage. He built deterministic filtering for URLs, diagnostic text, supported response bodies, tokens, email addresses, and secret-shaped fields. He created the TanStack Start delivery endpoint, Resend boundary, deterministic broken-app fixture, CI quality gate, and checksummed Chrome release workflow. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Reporters can preserve reproduction steps, bounded console and network evidence, environment details, and a recording or screenshot in one workflow”; “Every captured item remains reviewable and removable before it leaves extension-owned storage”; “Users can export a local Markdown-and-visual bundle or deliberately send a size-bounded report through the configured endpoint”; “Maintainers can exercise known failures and ship verifiable unpacked Chrome archives with published checksums”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:bugreceipt:contribution","case-study:bugreceipt:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed the selected-tab capture, manual-step, stop, review, edit, download, copy, and explicit email journeys.\nImplemented background session ownership, isolated page instrumentation, cross-origin interruption handling, and persistent evidence storage.\nBuilt deterministic filtering for URLs, diagnostic text, supported response bodies, tokens, email addresses, and secret-shaped fields.\nCreated the TanStack Start delivery endpoint, Resend boundary, deterministic broken-app fixture, CI quality gate, and checksummed","Reporters can preserve reproduction steps, bounded console and network evidence, environment details, and a recording or screenshot in one workflow.\nEvery captured item remains reviewable and removable before it leaves extension-owned storage.\nUsers can export a local Markdown-and-visual bundle or deliberately send a size-bounded report through the configured endpoint.\nMaintainers can exercise known failures and ship verifiable unpacked Chrome archives with published checksums."
  - id: "case-study:foliofarer:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Foliofarer case study?
    text: "A memorable 3D portfolio can hide the information recruiters need. Visitors whose devices, motion preferences, or assistive technology do not suit WebGL exploration can be shut out entirely. If the 3D scene owned the content, every fallback would be a reduced version of the real site. Montasim owned product designer and frontend engineer, covering product design, 3D scene runtime, accessible content paths, deterministic world generation, and testing. The solution also had to respect “Generated worlds must be deterministic enough for navigation, the minimap, landmarks, and tests to agree on the same route”; “Every piece of content must remain reachable without operating the 3D scene”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:foliofarer:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A memorable 3D portfolio can hide the information recruiters need. Visitors whose devices, motion preferences, or assistive technology do not suit WebGL exploration can be shut out entirely. If the 3D scene owned the content, every fallback would be a reduced version of the real site.\nGenerated worlds must be deterministic enough for navigation, the minimap, landmarks, and tests to agree on the same route.\nEvery piece of content must remain reachable without operating the 3D scene.\nRendering must adapt to reduced"
  - id: "case-study:foliofarer:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Foliofarer solution?
    text: "A Next.js route shell serves both the accessible Atlas and a React Three Fiber and Three.js scene with Rapier interaction. Both paths read the same validated portfolio data, which a deterministic journey compiler turns into route and world data. A representative decision was “Pair the explorable world with a complete Atlas”: WebGL should not be the only path to the information. Both surfaces read the same data, so the Atlas is a full interface rather than a reduced fallback. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:foliofarer:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Next.js routes host the accessible Atlas, which presents the professional history as a keyboard-friendly document.\nRoute shell and Atlas\nReact Three Fiber and Three.js render the explorable world, and Rapier handles physical interaction.\nScene runtime\nA deterministic journey compiler and generators produce route and world data from validated portfolio content.\nWorld data\nPlaywright, unit, and visual-baseline tests guard the compiler, interaction, and accessibility contracts, and the site ships as static"
  - id: "case-study:foliofarer:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Foliofarer?
    text: "Montasim implemented world generation, route validation, scene navigation, the minimap, landmarks, and Passport progression. He built the accessible Atlas and keyboard-friendly navigation. He added capability-aware rendering and reduced-motion behavior. He maintained compiler, interaction, accessibility, browser, and visual-baseline tests. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Visitors can explore a 3D career world built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, and Three.js”; “Visitors can read the same professional history through the accessible Atlas without using the scene”; “Visit progress is tracked locally through the Passport”; “The experience adapts to a broader range of device capabilities and user preferences”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:foliofarer:contribution","case-study:foliofarer:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Implemented world generation, route validation, scene navigation, the minimap, landmarks, and Passport progression.\nBuilt the accessible Atlas and keyboard-friendly navigation.\nAdded capability-aware rendering and reduced-motion behavior.\nMaintained compiler, interaction, accessibility, browser, and visual-baseline tests.","Visitors can explore a 3D career world built with Next.js, React Three Fiber, and Three.js.\nVisitors can read the same professional history through the accessible Atlas without using the scene.\nVisit progress is tracked locally through the Passport.\nThe experience adapts to a broader range of device capabilities and user preferences."
  - id: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Bangladesh Location Registry case study?
    text: "Bangladesh location records are spread across government sources that each use a different structure. That makes normalized lookup, provenance review, and repeatable machine consumption difficult for anyone building on the data. Montasim owned data modeling, validation, and release engineering, covering provenance, transformation, validation, and distribution. The solution also had to respect “Administrative boundaries, names, and facilities change over time”; “Source coverage and confidence vary, and neither establishes authoritative identity or address truth”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Bangladesh location records are spread across government sources that each use a different structure. That makes normalized lookup, provenance review, and repeatable machine consumption difficult for anyone building on the data.\nAdministrative boundaries, names, and facilities change over time.\nSource coverage and confidence vary, and neither establishes authoritative identity or address truth.\nHealth, emergency, eligibility, routing, or legal use requires confirmation from a current authoritative source."
  - id: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Bangladesh Location Registry solution?
    text: "Source and provenance manifests describe where each record comes from, and normalized records are governed by JSON Schema. JavaScript scripts validate those records and generate versioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite releases with samples and documentation. A representative decision was “Keep lineage beside normalized data”: Storing source lineage and known limitations next to the records keeps uncertainty visible instead of hiding it behind a clean format. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Source and provenance manifests record where each dataset came from, what it covers, and its limitations.\nSource records\nSource data is normalized into geographic and facility records governed by JSON Schema.\nTransformation\nVersioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite outputs are generated from the same model, with samples and documentation.\nRelease formats\nJavaScript scripts validate records against the schemas and produce deterministic release builds.\nValidation\nSource and provenance manifests describe where"
  - id: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Bangladesh Location Registry?
    text: "Montasim designed normalized geographic and facility schemas. He assembled provenance and coverage manifests. He built validation and deterministic release-generation scripts. He documented responsible use, the update process, samples, citation, and source terms. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Consumers can download structured location data as JSON, streaming JSONL, or SQLite”; “Consumers can validate records against the published schemas”; “Coverage and provenance limitations can be traced before the data is used”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:contribution","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed normalized geographic and facility schemas.\nAssembled provenance and coverage manifests.\nBuilt validation and deterministic release-generation scripts.\nDocumented responsible use, the update process, samples, citation, and source terms.","Consumers can download structured location data as JSON, streaming JSONL, or SQLite.\nConsumers can validate records against the published schemas.\nCoverage and provenance limitations can be traced before the data is used."
  - id: "case-study:educanvas:problem"
