    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment.\nPersonal workplace reports provide context, not verified statements of current company policy.\nCompany names and salary records can be ambiguous, so fuzzy matching cannot silently publish a result.\nAI answers must remain bounded by"
  - id: "case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the b4joinacompany solution?
    text: "Versioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while private checkpoints live separately in MongoDB. A representative decision was “Preserve provenance at the record level”: Reported, submitted, derived, and official information stay distinct, with source URLs and capture context retained for review. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Versioned records hold company, story, comment, and salary evidence.\nSource releases\nDeterministic rules handle identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking.\nResearch layer\nNext.js serves research briefs, compare, and Ask, alongside a versioned REST API.\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB store private checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions.\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while"
  - id: "case-study:b4joinacompany:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with b4joinacompany?
    text: "Montasim defined the product methodology and the separation between reports, derived signals, and official facts. He built company research, comparison, cited Ask, correction, and private checkpoint workflows. He designed the versioned API shared by the website and companion browser extension. He implemented optimistic checkpoint revisions and separated private workspace data from the public evidence release. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Visitors can research a company without creating an account and open original evidence for important claims”; “Prepared questions are generated by fixed rules, while Ask provides cited synthesis or an explicit evidence gap”; “Saved checkpoints retain the exact evidence version and append revisions on successful updates”; “The product supports comparison without producing a synthetic company score or declaring a winner”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:b4joinacompany:contribution","case-study:b4joinacompany:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Defined the product methodology and the separation between reports, derived signals, and official facts.\nBuilt company research, comparison, cited Ask, correction, and private checkpoint workflows.\nDesigned the versioned API shared by the website and companion browser extension.\nImplemented optimistic checkpoint revisions and separated private workspace data from the public evidence release.","Visitors can research a company without creating an account and open original evidence for important claims.\nPrepared questions are generated by fixed rules, while Ask provides cited synthesis or an explicit evidence gap.\nSaved checkpoints retain the exact evidence version and append revisions on successful updates.\nThe product supports comparison without producing a synthetic company score or declaring a winner."
  - id: "case-study:thoughtline:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant case study?
    text: "Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while keeping the author's own voice, intent, and control. The page is mutable third-party content, so passing it straight into a prompt would mix data, instructions, and potentially hostile text. An assistant that drafts well but cannot be trusted with page content and credentials is not useful. Montasim owned product designer and browser-extension engineer, covering browser integration, provider workflows, trust boundaries, and privacy. The solution also had to respect “LinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before it enters any prompt”; “OpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all required so the fixed free-only route can fall back without interrupting a writing task”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:thoughtline:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while keeping the author's own voice, intent, and control. The page is mutable third-party content, so passing it straight into a prompt would mix data, instructions, and potentially hostile text. An assistant that drafts well but cannot be trusted with page content and credentials is not useful.\nLinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before it enters any prompt.\nOpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all"
  - id: "case-study:thoughtline:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant solution?
    text: "LinkedIn content scripts and a manual paste input feed React side-panel workflows. Typed drafting and source-research ports route requests through curated free OpenRouter models first, with Gemini and Groq as fallbacks, and encrypted browser storage handles credentials, history, migrations, and backups under Manifest V3 permissions. A representative decision was “Depend on typed provider ports”: Provider response shapes never reach the interface, so fallback becomes an explicit policy and each provider can be replaced at its adapter. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:thoughtline:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "LinkedIn content scripts extract the visible conversation, and a bounded manual input accepts pasted posts.\nPage integration\nReact side-panel workflows cover replies, ideas, refinement, history, settings, and onboarding.\nExtension interface\nTyped drafting and source-research ports use curated free OpenRouter models first, apply bounded provider timeouts, and fall back to Gemini and Groq.\nProvider boundary\nEncrypted browser storage, migrations, and backup and export features sit behind Manifest V3"
  - id: "case-study:thoughtline:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Thoughtline - LinkedIn Writing Assistant?
    text: "Montasim implemented the reply, idea, refine, history, settings, and onboarding journeys. He built content extraction and validated prompt envelopes. He added manual four-direction replies, model provenance, and visible provider recovery states. He added encrypted credential storage, migrations, recovery and retention rules, configuration backup, and data archives. He established static, unit, packed-extension, Axe, responsive, and visual-regression gates. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Users can paste a post or select a visible conversation and draft four reply directions”; “Users can generate and refine post ideas in their configured voice”; “Requests follow a fixed free-only route across OpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq under defined fallback rules”; “Users can export and restore validated configurations and history”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:thoughtline:contribution","case-study:thoughtline:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Implemented the reply, idea, refine, history, settings, and onboarding journeys.\nBuilt content extraction and validated prompt envelopes.\nAdded manual four-direction replies, model provenance, and visible provider recovery states.\nAdded encrypted credential storage, migrations, recovery and retention rules, configuration backup, and data archives.\nEstablished static, unit, packed-extension, Axe, responsive, and visual-regression gates.","Users can paste a post or select a visible conversation and draft four reply directions.\nUsers can generate and refine post ideas in their configured voice.\nRequests follow a fixed free-only route across OpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq under defined fallback rules.\nUsers can export and restore validated configurations and history."
  - id: "case-study:routempo:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Routempo case study?
    text: "Repeatable routines are easy to schedule but hard to evaluate. When plans, actual outcomes, timezone changes, and external calendars live in separate systems, a person cannot tell what they intended from what they did. Storing everything on one recurring record also means every edit risks rewriting history. Montasim owned product designer and full-stack engineer, covering product workflows, domain modeling, data boundaries, integrations, and delivery. The solution also had to respect “Schedule and completion semantics must stay correct across user timezones, so the meaning of \"today\" cannot be left to display formatting”; “Reminders depend on browser permission, push subscriptions, and a scheduled server invocation, any of which can be missing”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:routempo:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Repeatable routines are easy to schedule but hard to evaluate. When plans, actual outcomes, timezone changes, and external calendars live in separate systems, a person cannot tell what they intended from what they did. Storing everything on one recurring record also means every edit risks rewriting history.\nSchedule and completion semantics must stay correct across user timezones, so the meaning of \"today\" cannot be left to display formatting.\nReminders depend on browser permission, push subscriptions, and a"
  - id: "case-study:routempo:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Routempo solution?
    text: "TanStack Start routes and a React interface sit on top of domain services for routines, sessions, and reviews. Drizzle repositories persist that history in Neon PostgreSQL behind Better Auth, while Web Push and Google Calendar and Tasks adapters, along with scheduled summary endpoints, stay at the edge. A representative decision was “Store durable behavioral records in PostgreSQL through Drizzle”: Browser-only state would be simpler to run, but a database gives queryable history and portable account data. The trade-off is more infrastructure to operate. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:routempo:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "TanStack Start routes and React screens handle routine planning, daily execution, and review.\nInterface\nRoutine, session, and review services own timezone conversion and keep planned intent separate from recorded outcomes.\nDomain\nDrizzle repositories store durable behavioral records in Neon PostgreSQL, with Better Auth managing accounts.\nData and auth\nWeb Push and Google Calendar and Tasks adapters, plus scheduled summary endpoints, connect the planner to reminders and external calendars.\nIntegrations\nTanStack"
  - id: "case-study:routempo:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Routempo?
    text: "Montasim built the flows for creating repeatable routines and running them day to day. He modeled sessions, check-ins, reviews, and account data in the database schema and migrations. He added calendar and task sync, push subscriptions, scheduled weekly summaries, import and export, and account deletion paths. He established test coverage with Vitest, Testing Library, and Playwright. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Users can plan routines and run them each day in their own timezone”; “Recorded outcomes stay separate from plans, so users can review real patterns over time”; “Users can move their account data in and out of the product”; “Users who opt in can connect Google services and receive reminders and weekly summaries”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:routempo:contribution","case-study:routempo:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Built the flows for creating repeatable routines and running them day to day.\nModeled sessions, check-ins, reviews, and account data in the database schema and migrations.\nAdded calendar and task sync, push subscriptions, scheduled weekly summaries, import and export, and account deletion paths.\nEstablished test coverage with Vitest, Testing Library, and Playwright.","Users can plan routines and run them each day in their own timezone.\nRecorded outcomes stay separate from plans, so users can review real patterns over time.\nUsers can move their account data in and out of the product.\nUsers who opt in can connect Google services and receive reminders and weekly summaries."
  - id: "case-study:postcraft:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the PostCraft case study?
    text: "Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools. Montasim owned product design and full-stack engineering, covering AI workflow, publishing integrations, and background jobs. The solution also had to respect “AI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas”; “LinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the PostCraft solution?
    text: "The Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules, infrastructure in core services, and durable delivery in Inngest. A representative decision was “Separate provider choice from generation”: A provider switcher walks configured models and keys, so product workflows do not depend on one vendor-specific client. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,"
  - id: "case-study:postcraft:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with PostCraft?
    text: "Montasim defined the end-to-end product flow from topic input to platform-ready output. He built the Next.js interface, API routes, domain services, and MongoDB repositories. He implemented multi-provider AI selection, reusable guardrails, scoring, and platform previews. He integrated authentication, account recovery, social OAuth, Inngest scheduling, and publishing records. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery”; “Provider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint”; “Publishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation”; “Operational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:postcraft:contribution","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Defined the end-to-end product flow from topic input to platform-ready output.\nBuilt the Next.js interface, API routes, domain services, and MongoDB repositories.\nImplemented multi-provider AI selection, reusable guardrails, scoring, and platform previews.\nIntegrated authentication, account recovery, social OAuth, Inngest scheduling, and publishing records.","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "case-study:tin-audit-checker:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the TIN Audit Checker - Bangladesh case study?
    text: "A list circulated as the NBR Risk-Based Audit selection for assessment year 2023-24, covering 72,342 returns, and searching it manually for one TIN is slow, especially on a phone. The obvious fix, a server-side search API, would send every person's tax identifier to another application server and its logs. Montasim owned data modeling, privacy design, and release engineering, covering client-side lookup, data provenance, and public distribution. The solution also had to respect “The data is a historical list, not a live audit or compliance status”; “The claimed NBR provenance, accuracy, completeness, and redistribution rights have not been established”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:tin-audit-checker:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A list circulated as the NBR Risk-Based Audit selection for assessment year 2023-24, covering 72,342 returns, and searching it manually for one TIN is slow, especially on a phone. The obvious fix, a server-side search API, would send every person's tax identifier to another application server and its logs.\nThe data is a historical list, not a live audit or compliance status.\nThe claimed NBR provenance, accuracy, completeness, and redistribution rights have not been established.\nBecause the dataset ships to the"
  - id: "case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the TIN Audit Checker - Bangladesh solution?
    text: "A Next.js page fetches the static JSON dataset once per session and caches it in memory. Each search normalizes the input and runs an exact match in the browser, and there is no API that receives a TIN. A representative decision was “Perform lookup entirely in the browser”: The server never receives an entered TIN, so no tax identifiers reach application logs. The trade-off is that the full list is downloadable by anyone. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A responsive Next.js page accepts a TIN and shows the match state with its mapped details.\nLookup interface\nThe static JSON list is fetched once per session and kept in memory for every later search.\nSession dataset\nInput is stripped of formatting characters and compared by exact digits, with no TIN submission API behind it.\nClient-side matching\nA Next.js page fetches the static JSON dataset once per session and caches it in memory. Each search normalizes the input and runs an exact match in the browser, and there"
  - id: "case-study:tin-audit-checker:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with TIN Audit Checker - Bangladesh?
    text: "Montasim converted the bundled list into a focused, responsive lookup. He implemented one fetch per session with in-memory caching and digit normalization. He added privacy, provenance, and methodology warnings with guidance to verify through official channels. He documented the deployment and the repository's missing tests, security policy, and license. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Users can search the historical list quickly, including on mobile”; “Typed TINs are never submitted to an application API”; “A match returns only the bounded details mapped from the list”; “Both matches and non-matches tell the user to confirm their status with current official sources”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:tin-audit-checker:contribution","case-study:tin-audit-checker:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Converted the bundled list into a focused, responsive lookup.\nImplemented one fetch per session with in-memory caching and digit normalization.\nAdded privacy, provenance, and methodology warnings with guidance to verify through official channels.\nDocumented the deployment and the repository's missing tests, security policy, and license.","Users can search the historical list quickly, including on mobile.\nTyped TINs are never submitted to an application API.\nA match returns only the bounded details mapped from the list.\nBoth matches and non-matches tell the user to confirm their status with current official sources."
  - id: "case-study:devtools:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the DevTools case study?
    text: "Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse. Montasim owned product design and full-stack engineering, covering developer utilities, shared interaction patterns, and account-backed tools. The solution also had to respect “Many transformations should stay in the browser, while network diagnostics necessarily contact a target or server endpoint”; “A catalog with more than 50 workflows must remain quick to search, navigate, and extend without duplicating page infrastructure”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:devtools:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse.\nMany transformations should stay in the browser, while network diagnostics necessarily contact a target or server endpoint.\nA catalog with more than 50 workflows must remain quick to search, navigate, and extend without duplicating page infrastructure.\nSaved items, shares, webhooks, and short links introduce"
  - id: "case-study:devtools:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the DevTools solution?
    text: "A navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool features own their tabs and logic, shared app infrastructure handles auth and storage, and route handlers isolate network and persistence work. A representative decision was “Make the catalog declarative”: The navigation registry is the single source for tool discovery, reducing drift between routes, menus, and command search. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:devtools:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A navigation registry defines the searchable routes, labels, and keyboard discovery for every tool.\nTool registry\nFocused modules implement the formatters, generators, testers, inspectors, and references.\nFeature modules\nLocal transforms, preferences, input history, and reusable state stay in the browser.\nBrowser boundary\nNext.js APIs, Better Auth, PostgreSQL, Prisma, and target services handle the work that has to leave the browser.\nServer boundary\nA navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool"
  - id: "case-study:devtools:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with DevTools?
    text: "Montasim designed and implemented the searchable toolbox and its shared tool interaction patterns. He built local formatters and generators alongside network diagnostics and reference utilities. He added account sync, expiring shares, short links, webhook inspection, and database-backed saved items. He established TypeScript, lint, production-build, and GitHub Actions checks for changes targeting main. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model”; “Common transformations can run without an account and without sending their current input to the server”; “Server-backed features add reuse and sharing without obscuring when data leaves the browser”; “The architecture supports both small local utilities and stateful workflows without forcing them through one execution path”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:devtools:contribution","case-study:devtools:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed and implemented the searchable toolbox and its shared tool interaction patterns.\nBuilt local formatters and generators alongside network diagnostics and reference utilities.\nAdded account sync, expiring shares, short links, webhook inspection, and database-backed saved items.\nEstablished TypeScript, lint, production-build, and GitHub Actions checks for changes targeting main.","More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model.\nCommon transformations can run without an account and without sending their current input to the server.\nServer-backed features add reuse and sharing without obscuring when data leaves the browser.\nThe architecture supports both small local utilities and stateful workflows without forcing them through one execution path."
  - id: "case-study:markdown-typing-svg:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Markdown Typing SVG case study?
    text: "Developers who want a typing animation in a GitHub profile or README usually have to edit SVG and CSS by hand and then host the result somewhere. That makes it slow to experiment with text, timing, or styling, and hard to share or reproduce a design someone else made. Montasim owned developer-tool design and full-stack engineering, covering developer workflow, API surface, and deployment. The solution also had to respect “Public embeds depend on the deployment staying available, because every image is generated on request”; “Successful responses are cached for 24 hours, so an updated design may not appear in an existing embed until the cache expires”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:markdown-typing-svg:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Developers who want a typing animation in a GitHub profile or README usually have to edit SVG and CSS by hand and then host the result somewhere. That makes it slow to experiment with text, timing, or styling, and hard to share or reproduce a design someone else made.\nPublic embeds depend on the deployment staying available, because every image is generated on request.\nSuccessful responses are cached for 24 hours, so an updated design may not appear in an existing embed until the cache expires.\nThere is no"
  - id: "case-study:markdown-typing-svg:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Markdown Typing SVG solution?
    text: "A React visual editor builds the design and serializes it into URL query parameters, which the server validates with Zod. The server then generates the SVG on demand and returns it with explicit image and cache headers, so no generated file is ever stored. A representative decision was “Encode configuration in the URL”: Every design becomes a shareable link that anyone can reproduce, and the service needs no database or session state to serve it. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:markdown-typing-svg:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "The React editor manages typography and animation controls, saved presets, and a live preview.\nVisual editor\nEach configuration is serialized into URL query parameters and validated with Zod before any rendering happens.\nQuery contract\nA server route escapes the text, renders the animated SVG, and sets explicit image and cache headers on the response.\nSVG generator\nA React visual editor builds the design and serializes it into URL query parameters, which the server validates with Zod. The server then generates the"
  - id: "case-study:markdown-typing-svg:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Markdown Typing SVG?
    text: "Montasim built multi-line typography and animation controls with a live preview. He implemented Markdown, HTML, and direct image URL output. He added validated server-side SVG generation with text escaping. He configured cache headers and browser-local presets. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Users can compose animated text visually instead of editing SVG by hand”; “A finished design can be embedded through Markdown, HTML, or a plain image URL”; “Any design can be reproduced from its query parameters alone”; “Invalid options produce an explicit validation response, and no generated files are stored”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:markdown-typing-svg:contribution","case-study:markdown-typing-svg:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Built multi-line typography and animation controls with a live preview.\nImplemented Markdown, HTML, and direct image URL output.\nAdded validated server-side SVG generation with text escaping.\nConfigured cache headers and browser-local presets.","Users can compose animated text visually instead of editing SVG by hand.\nA finished design can be embedded through Markdown, HTML, or a plain image URL.\nAny design can be reproduced from its query parameters alone.\nInvalid options produce an explicit validation response, and no generated files are stored."
  - id: "case-study:ramadan-clock:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Ramadan Clock case study?
    text: "People observing Ramadan in Bangladesh need clear Sehri and Iftar times for their own district, shown in local time. Those times depend on the external prayer-time source, the selected district, and later timetable corrections, so the maintenance workflow behind the public view has to be auditable. Montasim owned product design and full-stack engineering, covering product workflows, data boundaries, and delivery. The solution also had to respect “Calculated times depend on the source, calculation method, location, and the local authority people follow”; “External fetching needs retries and rate limiting, and it must not overwrite validated data indiscriminately”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:ramadan-clock:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "People observing Ramadan in Bangladesh need clear Sehri and Iftar times for their own district, shown in local time. Those times depend on the external prayer-time source, the selected district, and later timetable corrections, so the maintenance workflow behind the public view has to be auditable.\nCalculated times depend on the source, calculation method, location, and the local authority people follow.\nExternal fetching needs retries and rate limiting, and it must not overwrite validated data"
  - id: "case-study:ramadan-clock:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Ramadan Clock solution?
    text: "A Next.js application serves the public schedule and a protected admin area, and a schedule service handles queries, imports, status, and formatting. Times are stored in PostgreSQL through Prisma as unique date and location records behind a tagged cache, while an Aladhan adapter with retry and token-bucket controls fetches source data and export generators produce downloadable schedules. A representative decision was “Persist normalized date and location times”: Stored records keep the public view fast and stable and let an administrator's correction stick, instead of recalculating times on every public request. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:ramadan-clock:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Next.js pages serve the public schedule views and the authenticated admin area.\nInterface\nA schedule service handles queries, imports, live status, and time formatting.\nDomain\nPrisma stores times in PostgreSQL as unique date and location records, and a tagged cache serves public reads.\nData and integrations\nAn Aladhan adapter fetches source times with retry and token-bucket controls, and export generators produce PDF and image schedules.\nSource fetching and export\nA Next.js application serves the public schedule"
  - id: "case-study:ramadan-clock:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Ramadan Clock?
    text: "Montasim built the today, calendar, and location views with a live status display. He implemented authenticated administration with multi-district fetch progress, upload history, and cache controls. He created the data model and a validated JSON and CSV import path. He added PDF and image export, API documentation, Vitest tests, and operational guides. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Visitors can check today's times and the full Ramadan schedule for their district”; “Administrators can import, fetch, and manage location data”; “Duplicate and malformed records are rejected before they are written”; “Users can export schedules, and the source and accuracy guidance travels with the data”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:ramadan-clock:contribution","case-study:ramadan-clock:outcomes"
