  - id: "case-study:clm-api:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the CLM API solution?
    text: "NestJS messaging modules separate application services, domain contracts, and MongoDB persistence. Queued webhook processing connects provider events to conversations, automation, and real-time updates. A representative decision was “Persist coexistence state”: Replaced process-local coexistence bindings with repository-backed storage and added history-sync lifecycle handling (7654f7bb7). The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:clm-api:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Meta webhook handlers normalize inbound events for WhatsApp, Messenger, and Instagram, with event-specific deduplication and validation.\nProvider event processing\nApplication services coordinate keyword automation, routing, quoted replies, agent assignment, and SLA timers.\nConversation workflows\nMongoDB repositories retain coexistence bindings and conversation state. Redis and Socket.IO support presence, typing, and conversation events with access checks.\nPersistence and real-time delivery\nNestJS messaging modules"
  - id: "case-study:clm-api:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with CLM API?
    text: "Montasim implemented Meta messaging backend features and subsequent Instagram comment, Messenger postback, opt-in, referral, and reaction handling. He connected keyword automation and automatic agent assignment to the inbound message pipeline. He implemented durable WhatsApp coexistence bindings, signup completion, contact and history synchronization, and media lifecycle handling. He added presence, typing, join, leave, mark-read, quoted-reply, and SLA timer workflows with access checks. He added unit and end-to-end tests for coexistence, automation, routing, inbound events, and messaging flows. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Repository history contains 47 commits authored by Montasim between 28 May and 10 June 2026”; “Messaging workflows gained persistent coexistence state, event deduplication, and explicit conversation-routing fallbacks”; “Automated test sources cover provider event handling, real-time access checks, routing, and messaging integration paths; no production performance claim is inferred from those tests”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
