  - id: "case-study:coaching-management:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Coaching Management solution?
    text: "Next.js App Router combines server-rendered reads with interactive client workflows, Prisma models PostgreSQL tenancy and audit state, Supabase provides database and identity infrastructure, and adapter boundaries isolate biometric devices and SMS providers. A representative decision was “Make organization scope part of every protected query”: Tenant identity and role checks are applied at server boundaries so client state cannot broaden access to academic or financial records. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:coaching-management:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Next.js server and client components, shadcn/ui, TanStack Table, and forms support optimistic operational workflows.\nApplication interface\nThe domain models organizations, roles, levels, subjects, class sections, enrollments, attendance, fees, payments, expenses, and audit records.\nAcademic and finance domain\nPrisma over PostgreSQL provides indexed tenant queries and soft-delete history, with Supabase-backed authentication.\nPersistence and identity\nVendor adapters normalize biometric devices while encrypted"
  - id: "case-study:coaching-management:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Coaching Management?
    text: "Montasim designed the multi-tenant organization, role, academic, finance, attendance, and audit data model. He built management workflows for students, teachers, class sections, enrollment, fees, payments, expenses, and administrative roles. He defined the encrypted biometric credential boundary, shared device identity strategy, polling workflow, and vendor adapter model. He implemented the SMS scheduling and batch-delivery architecture for attendance notifications and marketing outreach. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Institute operators can manage academic, attendance, financial, and communication work from one tenant-aware platform”; “Granular roles separate platform administration, organization administration, finance, and academic coordination”; “Multiple biometric device vendors can feed one attendance model without leaking vendor-specific behavior into core workflows”; “Financial corrections and record removal retain audit evidence instead of erasing operational history”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:coaching-management:contribution","case-study:coaching-management:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Designed the multi-tenant organization, role, academic, finance, attendance, and audit data model.\nBuilt management workflows for students, teachers, class sections, enrollment, fees, payments, expenses, and administrative roles.\nDefined the encrypted biometric credential boundary, shared device identity strategy, polling workflow, and vendor adapter model.\nImplemented the SMS scheduling and batch-delivery architecture for attendance notifications and marketing outreach.","Institute operators can manage academic, attendance, financial, and communication work from one tenant-aware platform.\nGranular roles separate platform administration, organization administration, finance, and academic coordination.\nMultiple biometric device vendors can feed one attendance model without leaking vendor-specific behavior into core workflows.\nFinancial corrections and record removal retain audit evidence instead of erasing operational history."
  - id: "case-study:vidquery:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension case study?
    text: "Viewers often scrub through long YouTube videos to find one specific answer. The page already exposes metadata and a transcript that could answer the question directly. Without a way to query that context, the viewer spends time searching instead of learning. Montasim owned product designer and browser-extension engineer, covering browser integration, provider workflow, privacy, and release delivery. The solution also had to respect “Answer quality is limited by whether a transcript exists and how accurate it is”; “Gemini calls require the user's own API key and send bounded video context to an external provider”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
