    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Viewers often scrub through long YouTube videos to find one specific answer. The page already exposes metadata and a transcript that could answer the question directly. Without a way to query that context, the viewer spends time searching instead of learning.\nAnswer quality is limited by whether a transcript exists and how accurate it is.\nGemini calls require the user's own API key and send bounded video context to an external provider.\nChanges to YouTube routes or page structure, along with extension permission"
  - id: "case-study:vidquery:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension solution?
    text: "A content layer extracts metadata and transcripts from the open YouTube page and passes them to a React extension interface. A validated context envelope and a Gemini adapter form the provider boundary, while browser storage holds settings and a companion web app documents and distributes the Manifest V3 package. A representative decision was “Use visible page metadata and transcripts”: Downloading or processing the media itself would add cost, permissions, and complexity. The page already exposes enough text to answer most questions. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:vidquery:architecture"
