  - id: "case-study:vidquery:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with VidQuery - YouTube Q&A Extension?
    text: "Montasim built YouTube route detection and extraction of transcripts and metadata. He implemented Gemini-backed question answering with schema validation and bounded context. He added local handling of credentials and settings, along with explicit privacy guidance. He created release automation and quality gates for both the extension and the website. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Users can ask questions about an open YouTube video”; “Answers draw on the available transcript and visible metadata”; “Users keep control of their own provider credential”; “Maintainers can produce installable GitHub release artifacts”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:vidquery:contribution","case-study:vidquery:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Built YouTube route detection and extraction of transcripts and metadata.\nImplemented Gemini-backed question answering with schema validation and bounded context.\nAdded local handling of credentials and settings, along with explicit privacy guidance.\nCreated release automation and quality gates for both the extension and the website.","Users can ask questions about an open YouTube video.\nAnswers draw on the available transcript and visible metadata.\nUsers keep control of their own provider credential.\nMaintainers can produce installable GitHub release artifacts."
  - id: "case-study:client-parser:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the client-parser case study?
    text: "Analytics and diagnostics need a normalized view of which browser, OS, device, or bot made a request. The raw User-Agent string can be changed by any client, and many parsers are heavy or quietly encourage using the result for decisions it cannot support. Montasim owned library design and package engineering, covering typed APIs, compatibility, and package distribution. The solution also had to respect “User-Agent classification is heuristic, and any client can spoof it”; “Client Hints availability varies and may be restricted for privacy”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:client-parser:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Analytics and diagnostics need a normalized view of which browser, OS, device, or bot made a request. The raw User-Agent string can be changed by any client, and many parsers are heavy or quietly encourage using the result for decisions it cannot support.\nUser-Agent classification is heuristic, and any client can spoof it.\nClient Hints availability varies and may be restricted for privacy.\nResults cannot safely drive authentication, authorization, or capability decisions."
  - id: "case-study:client-parser:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the client-parser solution?
    text: "Pure parsing and normalization rules produce a typed result model, packaged with zero dependencies in both ESM and CommonJS. A workspace-linked playground and documentation app runs the library locally, and a regression corpus guards the rules as clients change. A representative decision was “State the heuristic boundary in the API”: The documentation says results suit analytics and presentation, points to feature detection for behavior, and rules out security decisions. Users build on whatever an API implies, so the limit belongs in the contract. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
