    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Upload, download, and HTTP code needs dependable mappings between filenames or extensions and media types. Carrying a broad MIME database into every bundle for that job adds weight most applications do not need.\nOne extension can have aliases or ambiguous mappings.\nLookup must normalize case, dots, and paths without implying that file content was inspected.\nESM and CommonJS consumers need stable typed exports with no runtime dependencies."
  - id: "case-study:mime-types-lite:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the mime-types-lite solution?
    text: "A curated media-type registry backs pure lookup and validation functions, which are built with tsup into a dual-format package with typed exports. A TanStack Start docs and playground app in the same workspace runs against the local package. A representative decision was “Keep the scope narrow and dependency-free”: A curated registry and a small set of helpers cover common upload and HTTP needs without a broad database dependency, at the cost of not covering every registered type. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
