  - id: "case-study:book-heaven:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Book Heaven case study?
    text: "The SupportKori integration was correct in source and merged to main, but Book Heaven's Vercel production deployment failed before the new build reached visitors. The live site therefore looked as if the widget change were missing, even though the actual fault was in the delivery path. The recovery needed to identify each build blocker without weakening existing API contracts or treating a merged pull request as proof of a successful release. Montasim owned incident diagnosis, build engineering, and release verification, covering next.js build reliability, dependency compatibility, and Vercel delivery. The solution also had to respect “The large Next.js application compiles many routes and integrations inside Vercel's finite build-memory envelope, and the default Node.js heap was exhausted during production validation”; “The Stripe dependency used a caret range, allowing a newer release whose TypeScript contract expected a later API version than the application intentionally used”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:book-heaven:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "The SupportKori integration was correct in source and merged to main, but Book Heaven's Vercel production deployment failed before the new build reached visitors. The live site therefore looked as if the widget change were missing, even though the actual fault was in the delivery path. The recovery needed to identify each build blocker without weakening existing API contracts or treating a merged pull request as proof of a successful release.\nThe large Next.js application compiles many routes and integrations"
  - id: "case-study:book-heaven:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Book Heaven solution?
    text: "A four-stage recovery loop separated source correctness, deterministic production compilation, platform deployment, and public runtime verification so each failure could be diagnosed at the boundary where it occurred. A representative decision was “Separate feature correctness from delivery health”: The widget implementation and the deployment pipeline were tested independently, preventing a stale production page from being misdiagnosed as a frontend defect. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:book-heaven:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A root-layout Next.js Script component owns the exact SupportKori URL, account ID, message, color, and position.\nSource contract\nPrisma generation and Next.js compilation run with a 4 GB Node.js heap and two build workers to keep resource use predictable.\nBuild envelope\nStripe is pinned to version 20.1.0 so its types remain aligned with the API version selected by the application.\nDependency contract\nGitHub status, the Vercel preview, the production deployment, and the returned public HTML form a chain of release"
  - id: "case-study:book-heaven:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Book Heaven?
    text: "Montasim confirmed that the merged root-layout integration was correct while the public site was still serving the previous deployment. He reproduced the production build and traced the failure through memory pressure and Stripe type-version drift. He updated all production build paths with a 4 GB heap, limited Next.js to two build workers, and pinned Stripe to the compatible release. He delivered the fixes through reviewed pull requests, then checked the Vercel preview, production status, and live HTML contract. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “The repaired main-branch build completed successfully on Vercel at the verified commit”; “Book Heaven's public site now loads the exact SupportKori script with the required ID, message, color, and position”; “Build behavior is less sensitive to heap exhaustion and excessive worker parallelism”; “Stripe type compatibility can no longer drift beyond the selected API contract through an implicit minor-version update”; “The release workflow now distinguishes code merged from deployment completed and production artifact verified”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:book-heaven:contribution","case-study:book-heaven:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Confirmed that the merged root-layout integration was correct while the public site was still serving the previous deployment.\nReproduced the production build and traced the failure through memory pressure and Stripe type-version drift.\nUpdated all production build paths with a 4 GB heap, limited Next.js to two build workers, and pinned Stripe to the compatible release.\nDelivered the fixes through reviewed pull requests, then checked the Vercel preview, production status, and live HTML contract.","The repaired main-branch build completed successfully on Vercel at the verified commit.\nBook Heaven's public site now loads the exact SupportKori script with the required ID, message, color, and position.\nBuild behavior is less sensitive to heap exhaustion and excessive worker parallelism.\nStripe type compatibility can no longer drift beyond the selected API contract through an implicit minor-version update.\nThe release workflow now distinguishes code merged from deployment completed and production artifact"
  - id: "case-study:github-readme-counter:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the GitHub README Counter case study?
    text: "README authors who want a number that changes cannot get one from Markdown alone, because Markdown cannot run JavaScript or call an API and render the result. The only dynamic thing a README can embed is an image URL, so a counter has to be a service that returns an image. Montasim owned API design and backend engineering, covering HTTP delivery, state management, and SVG generation. The solution also had to respect “Every image fetch is counted, including bots, caches, image proxies, renderers, and refreshes, so the value is a request count rather than unique human visitors”; “Counts persist to a file in a single process, so they can reset on an ephemeral filesystem and cannot be shared safely across processes”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:github-readme-counter:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "README authors who want a number that changes cannot get one from Markdown alone, because Markdown cannot run JavaScript or call an API and render the result. The only dynamic thing a README can embed is an image URL, so a counter has to be a service that returns an image.\nEvery image fetch is counted, including bots, caches, image proxies, renderers, and refreshes, so the value is a request count rather than unique human visitors.\nCounts persist to a file in a single process, so they can reset on an ephemeral"
  - id: "case-study:github-readme-counter:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the GitHub README Counter solution?
    text: "Express routes validate the query options and increment or read a named counter that is held in memory and persisted to a file. The current value is rendered into an escaped SVG response, and a separate JSON endpoint reports service health. A representative decision was “Return SVG”: Markdown will embed an image URL but not script output, and SVG keeps the response small and easy to customize. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:github-readme-counter:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "Express routes accept the counter name and display options and validate the query before doing any work.\nHTTP service\nNamed counters are kept in memory and persisted to a single file, with no database to provision.\nState\nA renderer escapes all user-supplied text into the SVG badge, and a JSON endpoint reports health, status, and uptime.\nSVG response\nExpress routes validate the query options and increment or read a named counter that is held in memory and persisted to a file. The current value is rendered into an"
  - id: "case-study:github-readme-counter:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with GitHub README Counter?
    text: "Montasim implemented named counters that increment and read on each request. He built configurable SVG output with escaped user text. He added health, status, and uptime reporting. He documented embedding, configuration, persistence, and deployment limitations. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Self-hosters can embed a counter in any README without provisioning a database”; “Users can customize the badge's colors, label, and style through the request”; “Separate counters can be kept for different identifiers”; “Self-hosters can check basic process health through the status endpoint”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:github-readme-counter:contribution","case-study:github-readme-counter:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Implemented named counters that increment and read on each request.\nBuilt configurable SVG output with escaped user text.\nAdded health, status, and uptime reporting.\nDocumented embedding, configuration, persistence, and deployment limitations.","Self-hosters can embed a counter in any README without provisioning a database.\nUsers can customize the badge's colors, label, and style through the request.\nSeparate counters can be kept for different identifiers.\nSelf-hosters can check basic process health through the status endpoint."
  - id: "case-study:mmh-re-annotation:problem"
    category: case-study
    question: What problem did Montasim address in the Clinical Movement Re-annotation case study?
    text: "Automated movement analysis can place measurement geometry on an imperfect frame or anatomical position. Clinical reviewers need a precise, explainable way to choose the right frame, correct lines and angles, compare reference guidance, and save revised measurements without losing the original exercise context. Within the MyMedicalHub team, his role covered frontend architecture and modernization within the MyMedicalHub team, spanning clinical review workflow, canvas geometry, legacy API integration, validation, error recovery, and automated testing. The solution also had to respect “Measurement geometry must remain correct while media is scaled, points are dragged, and line types restrict movement to one axis”; “The workflow depends on multiple tenant-specific legacy APIs and session values whose payloads can be missing, stale, or inconsistent”. This case is useful to hiring teams and clients because it shows how he discovers the technical and product constraints behind an apparently simple request."
