  - id: "case-study:mmh-re-annotation:architecture"
    category: case-study
    question: How did Montasim structure the Clinical Movement Re-annotation solution?
    text: "A Next.js and React review shell separates workflow state from Konva geometry, validates upstream payloads through Zod, maps legacy responses into typed domain models, and coordinates frame selection, alignment, verification, and persistence through a reducer-driven controller. A representative decision was “Separate geometry from the canvas renderer”: Pure coordinate and measurement functions can be tested without Konva, while the interface focuses on interaction and presentation. The value of this architecture is the reasoning behind it—Montasim connects a real constraint to an explicit boundary that another developer can review, test, and evolve."
    factIds[1]: "case-study:mmh-re-annotation:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[1]: "A reducer and controller own frame selection, alignment, verification, loading, failure, save, and completion transitions.\nReview workflow\nTyped points, lines, angles, plumb references, scaling rules, and measurement calculations stay independent of rendering components.\nGeometry domain\nReact Konva renders the patient frame, draggable key points, measurement geometry, reference guidance, and responsive controls.\nCanvas interface\nSchema validation, response mappers, diagnostics, and an API client normalize tenant"
  - id: "case-study:mmh-re-annotation:delivery"
    category: case-study
    question: What did Montasim deliver and achieve with Clinical Movement Re-annotation?
    text: "Montasim rebuilt the re-annotation experience around Next.js, React, TypeScript, and a structured clinical review flow. He separated annotation geometry, schemas, API mapping, reducer state, asset preloading, and canvas presentation into testable boundaries. He implemented frame selection, draggable line and angle correction, automated-analysis comparison, reference guidance, save, retake, and completion states. He added unit, integration, and browser-oriented coverage for geometry, upstream mappings, API contracts, and critical review interactions. The resulting capabilities and outcomes are “Reviewers can correct automated clinical measurements through a guided workflow instead of manipulating an opaque legacy canvas”; “Typed domain models preserve line, angle, direction, and measurement semantics across load, edit, render, and save operations”; “Malformed or unavailable upstream data produces explicit recovery guidance rather than partially initialized review state”; “The modernized boundaries make geometry and API behavior independently testable while legacy services continue to operate”. This gives a reviewer a traceable line from Montasim's implementation work to the product capabilities and limits that followed."
    factIds[2]: "case-study:mmh-re-annotation:contribution","case-study:mmh-re-annotation:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Rebuilt the re-annotation experience around Next.js, React, TypeScript, and a structured clinical review flow.\nSeparated annotation geometry, schemas, API mapping, reducer state, asset preloading, and canvas presentation into testable boundaries.\nImplemented frame selection, draggable line and angle correction, automated-analysis comparison, reference guidance, save, retake, and completion states.\nAdded unit, integration, and browser-oriented coverage for geometry, upstream mappings, API contracts, and critical","Reviewers can correct automated clinical measurements through a guided workflow instead of manipulating an opaque legacy canvas.\nTyped domain models preserve line, angle, direction, and measurement semantics across load, edit, render, and save operations.\nMalformed or unavailable upstream data produces explicit recovery guidance rather than partially initialized review state.\nThe modernized boundaries make geometry and API behavior independently testable while legacy services continue to operate."
  - id: "case-study:mmh-telemedicine:problem"
