  - id: "blog:building-email-signatures-that-survive-gmail"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Building Email Signatures That Survive Gmail”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Building Email Signatures That Survive Gmail” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Most email signatures look right in a design tool and break in Gmail. Classes, style blocks, SVG icons and percentage widths get stripped, images stop loading, and the layout changes from one inbox to the next. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:building-email-signatures-that-survive-gmail","case-study:sigcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Product engineering\nfalse\nAn email signature built from tables and inline styles, with PNG icons in any colour flowing into it\n/images/blog/email-signature.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-sigcraft\n2026-11-02\nbuilding-email-signatures-that-survive-gmail\nBuilding Email Signatures That Survive Gmail\nproduct","Most email signatures look right in a design tool and break in Gmail. Classes, style blocks, SVG icons and percentage widths get stripped, images stop loading, and the layout changes from one inbox to the next.\nGmail strips classes, style blocks and SVG, so every signature has to be built from tables with inline styles and fixed pixel widths.\nThe photo and icons load from a public URL each time an email opens, so the image host must stay stable and cache-friendly.\nMail apps' dark modes recolour text but never"
  - id: "blog:a-family-gift-ledger-that-lives-in-your-own-google-sheet"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Family Gift Ledger That Lives in Your Own Google Sheet”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Family Gift Ledger That Lives in Your Own Google Sheet” is framed as “A family's gift notebook is a real ledger”: In most Bangladeshi families, gifts at weddings, aqiqahs and birthdays are recorded in a paper notebook: who gave what, and what the family gave back. It is a financial record that matters for years. It is also hard to search, easy to lose and impossible for two relatives to update at once. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-family-gift-ledger-that-lives-in-your-own-google-sheet","blog:a-family-gift-ledger-that-lives-in-your-own-google-sheet:the-notebook"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Product engineering\nGiftbook records wedding, aqiqah and birthday gifts without a server. The ledger lives in the family's own Google Sheet, entries land offline first, and sync stops rather than corrupt a hand-edited sheet.\ntrue\nPhones writing gift entries to a local store that syncs into one shared spreadsheet\n/images/blog/giftbook-ledger.webp\nauthored\nproject-giftbook\n2026-10-09\n3\na-family-gift-ledger-that-lives-in-your-own-google-sheet\nA Family Gift Ledger That Lives in Your Own Google Sheet\nproduct","the-notebook\nThe problem\nIn most Bangladeshi families, gifts at weddings, aqiqahs and birthdays are recorded in a paper notebook: who gave what, and what the family gave back. It is a financial record that matters for years. It is also hard to search, easy to lose and impossible for two relatives to update at once.\nGeneric apps solve the search problem by putting that record on someone else's server, and they rarely handle Bangla numerals or lakh-style grouping. I wanted the digital notebook without either"
  - id: "blog:designing-a-local-first-family-tree-people-can-read"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Designing a Local-First Family Tree People Can Read”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Designing a Local-First Family Tree People Can Read” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Family history becomes difficult to trust when relationships are visually ambiguous, important life details are scattered across tools, and an export cannot recreate the archive that a family carefully assembled. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:designing-a-local-first-family-tree-people-can-read","case-study:rootstory:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Product engineering\ntrue\nA family tree with distinct parent, partner and sibling connections kept local to the browser\n/images/blog/family-tree.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-rootstory\n2026-10-08\ndesigning-a-local-first-family-tree-people-can-read\nDesigning a Local-First Family Tree People Can Read\nproduct","Family history becomes difficult to trust when relationships are visually ambiguous, important life details are scattered across tools, and an export cannot recreate the archive that a family carefully assembled.\nParent, child, partner, former-partner, and sibling relationships must remain visually distinct without making the tree difficult to scan.\nPortraits, contact details, notes, and living-person records are sensitive, so the application stores its working archive locally and does not silently upload it.\nA"
  - id: "blog:separating-flight-rules-from-the-renderer"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Separating Flight Rules from the Renderer in Air Traffic Control”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Separating Flight Rules from the Renderer in Air Traffic Control” is framed as “The renderer should display the rules”: Air Traffic Control asks players to draw routes for airliners, commuter aircraft, and helicopters. The interaction is simple, but traffic pacing, landing checks, and collisions need a consistent model beneath the visuals. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:separating-flight-rules-from-the-renderer","blog:separating-flight-rules-from-the-renderer:simulation"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Game engineering\nHow an Android, browser, Windows, and Linux arcade game separates simulation, visible collision geometry, and local progression across short gameplay sessions.\nfalse\nAircraft colliding on their airframe outline, with simulation separated from rendering\n/images/blog/flight-rules.webp\nauthored\nproject-air-traffic-control\n2026-09-29\n3\nseparating-flight-rules-from-the-renderer\nSeparating Flight Rules from the Renderer in Air Traffic Control\nproduct","simulation\nSimulation\nAir Traffic Control asks players to draw routes for airliners, commuter aircraft, and helicopters. The interaction is simple, but traffic pacing, landing checks, and collisions need a consistent model beneath the visuals.\nThe TypeScript simulation lives outside Phaser. It advances in fixed 1/60-second steps and uses seeded traffic generation. That boundary allows tests to exercise gameplay rules without creating a rendering scene. The fixed timestep describes the simulation, not a claim that"
  - id: "blog:gym-membership-debt-cannot-be-invented-by-a-calendar"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Gym Membership Calendar Must Not Invent Debt”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Gym Membership Calendar Must Not Invent Debt” is framed as “Gyms run on paper and memory”: Liftuno is a gym-management platform I am building for a client in Bangladesh. Many local gyms track members, payments, attendance and outstanding balances in paper records or in a staff member's head. Daily work is slow, and financial history becomes ambiguous the moment anyone disagrees. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:gym-membership-debt-cannot-be-invented-by-a-calendar","blog:gym-membership-debt-cannot-be-invented-by-a-calendar:paper"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Domain modeling\nLiftuno replaces paper ledgers for Bangladeshi gyms. The core design choice: a member who stops coming has expired coverage, not debt, so the calendar never invents money owed.\nfalse\nPrepaid coverage periods on a timeline with a gap that creates no debt\n/images/blog/gym-coverage.webp\nauthored\nproject-liftuno\n2026-08-28\n3\ngym-membership-debt-cannot-be-invented-by-a-calendar\nA Gym Membership Calendar Must Not Invent Debt\nproduct","paper\nThe real problem\nLiftuno is a gym-management platform I am building for a client in Bangladesh. Many local gyms track members, payments, attendance and outstanding balances in paper records or in a staff member's head. Daily work is slow, and financial history becomes ambiguous the moment anyone disagrees.\nThe product has to support English and Bangla, Bangladeshi taka, Asia/Dhaka dates and phone-first use, with an Android client planned on the same foundation.\nGyms run on paper and memory"
  - id: "blog:web-to-android-parity-needs-an-evidence-trail"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Web-to-Android Parity Needs an Evidence Trail”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Web-to-Android Parity Needs an Evidence Trail” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Teams turning an existing web product into an Android app can silently drop routes, states, integrations, and recovery paths. This happens when implementation starts before the product's capabilities have been inventoried. Without that inventory, an app can look like the website and still be missing workflows. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:web-to-android-parity-needs-an-evidence-trail","case-study:build-android-app-from-web:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Mobile engineering\nfalse\nWeb routes traced through a parity manifest to Android screens, with one deferred\n/images/blog/android-parity.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-build-android-app-from-web\nweb-to-android-parity-needs-an-evidence-trail\nWeb-to-Android Parity Needs an Evidence Trail\ntools","Teams turning an existing web product into an Android app can silently drop routes, states, integrations, and recovery paths. This happens when implementation starts before the product's capabilities have been inventoried. Without that inventory, an app can look like the website and still be missing workflows.\nEvery web capability needs an explicit Android disposition and evidence trail.\nPlatform adaptation must preserve product behavior without forcing browser interaction patterns onto Android.\nDeferred and"
  - id: "blog:making-agent-skills-comparable"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “How I Made AI Agent Skills Comparable Without Hiding Source Drift”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “How I Made AI Agent Skills Comparable Without Hiding Source Drift” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Reusable SKILL.md capabilities are scattered across repositories. Developers have no consistent way to compare them by purpose, host compatibility, maturity, or install path, so choosing a skill means reading many repositories by hand. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:making-agent-skills-comparable","case-study:skillfoliox:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Agent engineering\nfalse\nA grid of SKILL.md cards with version and host compatibility chips\n/images/blog/skill-catalog.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-skillfoliox\nmaking-agent-skills-comparable\nHow I Made AI Agent Skills Comparable Without Hiding Source Drift\ntools","Reusable SKILL.md capabilities are scattered across repositories. Developers have no consistent way to compare them by purpose, host compatibility, maturity, or install path, so choosing a skill means reading many repositories by hand.\nCatalog facts can drift from the source repositories and package releases they describe.\nFetching README files from GitHub can fail or be rate-limited.\nMarkdown from many authors must render safely and consistently without implying that every skill has identical support."
  - id: "blog:readme-generation-must-start-with-evidence"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Why I Made README Generation Start With Evidence”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Why I Made README Generation Start With Evidence” is framed as “Generated READMEs sound right and are often wrong”: Ask an AI to improve a README and you usually get polished prose that preserves stale claims, adds features that never existed and lists commands that are not in the project. Generic sections appear because the generator never inspected the repository or separated evidence from guesswork. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:readme-generation-must-start-with-evidence","blog:readme-generation-must-start-with-evidence:confident-wrong"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Documentation systems\nAI-written READMEs are confident and often wrong. The write-complete-project-readme skill reads the project first, treats the old README as a claim to verify, and labels every fact as verified, inferred or unknown.\nfalse\nRepository files sorted into verified, inferred and unknown before a README is written\n/images/blog/readme-evidence.webp\nauthored\nproject-write-complete-project-readme\n2026-08-28\n2\nreadme-generation-must-start-with-evidence\nWhy I Made README Generation Start With","AI-first does not mean letting AI do everything. It means constraining it so it cannot invent.\nconfident-wrong\nThe real problem\nAsk an AI to improve a README and you usually get polished prose that preserves stale claims, adds features that never existed and lists commands that are not in the project. Generic sections appear because the generator never inspected the repository or separated evidence from guesswork.\nThe problem is not the model's writing. It is that we let it write without proof.\nGenerated READMEs"
  - id: "blog:no-single-source-of-truth-for-project-metadata"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “There Is No Single Source of Truth for Project Metadata”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “There Is No Single Source of Truth for Project Metadata” is framed as “Why it was needed”: A project's name, version, links, and compatibility claims are repeated across manifests, lockfiles, documentation, deployment configuration, and catalogs, and those copies drift apart. Users then follow broken links, run the wrong install command, or read conflicting compatibility claims. Finding and reconciling every copy by hand is slow and easy to get wrong. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:no-single-source-of-truth-for-project-metadata","case-study:fix-project-metadata-mismatches:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Developer tooling\nfalse\nSeveral metadata files compared field by field with one version mismatch reported\n/images/blog/metadata-sources.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-fix-project-metadata-mismatches\nno-single-source-of-truth-for-project-metadata\nThere Is No Single Source of Truth for Project Metadata\ntools","A project's name, version, links, and compatibility claims are repeated across manifests, lockfiles, documentation, deployment configuration, and catalogs, and those copies drift apart. Users then follow broken links, run the wrong install command, or read conflicting compatibility claims. Finding and reconciling every copy by hand is slow and easy to get wrong.\nEach metadata field has its own authoritative source, so no single file can be treated as the truth for everything.\nA human-facing description must match"
  - id: "blog:one-green-dashboard-does-not-prove-a-release"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Release Is Not Done Because One Dashboard Is Green”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Release Is Not Done Because One Dashboard Is Green” is framed as “Every system shows its own version of done”: After a release, GitHub shows a tag, npm shows a package, CI shows a green run, and the README shows an install command. Each looks fine on its own. Together they can disagree: a v1.2.0 tag over a package.json still at 1.1.9, an npm publish from a different commit, an install command pointing at an older version. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:one-green-dashboard-does-not-prove-a-release","blog:one-green-dashboard-does-not-prove-a-release:partial-truth"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Release engineering\nA release can look finished in one system and be wrong in another. The verify-github-npm-release skill checks Git, GitHub, npm, CI and install docs read-only, and never confuses \"missing\" with \"could not verify\".\nfalse\nGit tag, package registry, CI and install docs checked side by side for one release\n/images/blog/release-verify.webp\nauthored\nproject-verify-github-npm-release\n2026-08-28\n2\none-green-dashboard-does-not-prove-a-release\nA Release Is Not Done Because One Dashboard Is Green\ntools","A green dashboard proves one system is fine. A release is all of them agreeing.\npartial-truth\nThe real problem\nAfter a release, GitHub shows a tag, npm shows a package, CI shows a green run, and the README shows an install command. Each looks fine on its own. Together they can disagree: a v1.2.0 tag over a package.json still at 1.1.9, an npm publish from a different commit, an install command pointing at an older version.\nI built the verify-github-npm-release skill for Claude Code and Codex to check all of those"
  - id: "blog:shipping-across-npm-github-and-agent-hosts"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Shipping Across npm, GitHub, and Agent Hosts Without Pretending It Is Atomic”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Shipping Across npm, GitHub, and Agent Hosts Without Pretending It Is Atomic” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Releasing a skill spans mutable local state and several independent remote systems. That makes partial success, version drift, and accidental publication easy, and an agent told to \"ship\" can easily do more than the maintainer intended. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:shipping-across-npm-github-and-agent-hosts","case-study:release-agent-skill:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Release engineering\nfalse\nValidate, pack, publish, release and verify as separate proven stages\n/images/blog/release-stages.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-release-agent-skill\nshipping-across-npm-github-and-agent-hosts\nShipping Across npm, GitHub, and Agent Hosts Without Pretending It Is Atomic\ntools","Releasing a skill spans mutable local state and several independent remote systems. That makes partial success, version drift, and accidental publication easy, and an agent told to \"ship\" can easily do more than the maintainer intended.\nPublishing, pushing, creating releases, deploying, and changing the catalog each need separate authorization.\nCodex and Claude Code require different install destinations but must share one portable payload.\nAn immutable artifact may already exist when a later stage fails, so the"
  - id: "blog:local-build-does-not-prove-deployability"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Successful Local Build Does Not Prove a Deployable System”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Successful Local Build Does Not Prove a Deployable System” is framed as “It builds on my machine, and fails on Netlify”: A build that succeeds locally can still fail on Netlify, or worse, deploy successfully and serve the wrong thing. The usual causes are not compiler errors. They are a wrong base path in a monorepo, a wrong publish directory, a static deployment of an SSR app, a missing adapter, or environment variables that only exist on one laptop. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:local-build-does-not-prove-deployability","blog:local-build-does-not-prove-deployability:works-locally"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Platform engineering\nA green local build only proves the code compiles. Deploys fail on base paths, publish directories, runtime models and redirects, so I built an agent skill that writes the build contract down first.\nfalse\nA passing local build next to a deployment pipeline with mismatched paths\n/images/blog/build-contract.webp\nauthored\nproject-make-app-netlify-ready\n2026-08-28\n2\nlocal-build-does-not-prove-deployability\nA Successful Local Build Does Not Prove a Deployable System\ntools","works-locally\nThe real problem\nA build that succeeds locally can still fail on Netlify, or worse, deploy successfully and serve the wrong thing. The usual causes are not compiler errors. They are a wrong base path in a monorepo, a wrong publish directory, a static deployment of an SSR app, a missing adapter, or environment variables that only exist on one laptop.\nStatic sites, SPAs, hybrid apps and SSR apps each need a different routing and function contract, and Netlify adapter behaviour depends on the framework"
  - id: "blog:publishing-one-entry-without-breaking-consumers"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Publishing One Catalog Entry Without Breaking Every Consumer”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Publishing One Catalog Entry Without Breaking Every Consumer” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Adding a skill to a catalog touches several consumers that duplicate its data, such as README indexes and the sitemap. It can also import stale package or repository facts, or treat a request to \"publish\" as permission to deploy. Either mistake breaks the catalog or makes an external change nobody approved. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:publishing-one-entry-without-breaking-consumers","case-study:update-skillfolio-catalog:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Agent engineering\nfalse\nOne updated catalog entry verified against every consumer\n/images/blog/catalog-entry.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-update-skillfolio-catalog\npublishing-one-entry-without-breaking-consumers\nPublishing One Catalog Entry Without Breaking Every Consumer\ntools","Adding a skill to a catalog touches several consumers that duplicate its data, such as README indexes and the sitemap. It can also import stale package or repository facts, or treat a request to \"publish\" as permission to deploy. Either mistake breaks the catalog or makes an external change nobody approved.\nCatalog schemas and the consumers that read them vary between target apps.\nRepository, package, version, and compatibility facts must be verified against primary sources.\nEditing the local catalog does not"
  - id: "blog:a-support-widget-is-part-of-the-product-interface"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Support Widget Is Part of the Product Interface”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Support Widget Is Part of the Product Interface” is framed as “Why it was needed”: A support widget can load without errors and still clash with a site's design, mount more than once, violate the Content Security Policy, or fail inside a browser extension. Because the widget is part of the product interface, each of these problems reaches users directly. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-support-widget-is-part-of-the-product-interface","case-study:integrate-supportkori-widget:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Frontend architecture\nfalse\nOne theme-matched support trigger on a website and in an extension panel\n/images/blog/support-widget.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-integrate-supportkori-widget\na-support-widget-is-part-of-the-product-interface\nA Support Widget Is Part of the Product Interface\ntools","A support widget can load without errors and still clash with a site's design, mount more than once, violate the Content Security Policy, or fail inside a browser extension. Because the widget is part of the product interface, each of these problems reaches users directly.\nIntegration must use the repository's established SupportKori contract rather than inventing a generic embed.\nWebsites and extensions have different lifecycle, CSP, permission, and iframe boundaries.\nRemote configuration and publication remain"
  - id: "blog:deploy-the-artifact-not-the-repository"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Deploy the Artifact, Not the Repository”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Deploy the Artifact, Not the Repository” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Teams that already have a built application often deploy it through a hosting flow designed for source code. That path can trigger unnecessary rebuilds, publish the wrong directory, or upload unsafe local artifacts such as secrets and caches. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:deploy-the-artifact-not-the-repository","case-study:deploy-prebuilt-app-to-netlify:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Platform engineering\nfalse\nOnly the built dist folder passing a preflight check before deployment\n/images/blog/artifact-deploy.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-deploy-prebuilt-app-to-netlify\ndeploy-the-artifact-not-the-repository\nDeploy the Artifact, Not the Repository\ntools","Teams that already have a built application often deploy it through a hosting flow designed for source code. That path can trigger unnecessary rebuilds, publish the wrong directory, or upload unsafe local artifacts such as secrets and caches.\nThe requested artifact must be validated before any remote mutation.\nSecrets, source trees, caches, and unrelated files must never enter the deploy payload.\nSite selection, production promotion, and deployment each require separate authorization."
  - id: "blog:making-a-codebase-public-safely"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Making a Codebase Public Is a Security and Operations Change”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Making a Codebase Public Is a Security and Operations Change” is framed as “Technically ready is not publicly ready”: A codebase can build and pass tests while its Git identity, metadata, security guidance, community files, secret hygiene and CI are nowhere near ready for public collaboration. Making it public exposes all of that at once, including whatever is buried in the history. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:making-a-codebase-public-safely","blog:making-a-codebase-public-safely:not-a-button"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Platform engineering\nFlipping a repository to public is not a button. It changes your security exposure and your operations, so the make-project-github-ready skill separates local preparation from every remote change.\nfalse\nA private repository passing through security and readiness checks before becoming public\n/images/blog/repo-public.webp\nauthored\nproject-make-project-github-ready\n2026-08-28\n2\nmaking-a-codebase-public-safely\nMaking a Codebase Public Is a Security and Operations Change\ntools","Making code public is a security and operations change, not a visibility toggle.\nnot-a-button\nThe real problem\nA codebase can build and pass tests while its Git identity, metadata, security guidance, community files, secret hygiene and CI are nowhere near ready for public collaboration. Making it public exposes all of that at once, including whatever is buried in the history.\nI built the make-project-github-ready skill for Claude Code and Codex to make that transition deliberate instead of a single"
  - id: "blog:release-notes-users-can-trust"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Turning Commit History Into Release Notes Users Can Trust”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Turning Commit History Into Release Notes Users Can Trust” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Commit logs describe implementation, not user impact. Release authors working from them can omit what users need to know, invent compatibility claims, or compare against the wrong boundary, which makes the notes misleading. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:release-notes-users-can-trust","case-study:publish-verified-github-release:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Release engineering\nfalse\nCommit history filtered into release notes with breaking changes separated\n/images/blog/release-notes.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-publish-verified-github-release\nrelease-notes-users-can-trust\nTurning Commit History Into Release Notes Users Can Trust\ntools","Commit logs describe implementation, not user impact. Release authors working from them can omit what users need to know, invent compatibility claims, or compare against the wrong boundary, which makes the notes misleading.\nAmbiguity in the tag, version, or commit range can materially change what the release contains.\nOnly user-visible changes supported by commits, diffs, issues, or changelogs belong in the notes.\nCreating or editing a GitHub release is a remote change that is distinct from drafting the notes."
  - id: "blog:when-the-page-is-right-but-the-link-card-is-wrong"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “The Page Looked Right. The Link Card Was Still Broken”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “The Page Looked Right. The Link Card Was Still Broken” is framed as “The browser and the crawler read different pages”: A familiar bug report: the page looks right, but when someone shares the link on LinkedIn, the card has no image, the wrong title, or an old preview. The page is fine. The link card is not. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:when-the-page-is-right-but-the-link-card-is-wrong","blog:when-the-page-is-right-but-the-link-card-is-wrong:two-views"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Web platform\nA page can look perfect in the browser while LinkedIn or Slack show a broken card. The fix starts by separating what your code controls from what the platform caches.\nfalse\nA correct web page beside a crawler view that sees only a broken link card\n/images/blog/link-card.webp\nauthored\nproject-fix-social-link-previews\n2026-08-28\n3\nwhen-the-page-is-right-but-the-link-card-is-wrong\nThe Page Looked Right. The Link Card Was Still Broken\ntools","Test the HTML a crawler receives, not the page you see.\ntwo-views\nThe real problem\nA familiar bug report: the page looks right, but when someone shares the link on LinkedIn, the card has no image, the wrong title, or an old preview. The page is fine. The link card is not.\nThe browser runs JavaScript, carries your session and renders the final page. A link-preview crawler fetches raw HTML, does not run your app and has no login. If the metadata only exists after hydration, or the image sits behind authentication,"
  - id: "blog:audit-design-systems-before-screenshots"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Why I Audit Design Systems Before Fixing Screenshots”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Why I Audit Design Systems Before Fixing Screenshots” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Visual inconsistencies are often reported from a single screenshot, with no record of the route, build, or state it came from and nothing to compare it against. Without responsive states or a systemic root cause, a fix tends to patch one page while the underlying drift stays in place. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:audit-design-systems-before-screenshots","case-study:find-ui-inconsistencies:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Frontend architecture\nfalse\nTwo pages with mismatched button styles traced back to shared design tokens\n/images/blog/design-audit.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-find-ui-inconsistencies\naudit-design-systems-before-screenshots\nWhy I Audit Design Systems Before Fixing Screenshots\ntools","Visual inconsistencies are often reported from a single screenshot, with no record of the route, build, or state it came from and nothing to compare it against. Without responsive states or a systemic root cause, a fix tends to patch one page while the underlying drift stays in place.\nA screenshot only counts as evidence when it is tied to a route, viewport, time or build, and state.\nResponsive and accessibility behavior can only be judged across multiple widths and interaction states.\nA request for an audit does"
  - id: "blog:version-the-workflow-not-just-the-form"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Version the Workflow, Not Just the Form”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Version the Workflow, Not Just the Form” is framed as “Requests live in chat, spreadsheets and memory”: Institutions handle operational requests across chat, spreadsheets, fixed forms and verbal follow-up. The submitted information ends up separated from who owns it, where it stands and who is accountable. FormFlow is a bilingual platform where institutions define their own forms and workflows, and every request carries its history in one record. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:version-the-workflow-not-just-the-form","blog:version-the-workflow-not-just-the-form:scattered"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Multi-tenant systems\nWhen an institution edits a form or its approval workflow, older requests should keep the meaning and process they started with. FormFlow pins every submission to immutable published versions.\nfalse\nSubmissions pinned to the form and workflow versions they entered\n/images/blog/versioned-workflow.webp\nauthored\nproject-formflow\n2026-08-28\n2\nversion-the-workflow-not-just-the-form\nVersion the Workflow, Not Just the Form\nproduct","scattered\nThe real problem\nInstitutions handle operational requests across chat, spreadsheets, fixed forms and verbal follow-up. The submitted information ends up separated from who owns it, where it stands and who is accountable. FormFlow is a bilingual platform where institutions define their own forms and workflows, and every request carries its history in one record.\nThe interesting problem appears months later. An admin adds a question to a form or an approval step to a workflow. What happens to requests"
  - id: "blog:full-page-screenshots-without-cloud-capture"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Capturing the Whole Page Without Sending It to the Cloud”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Capturing the Whole Page Without Sending It to the Cloud” is framed as “Chrome only captures what is visible”: Chrome's standard screenshot flow captures the visible viewport. Many full-page tools solve that by adding heavy editors, accounts or cloud upload. I wanted one complete, annotatable image without sending the page anywhere. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:full-page-screenshots-without-cloud-capture","blog:full-page-screenshots-without-cloud-capture:viewport-only"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Browser engineering\n1Snap captures a whole web page as one PNG inside Chrome, with no upload. The hard part was not taking the frames. It was leaving the page exactly as the user had it.\nfalse\nA tall web page captured as stitched frames and assembled locally into one image\n/images/blog/full-page-capture.webp\nauthored\nproject-1snap\n2026-08-28\n2\nfull-page-screenshots-without-cloud-capture\nCapturing the Whole Page Without Sending It to the Cloud\ntools","viewport-only\nThe real problem\nChrome's standard screenshot flow captures the visible viewport. Many full-page tools solve that by adding heavy editors, accounts or cloud upload. I wanted one complete, annotatable image without sending the page anywhere.\nBecause Chrome can only capture the active visible tab, 1Snap has to scroll the page and record frame after frame while the tab stays active, then stitch them together.\nChrome only captures what is visible"
  - id: "blog:reproducible-bug-reports-without-default-surveillance"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Turning ‘It Broke’ Into Reproducible Evidence Without Default Surveillance”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Turning ‘It Broke’ Into Reproducible Evidence Without Default Surveillance” is framed as “\"It broke\" is not a bug report”: Screenshots and vague descriptions lose almost everything a developer needs: the sequence of actions, the console errors, the failed request, the environment and what the page actually looked like. Reproducing the bug becomes guesswork. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:reproducible-bug-reports-without-default-surveillance","blog:reproducible-bug-reports-without-default-surveillance:it-broke"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Privacy engineering\nBugReceipt turns \"it broke\" into a report with steps, console and network evidence and a recording. The harder design problem was deciding what never to collect.\nfalse\nCaptured browser evidence passing through a local filter and review step before export\n/images/blog/bug-evidence.webp\nauthored\nproject-bugreceipt\n2026-08-28\n2\nreproducible-bug-reports-without-default-surveillance\nTurning ‘It Broke’ Into Reproducible Evidence Without Default Surveillance\ntools","it-broke\nThe real problem\nScreenshots and vague descriptions lose almost everything a developer needs: the sequence of actions, the console errors, the failed request, the environment and what the page actually looked like. Reproducing the bug becomes guesswork.\nBugReceipt is a Chrome extension that captures reproduction steps, bounded console and network evidence, environment details and a recording or screenshot in one reviewable report.\n\"It broke\" is not a bug report"
  - id: "blog:normalizing-location-data-without-erasing-provenance"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Normalizing Bangladesh Location Data Without Erasing Provenance”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Normalizing Bangladesh Location Data Without Erasing Provenance” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Bangladesh location records are spread across government sources that each use a different structure. That makes normalized lookup, provenance review, and repeatable machine consumption difficult for anyone building on the data. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:normalizing-location-data-without-erasing-provenance","case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Data engineering\nfalse\nA division, district and upazila hierarchy with source tags and export formats\n/images/blog/location-registry.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-bangladesh-location-registry\nnormalizing-location-data-without-erasing-provenance\nNormalizing Bangladesh Location Data Without Erasing Provenance\nproduct","Bangladesh location records are spread across government sources that each use a different structure. That makes normalized lookup, provenance review, and repeatable machine consumption difficult for anyone building on the data.\nAdministrative boundaries, names, and facilities change over time.\nSource coverage and confidence vary, and neither establishes authoritative identity or address truth.\nHealth, emergency, eligibility, routing, or legal use requires confirmation from a current authoritative source."
  - id: "blog:one-platform-three-institution-types-zero-forks"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “One Education Platform, Three Institution Types, Zero Codebase Forks”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “One Education Platform, Three Institution Types, Zero Codebase Forks” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Education organizations need familiar public information pages and publishing workflows. Cloning a whole codebase for each institution creates drift between the copies and makes every fix costly to maintain. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:one-platform-three-institution-types-zero-forks","case-study:educanvas:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Application architecture\nfalse\nOne codebase producing three differently themed institution websites\n/images/blog/one-codebase-three-themes.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-educanvas\none-platform-three-institution-types-zero-forks\nOne Education Platform, Three Institution Types, Zero Codebase Forks\nproduct","Education organizations need familiar public information pages and publishing workflows. Cloning a whole codebase for each institution creates drift between the copies and makes every fix costly to maintain.\nThree institution types and multiple layouts must share behavior without leaking content or theme between them.\nMongoDB, Google OAuth and Drive, and Resend are optional services that each deployment has to configure.\nPublic pages must stay usable on read-only defaults when database-backed administration is"
  - id: "blog:company-research-without-a-fake-score"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “How I Modeled Company Research Without Producing a Fake Score”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “How I Modeled Company Research Without Producing a Fake Score” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:company-research-without-a-fake-score","case-study:b4joinacompany:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Evidence systems\ntrue\nReported, submitted, official and unknown evidence with a crossed-out company score\n/images/blog/company-research.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-b4joinacompany\ncompany-research-without-a-fake-score\nHow I Modeled Company Research Without Producing a Fake Score\ncareer","Job seekers in Bangladesh often assemble company research from workplace stories, salary submissions, official pages, and interview conversations. Those sources have different levels of reliability, but conventional summaries can flatten them into an unfair good-or-bad judgment.\nPersonal workplace reports provide context, not verified statements of current company policy.\nCompany names and salary records can be ambiguous, so fuzzy matching cannot silently publish a result.\nAI answers must remain bounded by"
  - id: "blog:scheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Scheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Scheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:scheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab","case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Distributed systems\ntrue\nScheduled posts moving from a closed browser tab to a durable queue that publishes later\n/images/blog/durable-queue.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-postcraft\nscheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab\nScheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab\nproduct","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "blog:keeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “I Kept Tax-ID Lookups Out of the Server”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “I Kept Tax-ID Lookups Out of the Server” is framed as “A useful list in an unusable format”: A list circulated as the NBR risk-based audit selection for assessment year 2023-24, covering more than 72,000 returns. Searching a static list for one TIN is painful, especially on a phone, so I built a focused lookup. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:keeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server","blog:keeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server:the-list"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Privacy engineering\nChecking whether a TIN was on a published audit list should not mean sending that TIN to someone's server. The lookup runs entirely in the browser, and the tool is explicit about what that does and does not protect.\nfalse\nA tax ID lookup running entirely inside the browser with the server unaware of the query\n/images/blog/tin-browser.webp\nauthored\nproject-tin-audit-checker\n2026-08-28\n2\nkeeping-tax-id-lookups-out-of-the-server\nI Kept Tax-ID Lookups Out of the Server\nproduct","Before sending data to a server, ask whether you need to see it at all.\nthe-list\nThe real problem\nA list circulated as the NBR risk-based audit selection for assessment year 2023-24, covering more than 72,000 returns. Searching a static list for one TIN is painful, especially on a phone, so I built a focused lookup.\nThe obvious design is an API: the user types a TIN, the server searches and responds. That design also means every person's tax identifier lands in my server logs. I did not need to see anyone's TIN,"
  - id: "blog:fifty-developer-tools-without-fifty-architectures"
