    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “How I Scaled 50+ Developer Utilities Without 50 Architectures” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:fifty-developer-tools-without-fifty-architectures","case-study:devtools:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Frontend architecture\nfalse\nFifty developer tools sharing one searchable shell\n/images/blog/devtools-shell.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-devtools\nfifty-developer-tools-without-fifty-architectures\nHow I Scaled 50+ Developer Utilities Without 50 Architectures\ntools","Small development tasks often send people across unrelated single-purpose websites. Each tool has a different interface, unclear privacy behavior, and no shared path for search, history, saved work, or reuse.\nMany transformations should stay in the browser, while network diagnostics necessarily contact a target or server endpoint.\nA catalog with more than 50 workflows must remain quick to search, navigate, and extend without duplicating page infrastructure.\nSaved items, shares, webhooks, and short links introduce"
  - id: "blog:http-status-codes-are-data"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “HTTP Status Codes Are Data, Not a Switch Statement”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “HTTP Status Codes Are Data, Not a Switch Statement” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Developers need status-code names, categories, and lifecycle metadata. Without a library they fall back on memorized numeric checks or hand-written lists that go stale, and many alternatives bring a heavier dependency than the task needs. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:http-status-codes-are-data","case-study:http-status-lite:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Library design\nfalse\nHTTP status codes laid out as data, with obsolete and unused codes marked\n/images/blog/http-status.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-http-status-lite\nhttp-status-codes-are-data\nHTTP Status Codes Are Data, Not a Switch Statement\ntools","Developers need status-code names, categories, and lifecycle metadata. Without a library they fall back on memorized numeric checks or hand-written lists that go stale, and many alternatives bring a heavier dependency than the task needs.\nThe IANA registry changes over time.\nObsolete, temporary, and unused codes must remain distinguishable.\nTyping and ESM and CommonJS compatibility must not depend on live network access."
  - id: "blog:dynamic-readme-svgs-without-stored-images"
