    factIds[2]: "blog:dynamic-readme-svgs-without-stored-images","case-study:markdown-typing-svg:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "API design\nfalse\nA URL query rendered on demand into an animated typing SVG, with an error SVG fallback\n/images/blog/typing-svg.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-markdown-typing-svg\ndynamic-readme-svgs-without-stored-images\nGenerating Dynamic README SVGs Without Storing a Single Image\ntools","Developers who want a typing animation in a GitHub profile or README usually have to edit SVG and CSS by hand and then host the result somewhere. That makes it slow to experiment with text, timing, or styling, and hard to share or reproduce a design someone else made.\nPublic embeds depend on the deployment staying available, because every image is generated on request.\nSuccessful responses are cached for 24 hours, so an updated design may not appear in an existing embed until the cache expires.\nThere is no"
  - id: "blog:prayer-time-software-needs-provenance"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Prayer-Time Software Needs Provenance, Not False Precision”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Prayer-Time Software Needs Provenance, Not False Precision” is framed as “There is no single correct prayer time”: Ramadan Clock shows district-aware Sehri and Iftar schedules for Bangladesh. It sounds like a lookup table. It is not. Calculated times depend on the source, the calculation method, the exact location and the local authority people follow. Two respectable sources can disagree by minutes. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:prayer-time-software-needs-provenance","blog:prayer-time-software-needs-provenance:no-single-truth"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Data engineering\nSehri and Iftar times depend on the source, method and location. Ramadan Clock shows where each time comes from instead of pretending to a precision it cannot have.\nfalse\nDaily Sehri and Iftar times traced back to their source and calculation method\n/images/blog/prayer-provenance.webp\nauthored\nproject-ramadan-clock\n2026-08-28\n2\nprayer-time-software-needs-provenance\nPrayer-Time Software Needs Provenance, Not False Precision\nproduct","For a schedule people rely on, provenance is more honest than precision.\nno-single-truth\nThe real problem\nRamadan Clock shows district-aware Sehri and Iftar schedules for Bangladesh. It sounds like a lookup table. It is not. Calculated times depend on the source, the calculation method, the exact location and the local authority people follow. Two respectable sources can disagree by minutes.\nAn app that shows one time to the second, with no context, implies a certainty it does not have. For something people plan"
