  - id: "blog:one-attendance-model-for-multiple-biometric-vendors"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “One Attendance Model for Multiple Biometric Vendors”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “One Attendance Model for Multiple Biometric Vendors” is framed as “Institutes run on disconnected tools”: Coaching institutes often split student records, class structures, attendance devices, fee collection, expenses and SMS across separate tools. The client platform I am building brings them into one multi-tenant system, where each organisation must never see or change another organisation's data. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:one-attendance-model-for-multiple-biometric-vendors","blog:one-attendance-model-for-multiple-biometric-vendors:disconnected"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Multi-tenant systems\nA coaching platform had to accept attendance from two biometric vendors with different protocols. One internal device contract and an adapter per vendor kept hardware quirks out of the business logic.\nfalse\nTwo different biometric devices feeding one attendance model through adapters\n/images/blog/biometric-adapters.webp\nauthored\nproject-coaching-management\n2026-08-28\n3\none-attendance-model-for-multiple-biometric-vendors\nOne Attendance Model for Multiple Biometric Vendors\nproduct","disconnected\nThe real problem\nCoaching institutes often split student records, class structures, attendance devices, fee collection, expenses and SMS across separate tools. The client platform I am building brings them into one multi-tenant system, where each organisation must never see or change another organisation's data.\nAttendance was the hardest integration. Students and teachers share physical biometric devices, and the institutes use devices from two vendors, Steller and Tipsoi, with different protocols"
