    factIds[2]: "blog:video-qa-without-processing-video","case-study:vidquery:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "AI engineering\nfalse\nA video transcript answering a viewer's question without downloading the video\n/images/blog/video-qa.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-vidquery\nvideo-qa-without-processing-video\nI Stopped Treating Video Q&A as a Media-Processing Problem\ntools","Viewers often scrub through long YouTube videos to find one specific answer. The page already exposes metadata and a transcript that could answer the question directly. Without a way to query that context, the viewer spends time searching instead of learning.\nAnswer quality is limited by whether a transcript exists and how accurate it is.\nGemini calls require the user's own API key and send bounded video context to an external provider.\nChanges to YouTube routes or page structure, along with extension permission"
  - id: "blog:designing-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “User-Agent Parsing Is Heuristic. I Designed the API to Admit It”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “User-Agent Parsing Is Heuristic. I Designed the API to Admit It” is framed as “Every User-Agent parser is guessing”: Analytics and diagnostics need a normalised view of which browser, OS, device or bot made a request. The raw material is a User-Agent string that any client can change, plus Client Hints whose availability varies and may be restricted for privacy. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:designing-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty","blog:designing-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty:heuristic"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Library design\nUser-Agent parsing is a guess that anyone can spoof. client-parser combines User-Agent and Client Hints into one typed result, and its API says plainly that the result is for analytics, not decisions.\nfalse\nA spoofable user-agent string and client hints merged into one labelled best guess\n/images/blog/user-agent.webp\nauthored\nproject-client-parser\n2026-08-28\n2\ndesigning-an-api-that-admits-user-agent-uncertainty\nUser-Agent Parsing Is Heuristic. I Designed the API to Admit It\ntools","A User-Agent string is a claim, not a fact.\nheuristic\nThe real problem\nAnalytics and diagnostics need a normalised view of which browser, OS, device or bot made a request. The raw material is a User-Agent string that any client can change, plus Client Hints whose availability varies and may be restricted for privacy.\nMany parsers are heavy, and many APIs quietly encourage using the result for things it cannot support, such as gating features or making security decisions.\nEvery User-Agent parser is guessing"
