    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Filename-to-MIME Lookup Is Useful—Until You Mistake It for Validation” is framed as “Why it was needed”: Upload, download, and HTTP code needs dependable mappings between filenames or extensions and media types. Carrying a broad MIME database into every bundle for that job adds weight most applications do not need. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:mime-lookup-is-not-content-validation","case-study:mime-types-lite:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Library design\nfalse\nTwo PDF filenames mapped to the same MIME type while file bytes stay uninspected\n/images/blog/mime-lookup.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-mime-types-lite\nmime-lookup-is-not-content-validation\nFilename-to-MIME Lookup Is Useful—Until You Mistake It for Validation\ntools","Upload, download, and HTTP code needs dependable mappings between filenames or extensions and media types. Carrying a broad MIME database into every bundle for that job adds weight most applications do not need.\nOne extension can have aliases or ambiguous mappings.\nLookup must normalize case, dots, and paths without implying that file content was inspected.\nESM and CommonJS consumers need stable typed exports with no runtime dependencies."
  - id: "blog:a-merged-pull-request-is-not-a-deployed-feature"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Merged Pull Request Is Not a Deployed Feature”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Merged Pull Request Is Not a Deployed Feature” is framed as “The feature looked missing, but the code was right”: The task was small: add the SupportKori support widget to Book Heaven. The integration lived in the root layout as a Next.js Script component with the exact script URL, account ID, message, colour and position. It was reviewed and merged to main. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-merged-pull-request-is-not-a-deployed-feature","blog:a-merged-pull-request-is-not-a-deployed-feature:looked-missing"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Release engineering\nThe SupportKori widget was correct and merged, but visitors never saw it. The fault was in the delivery path, and fixing it meant treating merge, build, deploy and live HTML as four separate proofs.\nfalse\nA merged branch stopping at a broken build stage before it reaches production\n/images/blog/merged-is-not-deployed.webp\nauthored\nproject-book-heaven\n2026-08-28\n3\na-merged-pull-request-is-not-a-deployed-feature\nA Merged Pull Request Is Not a Deployed Feature\nproduct","A merged pull request proves the code was accepted. It does not prove anyone can see it.\nlooked-missing\nThe symptom\nThe task was small: add the SupportKori support widget to Book Heaven. The integration lived in the root layout as a Next.js Script component with the exact script URL, account ID, message, colour and position. It was reviewed and merged to main.\nThen someone opened the live site and the widget was not there. The natural reaction is to reopen the frontend code and look for a bug. That would have been"
  - id: "blog:a-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A README Counter Measures Requests, Not People”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A README Counter Measures Requests, Not People” is framed as “Markdown cannot run code, so the counter had to be an image”: A GitHub README cannot run JavaScript or call an API and render the result. If you want a number that changes, the only thing Markdown will embed is an image URL. So the counter is a small Express service that increments a named counter on each request and returns the current value as an SVG badge. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people","blog:a-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people:why-svg"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "API design\nA README visitor badge cannot count visitors. It counts requests from bots, caches, image proxies and refreshes, so I named it honestly and designed around what it really measures.\nfalse\nBots, caches and refreshes all feeding one request counter badge\n/images/blog/readme-counter.webp\nauthored\nproject-github-readme-counter\n2026-08-28\n2\na-readme-counter-measures-requests-not-people\nA README Counter Measures Requests, Not People\ntools","why-svg\nThe constraint\nA GitHub README cannot run JavaScript or call an API and render the result. If you want a number that changes, the only thing Markdown will embed is an image URL. So the counter is a small Express service that increments a named counter on each request and returns the current value as an SVG badge.\nSVG keeps the response tiny and customisable: colours, label and style come from the request, and all user-supplied text is escaped before it goes into the markup.\nMarkdown cannot run code, so the"
  - id: "blog:human-review-needs-a-geometry-domain"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Human Review of AI Measurements Needs a Geometry Domain”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Human Review of AI Measurements Needs a Geometry Domain” is framed as “AI measurements need a human correction path”: At MyMedicalHub, automated movement analysis places lines and angles on recorded frames to measure range of motion. Sometimes it chooses an imperfect frame or a slightly wrong anatomical position. A clinical reviewer then has to pick the right frame, correct the lines and angles, compare against reference guidance, and save a revised measurement without losing the exercise context. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:human-review-needs-a-geometry-domain","blog:human-review-needs-a-geometry-domain:ai-is-imperfect"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Clinical systems\nWhen AI places a clinical measurement on the wrong frame, a reviewer has to correct it precisely. That only became reliable once geometry, workflow and data validation stopped living inside the canvas.\nfalse\nA movement frame with measurement lines and an angle being corrected by hand\n/images/blog/geometry-review.webp\nauthored\nproject-mmh-re-annotation\n2026-08-28\n3\nhuman-review-needs-a-geometry-domain\nHuman Review of AI Measurements Needs a Geometry Domain\nproduct","If the tool for correcting AI is itself ambiguous, people end up trusting the AI more, not less.\nai-is-imperfect\nThe real problem\nAt MyMedicalHub, automated movement analysis places lines and angles on recorded frames to measure range of motion. Sometimes it chooses an imperfect frame or a slightly wrong anatomical position. A clinical reviewer then has to pick the right frame, correct the lines and angles, compare against reference guidance, and save a revised measurement without losing the exercise context.\nIn"
  - id: "blog:a-video-call-is-not-a-clinical-workflow"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Video Call Is Not a Clinical Workflow”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Video Call Is Not a Clinical Workflow” is framed as “The video was the easy part”: At MyMedicalHub, patients do guided movement assessments with a provider over a live session. On the surface it looks like a video call. In practice, both sides must stay synchronised while exercises change, instructions play, recordings start and stop, pain or strength questions appear, network quality shifts, and either participant can leave at any moment. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-video-call-is-not-a-clinical-workflow","blog:a-video-call-is-not-a-clinical-workflow:more-than-video"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Real-time systems\nRemote movement assessment needs more than video. Exercise state, recordings and either participant leaving at any moment all have to be explicit product states, or patients end up stuck.\nfalse\nTwo participant screens connected through a sequence of explicit session states\n/images/blog/clinical-workflow.webp\nauthored\nproject-mmh-telemedicine\n2026-08-28\n3\na-video-call-is-not-a-clinical-workflow\nA Video Call Is Not a Clinical Workflow\nproduct","more-than-video\nThe real problem\nAt MyMedicalHub, patients do guided movement assessments with a provider over a live session. On the surface it looks like a video call. In practice, both sides must stay synchronised while exercises change, instructions play, recordings start and stop, pain or strength questions appear, network quality shifts, and either participant can leave at any moment.\nCamera, microphone, permissions and WebRTC lifecycle events also behave differently across browsers and patient devices. A"
  - id: "blog:separating-surveys-from-completed-field-records"
    category: blog
    question: What engineering insight does Montasim share in “Separating Surveys from Completed Field Records”?
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “Separating Surveys from Completed Field Records” is framed as “Why it was needed”: School-dropout research needs a consistent way to organize survey definitions and completed field records. Separate resources make those responsibilities explicit while account-related modules provide a foundation for managing access. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
