  - id: "blog:a-routine-is-not-a-timestamp"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “A Routine Is Not a Timestamp: Designing Routempo for Real-World Time”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “A Routine Is Not a Timestamp: Designing Routempo for Real-World Time” is framed as “A recurring task was the wrong domain model”: Routempo started with a familiar product idea: let people define repeatable routines, remind them at the right time, and show their progress. The interface looked like a scheduling problem. The data quickly proved otherwise. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:a-routine-is-not-a-timestamp","blog:a-routine-is-not-a-timestamp:false-model"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "System design\nThe hard part of a routine planner was not recurrence. It was preserving user intent across timezones, actual outcomes, reminders, and external calendars.\nfalse\nA clock with timezones beside planned routines and recorded outcomes\n/images/blog/routine-time.webp\nauthored\nproject-routempo\n2026-08-22\n7\na-routine-is-not-a-timestamp\nA Routine Is Not a Timestamp: Designing Routempo for Real-World Time\nproduct","Time was not a formatting concern. It was part of the product's domain language.\nfalse-model\nThe real problem\nRoutempo started with a familiar product idea: let people define repeatable routines, remind them at the right time, and show their progress. The interface looked like a scheduling problem. The data quickly proved otherwise.\nA schedule records intention. It does not tell me whether the person started, completed, skipped, or changed the routine. If I store all of that on one recurring record, every edit"
  - id: "blog:the-linkedin-page-is-untrusted-input"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “The LinkedIn Page Is Untrusted Input: Building a Safer AI Writing Assistant”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “The LinkedIn Page Is Untrusted Input: Building a Safer AI Writing Assistant” is framed as “The assistant needed context without surrendering control”: Thoughtline helps a person understand a visible LinkedIn conversation and shape a reply or post in their own voice. Generating text was the easy demo. Building a browser extension I could trust with page content, API credentials, writing history, and provider failures was the actual project. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:the-linkedin-page-is-untrusted-input","blog:the-linkedin-page-is-untrusted-input:context-not-control"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "AI engineering\nThoughtline became reliable when I treated page content, model providers, browser permissions, and local credentials as separate trust boundaries.\ntrue\nLinkedIn page content sealed in an untrusted, bounded envelope before four reply drafts\n/images/blog/untrusted-envelope.webp\nauthored\nproject-thoughtline\n2026-08-21\n8\nthe-linkedin-page-is-untrusted-input\nThe LinkedIn Page Is Untrusted Input: Building a Safer AI Writing Assistant\ntools","An AI feature starts at a trust boundary, not at the model call.\ncontext-not-control\nThe real problem\nThoughtline helps a person understand a visible LinkedIn conversation and shape a reply or post in their own voice. Generating text was the easy demo. Building a browser extension I could trust with page content, API credentials, writing history, and provider failures was the actual project.\nThe assistant needs enough context to be useful, but the LinkedIn page is neither my database nor a trusted instruction"
  - id: "blog:i-refused-to-make-webgl-the-only-way-in"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “I Refused to Make WebGL the Only Way In: The Architecture Behind Foliofarer”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “I Refused to Make WebGL the Only Way In: The Architecture Behind Foliofarer” is framed as “A memorable portfolio can still hide the information that matters”: Foliofarer presents a career as an explorable 3D world. That makes for a strong first impression, but the product has a more important job: help a visitor understand my work. A recruiter should not need gaming instincts, a powerful GPU, precise pointer control, or a tolerance for motion to reach the evidence. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:i-refused-to-make-webgl-the-only-way-in","blog:i-refused-to-make-webgl-the-only-way-in:memorable-is-not-usable"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Frontend architecture\nA 3D portfolio can be memorable and still fail its users. I designed Foliofarer so content, navigation, performance, and testing never depended on one renderer.\nfalse\nA layered 3D world shown as equal to a plain accessible Atlas document\n/images/blog/accessible-atlas.webp\nauthored\nproject-foliofarer\n2026-08-20\n7\ni-refused-to-make-webgl-the-only-way-in\nI Refused to Make WebGL the Only Way In: The Architecture Behind Foliofarer\nproduct","Progressive enhancement only works when the enhanced layer is not the sole source of truth.\nmemorable-is-not-usable\nThe real problem\nFoliofarer presents a career as an explorable 3D world. That makes for a strong first impression, but the product has a more important job: help a visitor understand my work. A recruiter should not need gaming instincts, a powerful GPU, precise pointer control, or a tolerance for motion to reach the evidence.\nThe first architectural risk was obvious. If WebGL owned the content model,"
  - id: "blog:from-useeffect-chaos-to-deterministic-systems"
    category: blog
    question: "What engineering insight does Montasim share in “From useEffect Chaos to Deterministic Systems: Why I Rebuilt Our Biometric Pipeline with a Finite State Machine”?"
    text: "Montasim's opening insight in “From useEffect Chaos to Deterministic Systems: Why I Rebuilt Our Biometric Pipeline with a Finite State Machine” is framed as “From useEffect chaos to deterministic systems”: I deleted 40+ useEffects and replaced them with a Finite State Machine. The useful hiring signal is not the feature itself, but his ability to explain the constraint, trade-off, and engineering judgment behind it in a form another developer can challenge or apply."
    factIds[2]: "blog:from-useeffect-chaos-to-deterministic-systems","blog:from-useeffect-chaos-to-deterministic-systems:introduction"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "Architecture\nI deleted 40+ useEffects and replaced them with a Finite State Machine. That was the best decision I made for our biometric assessment pipeline.\nfalse\nA deterministic state machine coordinating a real-time biometric assessment pipeline\n/images/blog/deterministic-systems.webp\nauthored\n2026-05-16\n3\nfrom-useeffect-chaos-to-deterministic-systems\nFrom useEffect Chaos to Deterministic Systems: Why I Rebuilt Our Biometric Pipeline with a Finite State Machine\nproduct","introduction\nIntroduction\nI deleted 40+ useEffects and replaced them with a Finite State Machine.\nThat was the best decision I made for our biometric assessment pipeline.\nOur movement assessment system runs real-time computer vision (MediaPipe Pose, TensorFlow.js MoveNet), WebRTC video recording, and Azure text-to-speech audio guidance — all simultaneously in the browser.\nFrom useEffect chaos to deterministic systems"
