    factIds[4]: profile,"experience:experience-mymedicalhub-senior-software-engineer","experience:experience-mymedicalhub-software-engineer","case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "I started as a junior engineer at MyMedicalHub International, and **4+ years** later I lead a team of **3 engineers** building a HIPAA-compliant platform that **1,000+ patients and clinicians** rely on. I am proudest that **99.9%** of their AI assessment sessions now finish without a crash, and that we got there while cutting our cloud bill by **70%**.\n\nHealthcare is where I work, but it is not the only thing I build. I have also shipped **education**, **fitness**, **customer messaging** and **AI creator**","employment\nMyMedicalHub International Ltd.\nhttps://mymedicalhub.com\nLead a team of **3 engineers** and own frontend architecture across **3 microservices** of a HIPAA-compliant telemedicine and biometric-assessment platform used by **1,000+ patients and clinicians**. Replaced fragile React-hook state with a deterministic **finite-state-machine** engine, so **99.9%** of assessment sessions complete without a crash or freeze, with **60 FPS** MediaPipe pose estimation and real-time rep counting. Built a **Konva.js**","employment\nMyMedicalHub International Ltd.\nhttps://mymedicalhub.com\nOwned the biometric strength-assessment module end to end and led cloud cost and infrastructure work across **4 environments**. Cut cloud hosting cost **70%** (about **$10K a year**) by moving from self-managed VMs to **Azure App Service** with GitHub Actions CI/CD, and improved application performance **40%** to **Lighthouse 90+** by replacing 5 legacy UI libraries. Designed a **feature-flag system** and API-driven theming that serve **250+","The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,"
  - id: "hiring-fit-due-diligence:product-engineering-fit"
    category: hiring-fit-due-diligence
    question: Does Montasim show product thinking as well as technical depth?
    text: "Yes. His case studies frame problems in terms of user control, trust, privacy, operating constraints, and maintainability. Examples include keeping LinkedIn publishing manual in Thoughtline, keeping tax-ID lookup client-side, and designing scheduled publishing so delivery survives beyond an open browser request."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:thoughtline:problem","case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture","case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while keeping the author's own voice, intent, and control. The page is mutable third-party content, so passing it straight into a prompt would mix data, instructions, and potentially hostile text. An assistant that drafts well but cannot be trusted with page content and credentials is not useful.\nLinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before it enters any prompt.\nOpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all","A responsive Next.js page accepts a TIN and shows the match state with its mapped details.\nLookup interface\nThe static JSON list is fetched once per session and kept in memory for every later search.\nSession dataset\nInput is stripped of formatting characters and compared by exact digits, with no TIN submission API behind it.\nClient-side matching\nA Next.js page fetches the static JSON dataset once per session and caches it in memory. Each search normalizes the input and runs an exact match in the browser, and there","The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,"
  - id: "hiring-fit-due-diligence:reliability-fit"
    category: hiring-fit-due-diligence
    question: Why would Montasim suit a reliability-sensitive product?
    text: "His portfolio repeatedly makes failure behavior explicit: a 99.9%-reliable FSM biometric engine, resilient async-workflow principles, read-only release verification, provider trust boundaries, and durable scheduled publishing. That record shows reliability as an architectural concern rather than an after-the-fact test phase."
