  - id: "technical-depth:developer-tool-scale"
    category: technical-depth
    question: How did Montasim avoid building a separate architecture for every DevTools utility?
    text: "The DevTools case study defines shared contracts for tool metadata, validation, execution, state, and presentation, while keeping local transformations distinct from server-backed features. That lets more than 50 workflows share platform behavior without forcing unrelated tools into one implementation."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-devtools","case-study:devtools:architecture","case-study:devtools:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A suite of 50+ free developer tools for formatting, validating, generating and transforming data, where local tools keep your input in the browser by default.\n\ntrue\n2026-04-08T05:59:03Z\n88e28b53c9c618f016edbaada4918442f070d25e\n2026-04-12T09:19:56Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/devtools\nproject-devtools\nassets/projects/devtools.png\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app\nhttps://devtoolsn.vercel.app/icon.svg?icon.0qz0rw08sizuj.svg\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nCodeMirror 6\nTanStack Query\nPostgreSQL\nPrisma\nBetter Auth\nTailwind","A navigation registry defines the searchable routes, labels, and keyboard discovery for every tool.\nTool registry\nFocused modules implement the formatters, generators, testers, inspectors, and references.\nFeature modules\nLocal transforms, preferences, input history, and reusable state stay in the browser.\nBrowser boundary\nNext.js APIs, Better Auth, PostgreSQL, Prisma, and target services handle the work that has to leave the browser.\nServer boundary\nA navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool","More than 50 workflows are available through one catalog and command-search model.\nCommon transformations can run without an account and without sending their current input to the server.\nServer-backed features add reuse and sharing without obscuring when data leaves the browser.\nThe architecture supports both small local utilities and stateful workflows without forcing them through one execution path."
