  - id: "catalog-chronology-comparison:evidence-relationship"
    category: catalog-chronology-comparison
    question: "How do Montasim's projects, case studies, and blog articles relate?"
    text: "A project record identifies the product and stack; its case study explains problem, constraints, architecture, contribution, and outcomes; a project-linked blog article presents that same evidence as a readable engineering narrative. The layers are related views, not three independent accomplishments."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:architecture","blog:scheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,","Distributed systems\ntrue\nScheduled posts moving from a closed browser tab to a durable queue that publishes later\n/images/blog/durable-queue.webp\ncase-study-derived\nproject-postcraft\nscheduled-publishing-cannot-depend-on-an-open-tab\nScheduled Publishing Cannot Depend on an Open Browser Tab\nproduct"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:end-to-end-delivery"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What evidence shows Montasim can deliver a product end to end?
    text: "Montasim's case studies trace delivery from a defined problem through constraints, architecture, implementation, and outcomes across AI SaaS, extensions, data products, packages, and developer tools. PostCraft is a strong full-path example: he handled generation, brand controls, previews, scheduling, durable delivery, and provider integration. For a client, that is evidence he can reason about the whole product lifecycle rather than hand off responsibility at the UI boundary."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:postcraft:contribution","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","Defined the end-to-end product flow from topic input to platform-ready output.\nBuilt the Next.js interface, API routes, domain services, and MongoDB repositories.\nImplemented multi-provider AI selection, reusable guardrails, scoring, and platform previews.\nIntegrated authentication, account recovery, social OAuth, Inngest scheduling, and publishing records.","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:client-needs"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: Has Montasim worked directly from client requirements?
    text: "Yes. As a Web Developer at Codez, Montasim analyzed client needs, implemented the appropriate functionality, and created and executed test scenarios. His later case studies show the same skill at greater depth by turning user and business constraints into explicit technical boundaries. A prospective client can ask him to apply that process to their users, risks, integrations, and definition of success."
    factIds[2]: "experience:experience-codez-web-developer","case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "employment\nCodez Info Tech.\n\nBuilt and maintained websites and web applications for **2 client projects** using **PHP, JavaScript and MySQL**. Turned client requirements into scoped features, and wrote and ran **test scenarios** before each release.\nexperience-codez-web-developer\nBadda, Dhaka, Bangladesh\nC\n/images/organizations/codez.png\nMay 2021 - Nov 2021\nWeb Developer\nPHP\nJavaScript\nMySQL\nHTML\nCSS","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:publishing-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What product judgment does PostCraft demonstrate?
    text: "PostCraft treats social publishing as a lifecycle rather than a prompt box: one idea moves through generation, brand controls, platform previews, review, scheduling, and durable delivery. The architecture separates interactive work from background execution and keeps delivery status reviewable."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:architecture","case-study:postcraft:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,","One workspace now covers generation, comparison, refinement, preview, and delivery.\nProvider and key fallback reduce the workflow's dependency on a single AI endpoint.\nPublishing state is persisted for library and insights views instead of ending at generation.\nOperational limits remain explicit: publishing still depends on platform approval, scopes, account type, and API access."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:privacy-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What client-side privacy decision did Montasim make for tax-ID lookup?
    text: "For TIN Audit Checker, Montasim kept the 72,342-record lookup in the browser so the queried identifier did not need to reach an application server. The case study scopes the product to a historical list and avoids implying that a match is a current legal determination."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-tin-audit-checker","case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture","case-study:tin-audit-checker:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A private, browser-only lookup for checking whether a Bangladesh TIN appears on a historical NBR risk-based audit selection list for AY 2023-24, covering 72,342 returns.\n\nfalse\n2026-04-30T09:35:36Z\n7d915fdb881c99faea75b5ce829d15dce17359ba\n2026-04-30T11:59:39Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/tin-audit-checker\nproject-tin-audit-checker\nassets/projects/tin-audit-checker.png\nhttps://tin-audit-checker.netlify.app\nhttps://tin-audit-checker.netlify.app/favicon.ico?favicon.0x3dzn~oxb6tn.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind","A responsive Next.js page accepts a TIN and shows the match state with its mapped details.\nLookup interface\nThe static JSON list is fetched once per session and kept in memory for every later search.\nSession dataset\nInput is stripped of formatting characters and compared by exact digits, with no TIN submission API behind it.\nClient-side matching\nA Next.js page fetches the static JSON dataset once per session and caches it in memory. Each search normalizes the input and runs an exact match in the browser, and there","Users can search the historical list quickly, including on mobile.\nTyped TINs are never submitted to an application API.\nA match returns only the bounded details mapped from the list.\nBoth matches and non-matches tell the user to confirm their status with current official sources."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:job-seeker-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How did Montasim design company research without unfair scoring?
    text: "b4joinacompany keeps workplace stories, salary submissions, official sources, and interview evidence labeled by provenance instead of flattening them into a good-or-bad company score. That gives job seekers useful context while making uncertainty and source quality visible."
    factIds[2]: "project:project-b4joinacompany","case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "An evidence-labeled company research tool that helps job seekers prepare before they apply, interview or accept an offer.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-26T04:31:13Z\n9849284bf1ac0b399c7b9d9f59bbbb9b36ba9629\n2026-07-26T04:30:07Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/b4joinacompany\nproject-b4joinacompany\nassets/projects/b4joinacompany.png\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app\nhttps://b4joinacompany.netlify.app/icon.svg?icon.10kda_g1712l_.svg\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nGemini API\nGroq API\nBetter Auth\nREST","Versioned records hold company, story, comment, and salary evidence.\nSource releases\nDeterministic rules handle identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking.\nResearch layer\nNext.js serves research briefs, compare, and Ask, alongside a versioned REST API.\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB store private checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions.\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:configurable-platform"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How did Montasim avoid separate codebases for different education sites?
    text: "EduCanvas separates institution theme and content configuration from shared publishing, identity, and delivery workflows. The result supports multiple institution types without maintaining a forked application for each client, reducing drift while preserving controlled variation."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-educanvas","case-study:educanvas:architecture","case-study:educanvas:outcomes"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A theme-driven education website and content-management starter built with Next.js.\n\nfalse\n2026-07-27T17:41:53Z\nf0ea922b85f28025d1e3bf81d5a9b4ff413cda56\n2026-07-27T17:50:31Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/EduCanvas\nproject-educanvas\nassets/projects/educanvas.png\n\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind CSS\nMongoDB\nBetter Auth\nGoogle Drive API\nResend\nREST APIs\nRadix UI\nPlaywright\nSystem Design\nEduCanvas\ncontent-management\neducation\nnextjs\ntailwindcss\ntypescript\nwebsite-starter\ntemplate","Next.js routes serve both the public site and the admin area.\nApplication shell\nFeature-owned domain modules handle content, admissions, and media.\nContent services\nCentralized institution and visual-theme configuration supplies the tokens that give each deployment its identity.\nInstitution themes\nMongoDB, authentication, Google Drive, and email adapters run behind an enforced server boundary.\nIntegrations\nNext.js serves both public and admin routes, which call feature-owned modules for content, admissions, and","Developers can deploy one starter for three institution categories.\nAdministrators can publish content and manage enquiries.\nVisitors see the configured branding and layout on shared routes.\nDeployments can fall back to read-only repository defaults where documented."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:release-confidence"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How could Montasim improve delivery confidence for a client project?
    text: "His release-oriented agent skills validate builds, packages, tags, CI, install instructions, metadata, deployments, and public documentation as separate verifiable states. That approach makes partial failure visible and reduces the risk of declaring a release complete based on one system alone."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:release-agent-skill:architecture","case-study:verify-github-npm-release:outcomes","case-study:make-app-netlify-ready:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "SKILL.md orchestrates the release stages the agent follows and the authorization each one needs.\nSkill workflow\nDeterministic references define the preflight checks and release gates that must pass before publication.\nAutomation\nAn npm installer, a package contract, and consumer-side tests confirm what users actually receive.\nVerification\nA SKILL.md file guides the agent through each release stage, backed by deterministic preflight and release-gate references. An npm installer and package contract, verified by","Agents can detect drift between versions and tags.\nReports distinguish missing artifacts from verification that was blocked.\nEach report names the exact surface that failed.\nReports provide evidence links without claiming that an install was executed.","Project, framework, and lockfile detection establishes the runtime model before anything changes.\nSkill workflow\nA deterministic Python auditor checks netlify.toml and the output shape against documented rules for base, publish, functions, redirects, and adapters.\nAutomation\nLocal builds verify root and nested routes, and any remote change is handed off only with explicit authorization.\nVerification\nThe skill detects the package manager, framework, and runtime model, then writes down an explicit build contract. A"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:bounded-delivery"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: Can Montasim deliver a focused tool as well as a large platform?
    text: "Yes. His catalog ranges from narrow typed packages and single-purpose audit or SVG tools to multi-sided SaaS and healthcare platforms. The smaller case studies show deliberate scope control, while larger systems show authentication, persistence, scheduling, real-time behavior, and operational workflows."
    factIds[4]: "project:project-content-types-lite","project:project-tin-audit-checker","project:project-postcraft","experience:experience-mymedicalhub-senior-software-engineer"
    supportingExcerpts[4]: "Tiny, type-safe HTTP media type constants and utilities for parsing, formatting, matching, and validation.\n\nfalse\n2025-01-29T13:00:26Z\n78eeb6ca02149b23e1486078e8b02ac009faf0b5\n2025-01-29T11:05:22Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/content-types-lite\nproject-content-types-lite\nassets/projects/content-types-lite.png\nhttps://content-types-lite.netlify.app\nhttps://content-types-lite.netlify.app/favicon.svg\nhttps://www.npmjs.com/package/content-types-lite\nTypeScript\nESM\nCommonJS\ntsup\nTanStack Start\nReact\nTailwind","A private, browser-only lookup for checking whether a Bangladesh TIN appears on a historical NBR risk-based audit selection list for AY 2023-24, covering 72,342 returns.\n\nfalse\n2026-04-30T09:35:36Z\n7d915fdb881c99faea75b5ce829d15dce17359ba\n2026-04-30T11:59:39Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/tin-audit-checker\nproject-tin-audit-checker\nassets/projects/tin-audit-checker.png\nhttps://tin-audit-checker.netlify.app\nhttps://tin-audit-checker.netlify.app/favicon.ico?favicon.0x3dzn~oxb6tn.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nTailwind","A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","employment\nMyMedicalHub International Ltd.\nhttps://mymedicalhub.com\nLead a team of **3 engineers** and own frontend architecture across **3 microservices** of a HIPAA-compliant telemedicine and biometric-assessment platform used by **1,000+ patients and clinicians**. Replaced fragile React-hook state with a deterministic **finite-state-machine** engine, so **99.9%** of assessment sessions complete without a crash or freeze, with **60 FPS** MediaPipe pose estimation and real-time rep counting. Built a **Konva.js**"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:constraint-discovery"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How does Montasim uncover requirements beyond the visible feature?
    text: "His case studies explicitly separate the visible request from constraints such as trust, lifecycle, failure recovery, source provenance, accessibility, privacy, and ownership. This makes hidden product risks discussable before they become incidental implementation behavior."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:thoughtline:problem","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:foliofarer:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Writing a useful LinkedIn reply or post requires understanding the visible conversation while keeping the author's own voice, intent, and control. The page is mutable third-party content, so passing it straight into a prompt would mix data, instructions, and potentially hostile text. An assistant that drafts well but cannot be trusted with page content and credentials is not useful.\nLinkedIn page content is untrusted and must be bounded before it enters any prompt.\nOpenRouter, Gemini, and Groq keys are all","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","A memorable 3D portfolio can hide the information recruiters need. Visitors whose devices, motion preferences, or assistive technology do not suit WebGL exploration can be shut out entirely. If the 3D scene owned the content, every fallback would be a reduced version of the real site.\nGenerated worlds must be deterministic enough for navigation, the minimap, landmarks, and tests to agree on the same route.\nEvery piece of content must remain reachable without operating the 3D scene.\nRendering must adapt to reduced"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:business-economics"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What evidence shows Montasim considers project economics?
    text: "The clearest professional result is a 70% infrastructure-cost reduction from migrating Azure VMs to App Service and automating CI/CD. Product architectures such as shared EduCanvas workflows and common DevTools contracts also address duplication and maintenance cost, though those records do not publish monetary figures."
    factIds[3]: "experience:experience-mymedicalhub-software-engineer","case-study:educanvas:architecture","case-study:devtools:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "employment\nMyMedicalHub International Ltd.\nhttps://mymedicalhub.com\nOwned the biometric strength-assessment module end to end and led cloud cost and infrastructure work across **4 environments**. Cut cloud hosting cost **70%** (about **$10K a year**) by moving from self-managed VMs to **Azure App Service** with GitHub Actions CI/CD, and improved application performance **40%** to **Lighthouse 90+** by replacing 5 legacy UI libraries. Designed a **feature-flag system** and API-driven theming that serve **250+","Next.js routes serve both the public site and the admin area.\nApplication shell\nFeature-owned domain modules handle content, admissions, and media.\nContent services\nCentralized institution and visual-theme configuration supplies the tokens that give each deployment its identity.\nInstitution themes\nMongoDB, authentication, Google Drive, and email adapters run behind an enforced server boundary.\nIntegrations\nNext.js serves both public and admin routes, which call feature-owned modules for content, admissions, and","A navigation registry defines the searchable routes, labels, and keyboard discovery for every tool.\nTool registry\nFocused modules implement the formatters, generators, testers, inspectors, and references.\nFeature modules\nLocal transforms, preferences, input history, and reusable state stay in the browser.\nBrowser boundary\nNext.js APIs, Better Auth, PostgreSQL, Prisma, and target services handle the work that has to leave the browser.\nServer boundary\nA navigation registry acts as the catalog source of truth. Tool"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:data-trust"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: How does Montasim make data-heavy products more trustworthy?
    text: "The Bangladesh Location Registry retains source provenance through normalization, while company research preserves evidence labels and the TIN checker keeps lookups private and historically scoped. Across these products, the design avoids presenting normalized data as more certain or current than its sources allow."
    factIds[3]: "case-study:bangladesh-location-registry:architecture","case-study:b4joinacompany:architecture","case-study:tin-audit-checker:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "Source and provenance manifests record where each dataset came from, what it covers, and its limitations.\nSource records\nSource data is normalized into geographic and facility records governed by JSON Schema.\nTransformation\nVersioned JSON, JSONL, compressed, and SQLite outputs are generated from the same model, with samples and documentation.\nRelease formats\nJavaScript scripts validate records against the schemas and produce deterministic release builds.\nValidation\nSource and provenance manifests describe where","Versioned records hold company, story, comment, and salary evidence.\nSource releases\nDeterministic rules handle identity matching, evidence labels, and question ranking.\nResearch layer\nNext.js serves research briefs, compare, and Ask, alongside a versioned REST API.\nProduct surfaces\nBetter Auth and MongoDB store private checkpoints pinned to evidence revisions.\nPrivate workspace\nVersioned source files feed a deterministic research layer. The web product and versioned API consume the same evidence model, while","A responsive Next.js page accepts a TIN and shows the match state with its mapped details.\nLookup interface\nThe static JSON list is fetched once per session and kept in memory for every later search.\nSession dataset\nInput is stripped of formatting characters and compared by exact digits, with no TIN submission API behind it.\nClient-side matching\nA Next.js page fetches the static JSON dataset once per session and caches it in memory. Each search normalizes the input and runs an exact match in the browser, and there"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:multi-platform-product"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What does Montasim understand about multi-platform publishing?
    text: "PostCraft models one idea through platform-specific generation, scoring, previews, brand constraints, scheduling, and delivery to LinkedIn, X, and Facebook. The workflow acknowledges that platforms have different limits and that durable publishing cannot depend on a browser tab remaining open."
    factIds[3]: "project:project-postcraft","case-study:postcraft:problem","case-study:postcraft:architecture"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "A social media manager that generates, scores, previews and schedules posts for LinkedIn, X and Facebook, using Gemini and stored brand guardrails.\n\ntrue\n2026-05-21T22:57:30Z\n664e1557c35eebde3bdb3a106447b4716e103b2c\n2026-05-26T16:29:25Z\nhttps://github.com/montasim/PostCraft\nproject-postcraft\nassets/projects/postcraft.png\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app\nhttps://aipostcraft.vercel.app/favicon.ico?favicon.8ffa2b51.ico\n\nNext.js\nReact\nTypeScript\nMongoDB\nMongoose\nRedux Toolkit\nBetter Auth\nOpenRouter\nGemini API\nGroq","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status.","The workspace covers generation, scoring, editing, brand rules, and platform previews.\nWorkspace\nValidated domain workflows handle content, the library, accounts, and publishing.\nDomain services\nOpenRouter, Groq, Gemini, and ZhipuAI sit behind one switcher with model and key fallback.\nProvider layer\nMongoDB persists state, Inngest runs scheduled jobs, and social platform APIs receive the published posts.\nDelivery\nThe Next.js application keeps HTTP boundaries in route handlers, product logic in domain modules,"
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:working-style"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What working style could a client expect from Montasim?
    text: "Clients can expect a proactive, clear, collaborative, and detail-conscious working style, based on attributed feedback from managers and teammates. His case studies reinforce the same pattern: he makes scope, constraints, technical decisions, outcomes, and limitations explicit. In practice, that supports transparent trade-off conversations and reduces the risk of discovering important assumptions only after implementation."
    factIds[3]: "recommendation:1:tabbi-quadir","recommendation:3:shahriar-iqbal","case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "May 25, 2025\nTabbi Quadir\nmanaged Mohammad Montasim Al Mamun directly\nProud contributor in accelerating digital transformation in Bangladesh\nI highly recommend Montasim Al Mamun as a skilled and dedicated software engineer. I had the pleasure of working with Montasim at MMHI.\n\nDuring our time together, I was consistently impressed by his technical abilities and commitment to delivering high-quality work. He demonstrated strong problem-solving skills and took a proactive approach to addressing challenges.\n\nMontasim","July 1, 2024\nShahriar Iqbal\nworked with Mohammad Montasim Al Mamun but on different teams\nSoftware QA Engineer | Postman | JMeter | Agile | JIRA | Azure DevOps | Researcher | Explorer\nI have worked closely with Mohammad Montasim -Al- Mamun Shuvo at MyMedicalHUB International Ltd., where he has consistently demonstrated his exceptional expertise in frontend web development. Mohammad is an outstanding professional in every sense, and I wholeheartedly recommend him.\n\nMohammad possesses a deep knowledge of React,","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:scope-conversation"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What should a potential client discuss with Montasim before starting work?
    text: "Discuss the target users, business outcome, privacy and security constraints, reliability expectations, integrations, delivery environment, ownership after launch, and the evidence that will prove success. Then align price, current capacity, timeline, milestones, communication cadence, and engagement terms, which are not published in the portfolio. This gives Montasim enough context to recommend a bounded first phase instead of estimating a product whose scope is still unclear."
    factIds[2]: profile,"case-study:postcraft:problem"
    supportingExcerpts[2]: "I started as a junior engineer at MyMedicalHub International, and **4+ years** later I lead a team of **3 engineers** building a HIPAA-compliant platform that **1,000+ patients and clinicians** rely on. I am proudest that **99.9%** of their AI assessment sessions now finish without a crash, and that we got there while cutting our cloud bill by **70%**.\n\nHealthcare is where I work, but it is not the only thing I build. I have also shipped **education**, **fitness**, **customer messaging** and **AI creator**","Creators and small teams repeat the same editorial work for every social platform. They rewrite one idea for different limits, compare weak AI variants, check brand language, and then move the result into separate publishing tools.\nAI providers vary in availability, rate limits, model behavior, and key quotas.\nLinkedIn, X, and Facebook each impose different OAuth, account, content, and API requirements.\nScheduled publishing must survive beyond the request that created it and retain a reviewable status."
  - id: "client-delivery-product-thinking:proof-of-work"
    category: client-delivery-product-thinking
    question: What proof can a client review before contacting Montasim?
    text: "Before making contact, a client can review 55 project records, 55 structured case studies, repository links and verified commits, live or package links where published, technical articles, professional experience, credentials, and attributed recommendations. The most useful approach is to select the closest comparable case and ask Montasim what would transfer, what would change, and which risks he would validate first for the new project."
    factIds[3]: "derived:catalog-count:projects","derived:catalog-count:casestudy","derived:catalog-count:recommendations"
    supportingExcerpts[3]: "projects\n55","casestudy\n55","recommendations\n16"
